{"items":[{"user":{"account_id":10438857,"reputation":13785,"user_id":7696223,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-m7rmWbqxL8s/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAo/GsqJGi8qLdI/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Scotty Jamison","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7696223/scotty-jamison"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1718805208,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":26,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise, modern approach:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}).toString())\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>How it works:</strong> <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams toString() method</a> will convert the URLSearchParams instance into a string representation, which happens to be a properly encoded query string.</p>\n<p>It is also legal to leave out the <code>.toString()</code> call like so: <code>fetch('https://...' + new URLSearchParams(...))</code>. JavaScript automatically calls <code>.toString()</code> when an object is concatenated with a string. This does require future code readers to have a deeper understanding of the language to understand your code.</p>\n<p>A complete example of a fetch request with query parameters:</p>\n<p><div class=\"snippet\" data-lang=\"js\" data-hide=\"true\" data-console=\"true\" data-babel=\"false\" data-babel-preset-react=\"false\" data-babel-preset-ts=\"false\">\n<div class=\"snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden\">\n<pre class=\"snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>// Real example you can copy-paste and play with.\n// jsonplaceholder.typicode.com provides a dummy rest-api\n// for this sort of purpose.\nasync function doAsyncTask() {\n  const url = (\n    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/comments?' +\n    new URLSearchParams({ postId: 1 }).toString()\n  );\n\n  const result = await fetch(url)\n    .then(response =&gt; response.json());\n\n  console.log('Fetched from: ' + url);\n  console.log(result);\n}\n\ndoAsyncTask();</code></pre>\n</div>\n</div>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>If you are using/supporting...</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>IE:</strong> Internet Explorer does not provide native support for URLSearchParams or fetch, but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/fetch-ie8\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Node:</strong> As of Node 18 there is native support for the fetch API (in version 17.5 it was behind the <code>--experimental-fetch</code> flag). In older versions, you can add the fetch API through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>. URLSearchParams comes with Node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>TypeScript:</strong> The values provided to <code>URLSearchParams</code> will be auto-coerced into a string, which is why TypeScript's definition for <code>URLSearchParams</code> requires you to provide all values as strings. Those who don't use TypeScript may be comfortable providing other value types to this function if they feel the coercion behavior is intuitive. For examples, numbers will behave exactly as you would expect, while the way arrays get implicitly coerced may cause confusion to future readers.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>TypeScript v4 or older with Node:</strong> Older versions of TypeScript did not offer type definitions for the global URLSearchParams in the Node environment. The simplest workaround is to just import it from the <code>url</code> module. This later got resolved at some point during v4 (I'm not sure what the exact version is that fixes this). See <a href=\"https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped/issues/34960\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this issue</a> for more info.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"The previous edit put the description out of sync with the code sample. Rather than revert it, I just updated the description, because I personally prefer including the .toString() call, so it makes sense to showcase that in the first example.","revision_guid":"8D5CDA46-BCBC-4C30-8327-EE4C7363B03E"},{"user":{"account_id":3169410,"reputation":5184,"user_id":2678755,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/0UeLD.png?s=256","display_name":"Abdellah","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/2678755/abdellah"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1718791721,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":25,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise, modern approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}).toString())\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>How it works:</strong> When a string (e.g. the URL) is being concatenated with an instance of <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/URLSearchParams\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams</a>, <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">its toString() method</a> will automatically be called to convert the instance into a string representation, which happens to be a properly encoded query string. If the automatic invoking of <code>toString()</code> is too magical for your liking, you may prefer to explicitly call it like so: <code>fetch('https://...' + new URLSearchParams(...).toString())</code></p>\n<p>A complete example of a fetch request with query parameters:</p>\n<p><div class=\"snippet\" data-lang=\"js\" data-hide=\"true\" data-console=\"true\" data-babel=\"false\" data-babel-preset-react=\"false\" data-babel-preset-ts=\"false\">\n<div class=\"snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden\">\n<pre class=\"snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>// Real example you can copy-paste and play with.\n// jsonplaceholder.typicode.com provides a dummy rest-api\n// for this sort of purpose.\nasync function doAsyncTask() {\n  const url = (\n    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/comments?' +\n    new URLSearchParams({ postId: 1 }).toString()\n  );\n\n  const result = await fetch(url)\n    .then(response =&gt; response.json());\n\n  console.log('Fetched from: ' + url);\n  console.log(result);\n}\n\ndoAsyncTask();</code></pre>\n</div>\n</div>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>If you are using/supporting...</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>IE:</strong> Internet Explorer does not provide native support for URLSearchParams or fetch, but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/fetch-ie8\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Node:</strong> As of Node 18 there is native support for the fetch API (in version 17.5 it was behind the <code>--experimental-fetch</code> flag). In older versions, you can add the fetch API through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>. URLSearchParams comes with Node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>TypeScript:</strong> The values provided to <code>URLSearchParams</code> will be auto-coerced into a string, which is why TypeScript's definition for <code>URLSearchParams</code> requires you to provide all values as strings. Those who don't use TypeScript may be comfortable providing other value types to this function if they feel the coercion behavior is intuitive. For examples, numbers will behave exactly as you would expect, while the way arrays get implicitly coerced may cause confusion to future readers.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>TypeScript v4 or older with Node:</strong> Older versions of TypeScript did not offer type definitions for the global URLSearchParams in the Node environment. The simplest workaround is to just import it from the <code>url</code> module. This later got resolved at some point during v4 (I'm not sure what the exact version is that fixes this). See <a href=\"https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped/issues/34960\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this issue</a> for more info.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"fix the original example code","revision_guid":"1F830B0A-31AE-40D0-AA3A-1A4175E99BA1"},{"user":{"account_id":10438857,"reputation":13785,"user_id":7696223,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-m7rmWbqxL8s/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAo/GsqJGi8qLdI/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Scotty Jamison","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7696223/scotty-jamison"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1711741776,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":24,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise, modern approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>How it works:</strong> When a string (e.g. the URL) is being concatenated with an instance of <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/URLSearchParams\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams</a>, <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">its toString() method</a> will automatically be called to convert the instance into a string representation, which happens to be a properly encoded query string. If the automatic invoking of <code>toString()</code> is too magical for your liking, you may prefer to explicitly call it like so: <code>fetch('https://...' + new URLSearchParams(...).toString())</code></p>\n<p>A complete example of a fetch request with query parameters:</p>\n<p><div class=\"snippet\" data-lang=\"js\" data-hide=\"true\" data-console=\"true\" data-babel=\"false\" data-babel-preset-react=\"false\" data-babel-preset-ts=\"false\">\n<div class=\"snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden\">\n<pre class=\"snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>// Real example you can copy-paste and play with.\n// jsonplaceholder.typicode.com provides a dummy rest-api\n// for this sort of purpose.\nasync function doAsyncTask() {\n  const url = (\n    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/comments?' +\n    new URLSearchParams({ postId: 1 }).toString()\n  );\n\n  const result = await fetch(url)\n    .then(response =&gt; response.json());\n\n  console.log('Fetched from: ' + url);\n  console.log(result);\n}\n\ndoAsyncTask();</code></pre>\n</div>\n</div>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>If you are using/supporting...</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>IE:</strong> Internet Explorer does not provide native support for URLSearchParams or fetch, but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/fetch-ie8\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Node:</strong> As of Node 18 there is native support for the fetch API (in version 17.5 it was behind the <code>--experimental-fetch</code> flag). In older versions, you can add the fetch API through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>. URLSearchParams comes with Node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>TypeScript:</strong> The values provided to <code>URLSearchParams</code> will be auto-coerced into a string, which is why TypeScript's definition for <code>URLSearchParams</code> requires you to provide all values as strings. Those who don't use TypeScript may be comfortable providing other value types to this function if they feel the coercion behavior is intuitive. For examples, numbers will behave exactly as you would expect, while the way arrays get implicitly coerced may cause confusion to future readers.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>TypeScript v4 or older with Node:</strong> Older versions of TypeScript did not offer type definitions for the global URLSearchParams in the Node environment. The simplest workaround is to just import it from the <code>url</code> module. This later got resolved at some point during v4 (I'm not sure what the exact version is that fixes this). See <a href=\"https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped/issues/34960\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this issue</a> for more info.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"Newer versions of TypeScript play nicer with Node","revision_guid":"0A5359B3-EA50-4F21-85D9-1EFE69E73C61"},{"user":{"account_id":2839770,"reputation":1103,"user_id":3782128,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/KhCoZ.png?s=256","display_name":"Rub&#233;n Pozo","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/3782128/rub%c3%a9n-pozo"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1710775225,"post_id":78161108,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"vote_based","comment":"<b>Post Undeleted</b> by <a href=\"/users/3782128/rub%c3%a9n-pozo\" dir=\"auto\">Rub&#233;n Pozo</a>","revision_guid":"81BD39F2-D6C0-44DB-9BDF-5C0881AF0212"},{"user":{"account_id":2839770,"reputation":1103,"user_id":3782128,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/KhCoZ.png?s=256","display_name":"Rub&#233;n Pozo","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/3782128/rub%c3%a9n-pozo"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1710775218,"post_id":78161108,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":3,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>I have found an alternative solution with pyyaml. The strategy is convert the dict into a string with the format &quot;k1.k2.k3: value&quot; and then convert it into a dict. Probably not the most efficient, but it works.</p>\n<pre><code>import yaml\nfrom copy import deepcopy\n\nmy_yaml = &quot;&quot;&quot;\nservice.scalingPolicy.capacity:\n    min: 1\n    max: 50\n&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n\nclass YamlUtils:\n\n    def yaml_dict_to_text(self, yaml_dict, parent_key, yaml_text):\n        return_value = yaml_text.split('\\n')\n        for key, value in yaml_dict.items():\n            key_string = key\n            if parent_key != &quot;&quot;:\n                key_string = parent_key + &quot;.&quot; + key_string\n            if isinstance(value, dict):\n                return_value.append(self.yaml_dict_to_text(value, key_string, yaml_text))\n            else:\n                return_value.append(&quot;{0}: {1}&quot;.format(\n                    key_string,\n                    value\n                )\n                )\n        return '\\n'.join(return_value)\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def convert_to_dict(source_string, split_symbol='.', value=None):\n        return_value = value\n        elements = source_string.split(split_symbol)\n        for element in reversed(elements):\n            if element:\n                return_value = {element: return_value}\n        return return_value\n\n    def dict_of_dicts_merge(self, x, y):\n        z = {}\n        try:\n            overlapping_keys = x.keys() &amp; y.keys()\n            for key in overlapping_keys:\n                z[key] = self.dict_of_dicts_merge(x[key], y[key])\n            for key in x.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n                z[key] = deepcopy(x[key])\n            for key in y.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n                z[key] = deepcopy(y[key])\n        except Exception as e:\n            print(&quot;Error merging dicts:&quot;, x, y, str(e))\n        return z\n\n    def text_to_yaml_dict(self, yaml_text):\n        return_value = {}\n        yaml_list = yaml_text.split('\\n')\n        for line in yaml_list:\n            line_items = line.split(':')\n            if len(line_items) &gt;= 2:\n                line_key = line_items[0]\n                line_value = line_items[1].lstrip()\n                line_dict = self.convert_to_dict(line_key, '.', line_value)\n                return_value = self.dict_of_dicts_merge(return_value, line_dict)\n        return return_value\n\n\ndef main():\n    try:\n        yaml_dict = yaml.safe_load(my_yaml)\n        yaml_utils = YamlUtils()\n        processed_yaml_text = yaml_utils.yaml_dict_to_text(\n            yaml_dict,\n            &quot;&quot;,\n            &quot;&quot;,\n        )\n        processed_yaml_dict = yaml_utils.text_to_yaml_dict(processed_yaml_text)\n        print(&quot;Min:&quot;, processed_yaml_dict['service']['scalingPolicy']['capacity']['min'])\n        print(&quot;Max:&quot;, processed_yaml_dict['service']['scalingPolicy']['capacity']['max'])\n        data = yaml.dump(processed_yaml_dict, indent=True)\n        print(&quot;New yaml:&quot;, data)\n    except yaml.YAMLError as exc:\n        print(exc)\n\n\nif __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:\n    main()\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"added 1631 characters in body","revision_guid":"1DFCDB21-2D50-45DE-A3CE-F8208AE390F5"},{"user":{"account_id":2839770,"reputation":1103,"user_id":3782128,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/KhCoZ.png?s=256","display_name":"Rub&#233;n Pozo","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/3782128/rub%c3%a9n-pozo"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1710491205,"post_id":78161108,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"vote_based","comment":"<b>Post Deleted</b> by <a href=\"/users/3782128/rub%c3%a9n-pozo\" dir=\"auto\">Rub&#233;n Pozo</a>","revision_guid":"427906E4-1B85-49BD-9763-A71A7419DA02"},{"user":{"account_id":2839770,"reputation":1103,"user_id":3782128,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/KhCoZ.png?s=256","display_name":"Rub&#233;n Pozo","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/3782128/rub%c3%a9n-pozo"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1710490888,"post_id":78161108,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":2,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>I have found an alternative solution with pyyaml replacing recursively dict keys with dot. I expect could be helpful.</p>\n<pre><code>import yaml\n\nmy_yaml = &quot;&quot;&quot;\nservice.scalingPolicy.capacity:\n    min: 1\n    max: 50\n&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n\ndef convert_to_dict(source_string, split_symbol='.', value=None):\n    return_value = value\n    elements = source_string.split(split_symbol)\n    for element in reversed(elements):\n        if element:\n            return_value = {element: return_value}\n    return return_value\n\n\ndef split_dots(source_dict):\n    return_value = {}\n    for key in source_dict:\n        new_value = source_dict[key]\n        if isinstance(source_dict[key], dict):\n            new_value = split_dots(source_dict[key])\n        new_key = key\n        if '.' in key:\n            new_dict = convert_to_dict(key, '.', new_value)\n            new_key = list(new_dict.keys())[0]\n            new_value = new_dict[new_key]\n        if new_key in return_value:\n            return_value[new_key].update(new_value)\n        else:\n            return_value[new_key] = new_value\n    return return_value\n\n\ndef main():\n    try:\n        yaml_dict = yaml.safe_load(my_yaml)\n        processed_yaml_dict = split_dots(yaml_dict)\n        print(&quot;Min:&quot;, processed_yaml_dict['service']['scalingPolicy']['capacity']['min'])\n        print(&quot;Max:&quot;, processed_yaml_dict['service']['scalingPolicy']['capacity']['max'])\n        data = yaml.dump(processed_yaml_dict, indent=True)\n        print(&quot;New yaml:&quot;, data)\n    except yaml.YAMLError as exc:\n        print(exc)\n\n\nif __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:\n    main()\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"added 121 characters in body","revision_guid":"A2D72B1E-B5E1-4FFD-A08C-03D66410CDC1"},{"user":{"account_id":2839770,"reputation":1103,"user_id":3782128,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/KhCoZ.png?s=256","display_name":"Rub&#233;n Pozo","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/3782128/rub%c3%a9n-pozo"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1710425106,"post_id":78161108,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":1,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>I have found an alternative solution with pyyaml replacing recursively dict keys with dot. I expect could be helpful.</p>\n<pre><code>import yaml\n\nmy_yaml = &quot;&quot;&quot;\nservice.scalingPolicy.capacity:\n    min: 1\n    max: 50\n&quot;&quot;&quot;\n\n\ndef convert_to_dict(source_string, split_symbol='.', value=None):\n    return_value = value\n    elements = source_string.split(split_symbol)\n    for element in reversed(elements):\n        if element:\n            return_value = {element: return_value}\n    return return_value\n\n\ndef split_dots(source_dict):\n    return_value = {}\n    for key in source_dict:\n        new_value = source_dict[key]\n        if isinstance(source_dict[key], dict):\n            new_value = split_dots(source_dict[key])\n        new_key = key\n        if '.' in key:\n            new_dict = convert_to_dict(key, '.', new_value)\n            new_key = list(new_dict.keys())[0]\n            new_value = new_dict[new_key]\n        return_value[new_key] = new_value\n    return return_value\n\n\ndef main():\n    try:\n        yaml_dict = yaml.safe_load(my_yaml)\n        processed_yaml_dict = split_dots(yaml_dict)\n        print(&quot;Min:&quot;, processed_yaml_dict['service']['scalingPolicy']['capacity']['min'])\n        print(&quot;Max:&quot;, processed_yaml_dict['service']['scalingPolicy']['capacity']['max'])\n        data = yaml.dump(processed_yaml_dict, indent=True)\n        print(&quot;New yaml:&quot;, data)\n    except yaml.YAMLError as exc:\n        print(exc)\n\n\nif __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:\n    main()\n</code></pre>\n","revision_guid":"2BAF3C6E-A8A2-4825-B896-245F5C8F6AA7"},{"user":{"account_id":10438857,"reputation":13785,"user_id":7696223,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-m7rmWbqxL8s/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAo/GsqJGi8qLdI/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Scotty Jamison","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7696223/scotty-jamison"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1701736874,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":23,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise, modern approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>How it works:</strong> When a string (e.g. the URL) is being concatenated with an instance of <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/URLSearchParams\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams</a>, <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">its toString() method</a> will automatically be called to convert the instance into a string representation, which happens to be a properly encoded query string. If the automatic invoking of <code>toString()</code> is too magical for your liking, you may prefer to explicitly call it like so: <code>fetch('https://...' + new URLSearchParams(...).toString())</code></p>\n<p>A complete example of a fetch request with query parameters:</p>\n<p><div class=\"snippet\" data-lang=\"js\" data-hide=\"true\" data-console=\"true\" data-babel=\"false\" data-babel-preset-react=\"false\" data-babel-preset-ts=\"false\">\n<div class=\"snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden\">\n<pre class=\"snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>// Real example you can copy-paste and play with.\n// jsonplaceholder.typicode.com provides a dummy rest-api\n// for this sort of purpose.\nasync function doAsyncTask() {\n  const url = (\n    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/comments?' +\n    new URLSearchParams({ postId: 1 }).toString()\n  );\n\n  const result = await fetch(url)\n    .then(response =&gt; response.json());\n\n  console.log('Fetched from: ' + url);\n  console.log(result);\n}\n\ndoAsyncTask();</code></pre>\n</div>\n</div>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>If you are using/supporting...</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>IE:</strong> Internet Explorer does not provide native support for URLSearchParams or fetch, but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/fetch-ie8\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Node:</strong> As of Node 18 there is native support for the fetch API (in version 17.5 it was behind the <code>--experimental-fetch</code> flag). In older versions, you can add the fetch API through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>. URLSearchParams comes with Node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>TypeScript:</strong> The values provided to <code>URLSearchParams</code> will be auto-coerced into a string, which is why TypeScript's definition for <code>URLSearchParams</code> requires you to provide all values as strings. Those who don't use TypeScript may be comfortable providing other value types to this function if they feel the coercion behavior is intuitive. For examples, numbers will behave exactly as you would expect, while the way arrays get implicitly coerced may cause confusion to future readers.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Node + TypeScript:</strong> If you're using Node and TypeScript together you'll find that, due to some technical limitations, TypeScript does not offer type definitions for the global URLSearchParams. The simplest workaround is to just import it from the <code>url</code> module. See <a href=\"https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped/issues/34960\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this issue</a> for more info.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"Some touch-ups","revision_guid":"AD65A580-6222-46C1-AE62-4E8B57A29A70"},{"user":{"account_id":10438857,"reputation":13785,"user_id":7696223,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-m7rmWbqxL8s/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAo/GsqJGi8qLdI/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Scotty Jamison","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7696223/scotty-jamison"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1698509538,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":22,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise, modern approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>How it works:</strong> When a string (e.g. the URL) is being concatenated with an instance of <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/URLSearchParams\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams</a>, <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">its toString() method</a> will automatically be called to convert the instance into a string representation, which happens to be a properly encoded query string. If the automatic invoking of <code>toString()</code> is too magical for your liking, you may prefer to explicitly call it like so: <code>fetch('https://...' + new URLSearchParams(...).toString())</code></p>\n<p>A complete example of a fetch request with query parameters:</p>\n<p><div class=\"snippet\" data-lang=\"js\" data-hide=\"true\" data-console=\"true\" data-babel=\"false\" data-babel-preset-react=\"false\" data-babel-preset-ts=\"false\">\n<div class=\"snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden\">\n<pre class=\"snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>// Real example you can copy-paste and play with.\n// jsonplaceholder.typicode.com provides a dummy rest-api\n// for this sort of purpose.\nasync function doAsyncTask() {\n  const url = (\n    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/comments?' +\n    new URLSearchParams({ postId: 1 }).toString()\n  );\n\n  const result = await fetch(url)\n    .then(response =&gt; response.json());\n\n  console.log('Fetched from: ' + url);\n  console.log(result);\n}\n\ndoAsyncTask();</code></pre>\n</div>\n</div>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>If you are using/supporting...</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>IE:</strong> Internet Explorer does not provide native support for URLSearchParams or fetch, but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/fetch-ie8\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Node:</strong> As of Node 18 there is native support for the fetch API (in version 17.5 it was behind the <code>--experimental-fetch</code> flag). In older versions, you can add the fetch API through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>. URLSearchParams comes with Node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>TypeScript:</strong> The values provided to <code>URLSearchParams</code> will be auto-coerced to a string, which is why TypeScript's definition for <code>URLSearchParams</code> requires you to provide all values as strings. Those who don't use TypeScript may be comfortable providing other value types to this function if they feel the coercion behavior is intuitive. For examples, numbers will behave exactly as you would expect, while the behavior of arrays may cause confusion to future readers.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Node + TypeScript:</strong> If you're using Node and TypeScript together you'll find that, due to some technical limitations, TypeScript does not offer type definitions for the global URLSearchParams. The simplest workaround is to just import it from the <code>url</code> module. See <a href=\"https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped/issues/34960\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">here</a> for more info.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"Thingss like numbers behave exactly as one would would expect, I see no reason to discourage their use even though TypeScript did. Reverting previous edits and adding a snippet to explain TypeScript&#39;s behavior.","revision_guid":"68E1729D-C2DA-4D27-B13C-FED86331662C"},{"user":{"account_id":5392262,"reputation":5168,"user_id":4294399,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wjTzAAY8.jpg?s=256","display_name":"General Grievance","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/4294399/general-grievance"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1698436531,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":21,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise, modern approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    // Strictly, values should be strings. JS may convert anyway, but TS complains\n    bar: '2',\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>How it works:</strong> When a string (e.g. the URL) is being concatenated with an instance of <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/URLSearchParams\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams</a>, <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">its toString() method</a> will automatically be called to convert the instance into a string representation, which happens to be a properly encoded query string. If the automatic invoking of <code>toString()</code> is too magical for your liking, you may prefer to explicitly call it like so: <code>fetch('https://...' + new URLSearchParams(...).toString())</code></p>\n<p>A complete example of a fetch request with query parameters:</p>\n<p><div class=\"snippet\" data-lang=\"js\" data-hide=\"true\" data-console=\"true\" data-babel=\"false\" data-babel-preset-react=\"false\" data-babel-preset-ts=\"false\">\n<div class=\"snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden\">\n<pre class=\"snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>// Real example you can copy-paste and play with.\n// jsonplaceholder.typicode.com provides a dummy rest-api\n// for this sort of purpose.\nasync function doAsyncTask() {\n  const url = (\n    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/comments?' +\n    new URLSearchParams({ postId: 1 }).toString()\n  );\n\n  const result = await fetch(url)\n    .then(response =&gt; response.json());\n\n  console.log('Fetched from: ' + url);\n  console.log(result);\n}\n\ndoAsyncTask();</code></pre>\n</div>\n</div>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>If you are using/supporting...</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>IE:</strong> Internet Explorer does not provide native support for URLSearchParams or fetch, but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/fetch-ie8\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Node:</strong> As of Node 18 there is native support for the fetch API (in version 17.5 it was behind the <code>--experimental-fetch</code> flag). In older versions, you can add the fetch API through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>. URLSearchParams comes with Node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Node + TypeScript:</strong> If you're using Node and TypeScript together you'll find that, due to some technical limitations, TypeScript does not offer type definitions for the global URLSearchParams. The simplest workaround is to just import it from the <code>url</code> module. See <a href=\"https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped/issues/34960\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">here</a> for more info.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"Used string value instead of number. Docs on URLSearchParams say this should be a record of string -&gt; string.","revision_guid":"B84CBB2B-D651-416F-BE32-40F0298BE11B"},{"user":{"account_id":10438857,"reputation":13785,"user_id":7696223,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-m7rmWbqxL8s/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAo/GsqJGi8qLdI/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Scotty Jamison","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7696223/scotty-jamison"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1692147242,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":20,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise, modern approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>How it works:</strong> When a string (e.g. the URL) is being concatenated with an instance of <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/URLSearchParams\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams</a>, <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">its toString() method</a> will automatically be called to convert the instance into a string representation, which happens to be a properly encoded query string. If the automatic invoking of <code>toString()</code> is too magical for your liking, you may prefer to explicitly call it like so: <code>fetch('https://...' + new URLSearchParams(...).toString())</code></p>\n<p>A complete example of a fetch request with query parameters:</p>\n<p><div class=\"snippet\" data-lang=\"js\" data-hide=\"true\" data-console=\"true\" data-babel=\"false\" data-babel-preset-react=\"false\" data-babel-preset-ts=\"false\">\n<div class=\"snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden\">\n<pre class=\"snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>// Real example you can copy-paste and play with.\n// jsonplaceholder.typicode.com provides a dummy rest-api\n// for this sort of purpose.\nasync function doAsyncTask() {\n  const url = (\n    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/comments?' +\n    new URLSearchParams({ postId: 1 }).toString()\n  );\n\n  const result = await fetch(url)\n    .then(response =&gt; response.json());\n\n  console.log('Fetched from: ' + url);\n  console.log(result);\n}\n\ndoAsyncTask();</code></pre>\n</div>\n</div>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>If you are using/supporting...</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>IE:</strong> Internet Explorer does not provide native support for URLSearchParams or fetch, but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/fetch-ie8\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Node:</strong> As of Node 18 there is native support for the fetch API (in version 17.5 it was behind the <code>--experimental-fetch</code> flag). In older versions, you can add the fetch API through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>. URLSearchParams comes with Node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Node + TypeScript:</strong> If you're using Node and TypeScript together you'll find that, due to some technical limitations, TypeScript does not offer type definitions for the global URLSearchParams. The simplest workaround is to just import it from the <code>url</code> module. See <a href=\"https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped/issues/34960\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">here</a> for more info.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"Fix linked text. (I still want to include &quot;its&quot; in the link, to make it clear that I&#39;m linking to URLSearchParam&#39;s toString method, and not information about the toString protocol in general.","revision_guid":"4BA68C4C-7AAD-44DB-BA38-B1206AD79AE8"},{"user":{"account_id":10438857,"reputation":13785,"user_id":7696223,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-m7rmWbqxL8s/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAo/GsqJGi8qLdI/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Scotty Jamison","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7696223/scotty-jamison"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":true,"creation_date":1692140813,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":19,"body":"<p>A concise, modern approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>How it works:</strong> When a string (e.g. the URL) is being concatenated with an instance of <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/URLSearchParams\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams</a>, <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">its toString()</a> method will automatically be called to convert the instance into a string representation, which happens to be a properly encoded query string. If the automatic invoking of <code>toString()</code> is too magical for your liking, you may prefer to explicitly call it like so: <code>fetch('https://...' + new URLSearchParams(...).toString())</code></p>\n<p>A complete example of a fetch request with query parameters:</p>\n<p><div class=\"snippet\" data-lang=\"js\" data-hide=\"true\" data-console=\"true\" data-babel=\"false\" data-babel-preset-react=\"false\" data-babel-preset-ts=\"false\">\n<div class=\"snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden\">\n<pre class=\"snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>// Real example you can copy-paste and play with.\n// jsonplaceholder.typicode.com provides a dummy rest-api\n// for this sort of purpose.\nasync function doAsyncTask() {\n  const url = (\n    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/comments?' +\n    new URLSearchParams({ postId: 1 }).toString()\n  );\n\n  const result = await fetch(url)\n    .then(response =&gt; response.json());\n\n  console.log('Fetched from: ' + url);\n  console.log(result);\n}\n\ndoAsyncTask();</code></pre>\n</div>\n</div>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>If you are using/supporting...</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>IE:</strong> Internet Explorer does not provide native support for URLSearchParams or fetch, but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/fetch-ie8\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Node:</strong> As of Node 18 there is native support for the fetch API (in version 17.5 it was behind the <code>--experimental-fetch</code> flag). In older versions, you can add the fetch API through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>. URLSearchParams comes with Node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Node + TypeScript:</strong> If you're using Node and TypeScript together you'll find that, due to some technical limitations, TypeScript does not offer type definitions for the global URLSearchParams. The simplest workaround is to just import it from the <code>url</code> module. See <a href=\"https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped/issues/34960\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">here</a> for more info.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"Rollback to Revision 17 - Edit approval overridden by post owner or moderator","revision_guid":"AF16673D-B591-4316-8FBA-AA86E0888DDB"},{"user":{"account_id":13412252,"reputation":182,"user_id":9678200,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/93c5bc9145a3736cc28a9f6e95ad164b?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Dan SimBed","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/9678200/dan-simbed"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1692132377,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":18,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise, modern approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>How it works:</strong> When a string (e.g. the URL) is being concatenated with an instance of <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/URLSearchParams\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams</a>, its <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">toString()</a> method will automatically be called to convert the instance into a string representation, which happens to be a properly encoded query string. If the automatic invoking of <code>toString()</code> is too magical for your liking, you may prefer to explicitly call it like so: <code>fetch('https://...' + new URLSearchParams(...).toString())</code></p>\n<p>A complete example of a fetch request with query parameters:</p>\n<p><div class=\"snippet\" data-lang=\"js\" data-hide=\"true\" data-console=\"true\" data-babel=\"false\" data-babel-preset-react=\"false\" data-babel-preset-ts=\"false\">\n<div class=\"snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden\">\n<pre class=\"snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>// Real example you can copy-paste and play with.\n// jsonplaceholder.typicode.com provides a dummy rest-api\n// for this sort of purpose.\nasync function doAsyncTask() {\n  const url = (\n    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/comments?' +\n    new URLSearchParams({ postId: 1 }).toString()\n  );\n\n  const result = await fetch(url)\n    .then(response =&gt; response.json());\n\n  console.log('Fetched from: ' + url);\n  console.log(result);\n}\n\ndoAsyncTask();</code></pre>\n</div>\n</div>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>If you are using/supporting...</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>IE:</strong> Internet Explorer does not provide native support for URLSearchParams or fetch, but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/fetch-ie8\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Node:</strong> As of Node 18 there is native support for the fetch API (in version 17.5 it was behind the <code>--experimental-fetch</code> flag). In older versions, you can add the fetch API through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>. URLSearchParams comes with Node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Node + TypeScript:</strong> If you're using Node and TypeScript together you'll find that, due to some technical limitations, TypeScript does not offer type definitions for the global URLSearchParams. The simplest workaround is to just import it from the <code>url</code> module. See <a href=\"https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped/issues/34960\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">here</a> for more info.</p>\n<p> </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"corrected position of &#39;its&#39; outside of &#39;its toString()&#39; link","revision_guid":"255753BF-B38A-4AAF-83FC-C972884A6188"},{"user":{"account_id":6321039,"reputation":407685,"user_id":4909087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Zcszo.png?s=256","display_name":"coldspeed95","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/4909087/coldspeed95"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1681541638,"post_id":53954986,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":18,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h3>How to deal with <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> and <code>ChainedAssignmentError</code> in Pandas?</h3>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This post is meant for readers who,</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Would like to understand what this warning means</li>\n<li>Would like to understand different ways of suppressing this warning</li>\n<li>Would like to understand how to improve their code and follow good practices to avoid this warning in the future.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Setup</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>np.random.seed(0)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(np.random.choice(10, (3, 5)), columns=list('ABCDE'))\ndf\n   A  B  C  D  E\n0  5  0  3  3  7\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<h1>What is the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>?</h1>\n<p>To know how to deal with this warning, it is important to understand what it means and why it is raised in the first place.</p>\n<p>When filtering DataFrames, it is possible slice/index a frame to return either a <em>view</em>, or a <em>copy</em>, depending on the internal layout and various implementation details. A &quot;view&quot; is, as the term suggests, a view into the original data, so modifying the view may modify the original object. On the other hand, a &quot;copy&quot; is a replication of data from the original, and modifying the copy has no effect on the original.</p>\n<p>As mentioned by other answers, the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> was created to flag &quot;chained assignment&quot; operations. Consider <code>df</code> in the setup above. Suppose you would like to select all values in column &quot;B&quot; where values in column &quot;A&quot; is &gt; 5. Pandas allows you to do this in different ways, some more correct than others. For example,</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B']\n\n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B']\n\n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>These return the same result, so if you are only reading these values, it makes no difference. So, what is the issue? The problem with chained assignment, is that it is generally difficult to predict whether a view or a copy is returned, <strong>so this largely becomes an issue when you are attempting to assign values back.</strong> To build on the earlier example, consider how this code is executed by the interpreter:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__setitem__((df.A &gt; 5, 'B'), 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>With a single <code>__setitem__</code> call to <code>df</code>. OTOH, consider this code:</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__getitem__(df.A &gt; 5).__setitem__('B', 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now, depending on whether <code>__getitem__</code> returned a view or a copy, the <code>__setitem__</code> operation <strong>may not work</strong>.</p>\n<p>In general, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.loc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>loc</code></a> for label-based assignment, and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iloc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iloc</code></a> for integer/positional based assignment, as the spec guarantees that they always operate on the original. Additionally, for setting a single cell, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.at.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>at</code></a> and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iat.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iat</code></a>.</p>\n<p>More can be found in the <a href=\"http://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/indexing.html#returning-a-view-versus-a-copy\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">documentation</a>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>\nAll boolean indexing operations done with <code>loc</code> can also be done with <code>iloc</code>. The only difference is that <code>iloc</code> expects either\nintegers/positions for index or a numpy array of boolean values, and\ninteger/position indexes for the columns.</p>\n<p>For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written nas</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[(df.A &gt; 5).values, 1] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'A'] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written as</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[1, 0] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>And so on.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>from pandas &gt;= 2.0, you can enable <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/copy_on_write.html#copy-on-write-optimizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Copy-on-write optimizations</a> to save on memory and avoid making copies of data until written to (if possible).</h3>\n<p>This can be enabled by</p>\n<pre><code>pd.options.mode.copy_on_write = True\n</code></pre>\n<p>After this, attempts to make chained assignments will result in</p>\n<pre><code>ChainedAssignmentError: A value is trying to be set on a copy of a DataFrame or Series through chained assignment.\nWhen using the Copy-on-Write mode, such chained assignment never works to update the original DataFrame or Series, because the intermediate object on which we are setting values always behaves as a copy.\n\nTry using '.loc[row_indexer, col_indexer] = value' instead, to perform the assignment in a single step.\n</code></pre>\n<p>The error is raised in a similar setting to the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>.</p>\n<hr />\n<h1>Just tell me how to suppress the warning!</h1>\n<p>Consider a simple operation on the &quot;A&quot; column of <code>df</code>. Selecting &quot;A&quot; and dividing by 2 will raise the warning, but the operation will work.</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>df2 = df[['A']]\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n/Library/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.6/lib/python3.6/site-packages/IPython/__main__.py:1: SettingWithCopyWarning:\nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n\ndf2\n     A\n0  2.5\n1  4.5\n2  3.5\n</code></pre>\n<p>There are a couple ways of directly silencing this warning:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p>(recommended) <strong>Use <code>loc</code> to slice subsets</strong>:</p>\n<pre><code> df2 = df.loc[:, ['A']]\n df2['A'] /= 2     # Does not raise\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Change <code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment</code></strong>\nCan be set to <code>None</code>, <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code>, or <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code>. <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code> is the default. <code>None</code> will suppress the warning entirely, and <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code> will throw a <code>SettingWithCopyError</code>, preventing the operation from going through.</p>\n<pre><code> pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = None\n df2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Make a <code>deepcopy</code></strong></p>\n<pre><code> df2 = df[['A']].copy(deep=True)\n df2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/20625582/how-to-deal-with-settingwithcopywarning-in-pandas#comment62589711_20625582\">@Peter Cotton</a> in the comments, came up with a nice way of non-intrusively changing the mode (modified from <a href=\"https://gist.github.com/notbanker/2be3ed34539c86e22ffdd88fd95ad8bc\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this gist</a>) using a context manager, to set the mode only as long as it is required, and the reset it back to the original state when finished.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>class ChainedAssignent:\n    def __init__(self, chained=None):\n        acceptable = [None, 'warn', 'raise']\n        assert chained in acceptable, &quot;chained must be in &quot; + str(acceptable)\n        self.swcw = chained\n\n    def __enter__(self):\n        self.saved_swcw = pd.options.mode.chained_assignment\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.swcw\n        return self\n\n    def __exit__(self, *args):\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.saved_swcw\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The usage is as follows:</p>\n<pre><code># Some code here\nwith ChainedAssignent():\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n# More code follows\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or, to raise the exception</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>with ChainedAssignent(chained='raise'):\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n\nSettingWithCopyError:\nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<h1>The &quot;XY Problem&quot;: What am I doing wrong?</h1>\n<p>A lot of the time, users attempt to look for ways of suppressing this exception without fully understanding why it was raised in the first place. This is a good example of an <a href=\"https://meta.stackexchange.com/q/66377\">XY problem</a>, where users attempt to solve a problem &quot;Y&quot; that is actually a symptom of a deeper rooted problem &quot;X&quot;. Questions will be raised based on common problems that encounter this warning, and solutions will then be presented.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 1</strong>\nI have a DataFrame</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>df\n       A  B  C  D  E\n    0  5  0  3  3  7\n    1  9  3  5  2  4\n    2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I want to assign values in col &quot;A&quot; &gt; 5 to 1000. My expected output is</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>      A  B  C  D  E\n0     5  0  3  3  7\n1  1000  3  5  2  4\n2  1000  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Wrong way to do this:</p>\n<pre><code>df.A[df.A &gt; 5] = 1000         # works, because df.A returns a view\ndf[df.A &gt; 5]['A'] = 1000      # does not work\ndf.loc[df.A &gt; 5]['A'] = 1000   # does not work\n</code></pre>\n<p>Right way using <code>loc</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'A'] = 1000\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 2<sup>1</sup></strong>\nI am trying to set the value in cell (1, 'D') to 12345. My expected output is</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>   A  B  C      D  E\n0  5  0  3      3  7\n1  9  3  5  12345  4\n2  7  6  8      8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I have tried different ways of accessing this cell, such as\n<code>df['D'][1]</code>. What is the best way to do this?</p>\n<p><sub>1. This question isn't specifically related to the warning, but\nit is good to understand how to do this particular operation correctly\nso as to avoid situations where the warning could potentially arise in\nfuture.</sub></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You can use any of the following methods to do this.</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iloc[1, 3] = 12345\ndf.at[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iat[1, 3] = 12345\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 3</strong>\nI am trying to subset values based on some condition. I have a\nDataFrame</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I would like to assign values in &quot;D&quot; to 123 such that &quot;C&quot; == 5. I\ntried</p>\n<pre><code>df2.loc[df2.C == 5, 'D'] = 123\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which seems fine but I am <em>still</em> getting the\n<code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>! How do I fix this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is actually probably because of code higher up in your pipeline. Did you create <code>df2</code> from something larger, like</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>? In this case, boolean indexing will return a view, so <code>df2</code> will reference the original. What you'd need to do is assign <code>df2</code> to a <em>copy</em>:</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5].copy()\n# Or,\n# df2 = df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, :]\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 4</strong>\nI'm trying to drop column &quot;C&quot; in-place from</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>But using</p>\n<pre><code>df2.drop('C', axis=1, inplace=True)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Throws <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>. Why is this happening?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is because <code>df2</code> must have been created as a view from some other slicing operation, such as</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>The solution here is to either make a <code>copy()</code> of <code>df</code>, or use <code>loc</code>, as before.</p>\n\n","comment":"added 929 characters in body","revision_guid":"193FD7AF-6D7E-496C-B0FA-B99158D0C961"},{"user":{"account_id":24885,"reputation":30796,"user_id":63550,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/RIZKi.png?s=256","display_name":"Peter Mortensen","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/63550/peter-mortensen"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1663775903,"post_id":53954986,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":17,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h3>How to deal with <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> in Pandas?</h3>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This post is meant for readers who,</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Would like to understand what this warning means</li>\n<li>Would like to understand different ways of suppressing this warning</li>\n<li>Would like to understand how to improve their code and follow good practices to avoid this warning in the future.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Setup</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>np.random.seed(0)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(np.random.choice(10, (3, 5)), columns=list('ABCDE'))\ndf\n   A  B  C  D  E\n0  5  0  3  3  7\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<h1>What is the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>?</h1>\n<p>To know how to deal with this warning, it is important to understand what it means and why it is raised in the first place.</p>\n<p>When filtering DataFrames, it is possible slice/index a frame to return either a <em>view</em>, or a <em>copy</em>, depending on the internal layout and various implementation details. A &quot;view&quot; is, as the term suggests, a view into the original data, so modifying the view may modify the original object. On the other hand, a &quot;copy&quot; is a replication of data from the original, and modifying the copy has no effect on the original.</p>\n<p>As mentioned by other answers, the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> was created to flag &quot;chained assignment&quot; operations. Consider <code>df</code> in the setup above. Suppose you would like to select all values in column &quot;B&quot; where values in column &quot;A&quot; is &gt; 5. Pandas allows you to do this in different ways, some more correct than others. For example,</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B']\n\n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B']\n\n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>These return the same result, so if you are only reading these values, it makes no difference. So, what is the issue? The problem with chained assignment, is that it is generally difficult to predict whether a view or a copy is returned, <strong>so this largely becomes an issue when you are attempting to assign values back.</strong> To build on the earlier example, consider how this code is executed by the interpreter:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__setitem__((df.A &gt; 5, 'B'), 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>With a single <code>__setitem__</code> call to <code>df</code>. OTOH, consider this code:</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__getitem__(df.A &gt; 5).__setitem__('B', 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now, depending on whether <code>__getitem__</code> returned a view or a copy, the <code>__setitem__</code> operation <strong>may not work</strong>.</p>\n<p>In general, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.loc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>loc</code></a> for label-based assignment, and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iloc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iloc</code></a> for integer/positional based assignment, as the spec guarantees that they always operate on the original. Additionally, for setting a single cell, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.at.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>at</code></a> and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iat.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iat</code></a>.</p>\n<p>More can be found in the <a href=\"http://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/indexing.html#returning-a-view-versus-a-copy\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">documentation</a>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Note</strong>\nAll boolean indexing operations done with <code>loc</code> can also be done with <code>iloc</code>. The only difference is that <code>iloc</code> expects either\nintegers/positions for index or a numpy array of boolean values, and\ninteger/position indexes for the columns.</p>\n<p>For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written nas</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[(df.A &gt; 5).values, 1] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'A'] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written as</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[1, 0] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>And so on.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h1>Just tell me how to suppress the warning!</h1>\n<p>Consider a simple operation on the &quot;A&quot; column of <code>df</code>. Selecting &quot;A&quot; and dividing by 2 will raise the warning, but the operation will work.</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>df2 = df[['A']]\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n/Library/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.6/lib/python3.6/site-packages/IPython/__main__.py:1: SettingWithCopyWarning:\nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n\ndf2\n     A\n0  2.5\n1  4.5\n2  3.5\n</code></pre>\n<p>There are a couple ways of directly silencing this warning:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p>(recommended) <strong>Use <code>loc</code> to slice subsets</strong>:</p>\n<pre><code> df2 = df.loc[:, ['A']]\n df2['A'] /= 2     # Does not raise\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Change <code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment</code></strong>\nCan be set to <code>None</code>, <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code>, or <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code>. <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code> is the default. <code>None</code> will suppress the warning entirely, and <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code> will throw a <code>SettingWithCopyError</code>, preventing the operation from going through.</p>\n<pre><code> pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = None\n df2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Make a <code>deepcopy</code></strong></p>\n<pre><code> df2 = df[['A']].copy(deep=True)\n df2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/20625582/how-to-deal-with-settingwithcopywarning-in-pandas#comment62589711_20625582\">@Peter Cotton</a> in the comments, came up with a nice way of non-intrusively changing the mode (modified from <a href=\"https://gist.github.com/notbanker/2be3ed34539c86e22ffdd88fd95ad8bc\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this gist</a>) using a context manager, to set the mode only as long as it is required, and the reset it back to the original state when finished.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>class ChainedAssignent:\n    def __init__(self, chained=None):\n        acceptable = [None, 'warn', 'raise']\n        assert chained in acceptable, &quot;chained must be in &quot; + str(acceptable)\n        self.swcw = chained\n\n    def __enter__(self):\n        self.saved_swcw = pd.options.mode.chained_assignment\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.swcw\n        return self\n\n    def __exit__(self, *args):\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.saved_swcw\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The usage is as follows:</p>\n<pre><code># Some code here\nwith ChainedAssignent():\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n# More code follows\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or, to raise the exception</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>with ChainedAssignent(chained='raise'):\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n\nSettingWithCopyError:\nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<h1>The &quot;XY Problem&quot;: What am I doing wrong?</h1>\n<p>A lot of the time, users attempt to look for ways of suppressing this exception without fully understanding why it was raised in the first place. This is a good example of an <a href=\"https://meta.stackexchange.com/q/66377\">XY problem</a>, where users attempt to solve a problem &quot;Y&quot; that is actually a symptom of a deeper rooted problem &quot;X&quot;. Questions will be raised based on common problems that encounter this warning, and solutions will then be presented.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 1</strong>\nI have a DataFrame</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>df\n       A  B  C  D  E\n    0  5  0  3  3  7\n    1  9  3  5  2  4\n    2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I want to assign values in col &quot;A&quot; &gt; 5 to 1000. My expected output is</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>      A  B  C  D  E\n0     5  0  3  3  7\n1  1000  3  5  2  4\n2  1000  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Wrong way to do this:</p>\n<pre><code>df.A[df.A &gt; 5] = 1000         # works, because df.A returns a view\ndf[df.A &gt; 5]['A'] = 1000      # does not work\ndf.loc[df.A &gt; 5]['A'] = 1000   # does not work\n</code></pre>\n<p>Right way using <code>loc</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'A'] = 1000\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 2<sup>1</sup></strong>\nI am trying to set the value in cell (1, 'D') to 12345. My expected output is</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>   A  B  C      D  E\n0  5  0  3      3  7\n1  9  3  5  12345  4\n2  7  6  8      8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I have tried different ways of accessing this cell, such as\n<code>df['D'][1]</code>. What is the best way to do this?</p>\n<p><sub>1. This question isn't specifically related to the warning, but\nit is good to understand how to do this particular operation correctly\nso as to avoid situations where the warning could potentially arise in\nfuture.</sub></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You can use any of the following methods to do this.</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iloc[1, 3] = 12345\ndf.at[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iat[1, 3] = 12345\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 3</strong>\nI am trying to subset values based on some condition. I have a\nDataFrame</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I would like to assign values in &quot;D&quot; to 123 such that &quot;C&quot; == 5. I\ntried</p>\n<pre><code>df2.loc[df2.C == 5, 'D'] = 123\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which seems fine but I am <em>still</em> getting the\n<code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>! How do I fix this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is actually probably because of code higher up in your pipeline. Did you create <code>df2</code> from something larger, like</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>? In this case, boolean indexing will return a view, so <code>df2</code> will reference the original. What you'd need to do is assign <code>df2</code> to a <em>copy</em>:</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5].copy()\n# Or,\n# df2 = df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, :]\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 4</strong>\nI'm trying to drop column &quot;C&quot; in-place from</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>But using</p>\n<pre><code>df2.drop('C', axis=1, inplace=True)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Throws <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>. Why is this happening?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is because <code>df2</code> must have been created as a view from some other slicing operation, such as</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>The solution here is to either make a <code>copy()</code> of <code>df</code>, or use <code>loc</code>, as before.</p>\n\n","comment":"Fixed the weird syntax highlighting (as a result, the diff looks more extensive than it really is - use view &quot;Side-by-side Markdown&quot; to compare) - an alternative would be to separate the code and the output.","revision_guid":"C3755E44-1193-40FC-9BD6-AD930DA36E4F"},{"user":{"account_id":10438857,"reputation":13785,"user_id":7696223,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-m7rmWbqxL8s/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAo/GsqJGi8qLdI/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Scotty Jamison","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7696223/scotty-jamison"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1650502433,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":17,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise, modern approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>How it works:</strong> When a string (e.g. the URL) is being concatenated with an instance of <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/URLSearchParams\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams</a>, <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">its toString()</a> method will automatically be called to convert the instance into a string representation, which happens to be a properly encoded query string. If the automatic invoking of <code>toString()</code> is too magical for your liking, you may prefer to explicitly call it like so: <code>fetch('https://...' + new URLSearchParams(...).toString())</code></p>\n<p>A complete example of a fetch request with query parameters:</p>\n<p><div class=\"snippet\" data-lang=\"js\" data-hide=\"true\" data-console=\"true\" data-babel=\"false\" data-babel-preset-react=\"false\" data-babel-preset-ts=\"false\">\n<div class=\"snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden\">\n<pre class=\"snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>// Real example you can copy-paste and play with.\n// jsonplaceholder.typicode.com provides a dummy rest-api\n// for this sort of purpose.\nasync function doAsyncTask() {\n  const url = (\n    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/comments?' +\n    new URLSearchParams({ postId: 1 }).toString()\n  );\n\n  const result = await fetch(url)\n    .then(response =&gt; response.json());\n\n  console.log('Fetched from: ' + url);\n  console.log(result);\n}\n\ndoAsyncTask();</code></pre>\n</div>\n</div>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>If you are using/supporting...</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>IE:</strong> Internet Explorer does not provide native support for URLSearchParams or fetch, but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/fetch-ie8\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Node:</strong> As of Node 18 there is native support for the fetch API (in version 17.5 it was behind the <code>--experimental-fetch</code> flag). In older versions, you can add the fetch API through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>. URLSearchParams comes with Node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Node + TypeScript:</strong> If you're using Node and TypeScript together you'll find that, due to some technical limitations, TypeScript does not offer type definitions for the global URLSearchParams. The simplest workaround is to just import it from the <code>url</code> module. See <a href=\"https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped/issues/34960\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">here</a> for more info.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"Fix casing","revision_guid":"CE8D6D13-D7F0-4D11-95F9-0BF9531F1DC9"},{"user":{"account_id":10438857,"reputation":13785,"user_id":7696223,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-m7rmWbqxL8s/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAo/GsqJGi8qLdI/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Scotty Jamison","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7696223/scotty-jamison"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1650502113,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":16,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise, modern approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>How it works:</strong> When a string (e.g. the URL) is being concatenated with an instance of <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/URLSearchParams\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams</a>, <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">its toString()</a> method will automatically be called to convert the instance into a string representation, which happens to be a properly encoded query string. If the automatic invoking of <code>toString()</code> is too magical for your liking, you may prefer to explicitly call it like so: <code>fetch('https://...' + new URLSearchParams(...).toString())</code></p>\n<p>A complete example of a fetch request with query parameters:</p>\n<p><div class=\"snippet\" data-lang=\"js\" data-hide=\"true\" data-console=\"true\" data-babel=\"false\" data-babel-preset-react=\"false\" data-babel-preset-ts=\"false\">\n<div class=\"snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden\">\n<pre class=\"snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>// Real example you can copy-paste and play with.\n// jsonplaceholder.typicode.com provides a dummy rest-api\n// for this sort of purpose.\nasync function doAsyncTask() {\n  const url = (\n    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/comments?' +\n    new URLSearchParams({ postId: 1 }).toString()\n  );\n\n  const result = await fetch(url)\n    .then(response =&gt; response.json());\n\n  console.log('Fetched from: ' + url);\n  console.log(result);\n}\n\ndoAsyncTask();</code></pre>\n</div>\n</div>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>If you are using/supporting...</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>IE:</strong> Internet Explorer does not provide native support for URLSearchParams or fetch, but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/fetch-ie8\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Node:</strong> As of Node 18 there is native support for the fetch API (in version 17.5 it was behind the <code>--experimental-fetch</code> flag). In older versions, you can add the fetch API through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>. URLSearchParams comes with node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Node + TypeScript:</strong> If you're using node and typescript together you'll find that, due to some technical limitations, typescript does not offer type definitions for the global URLSearchParams. The simplest workaround is to just import it from the <code>url</code> module. See <a href=\"https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped/issues/34960\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">here</a> for more info.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"added 1240 characters in body","revision_guid":"E74E901E-B6B6-4E73-8F9A-41C0E4F0D66C"},{"user":{"account_id":10438857,"reputation":13785,"user_id":7696223,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-m7rmWbqxL8s/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAo/GsqJGi8qLdI/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Scotty Jamison","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7696223/scotty-jamison"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1650501276,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":15,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise, modern approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams's toString()</a> function will convert the provided query args into a string. In JavaScript, when you concatenate an object with a string, that object's toString() function will automatically be called for you. You may prefer to be more explicit about what's happening here, and choose to call <code>.toString()</code> yourself before concatenating the two values together, like this: <code>fetch('https://...' + new URLSearchParams(...).toString())</code></p>\n<p>A complete example of a fetch request with query parameters:</p>\n<p><div class=\"snippet\" data-lang=\"js\" data-hide=\"true\" data-console=\"true\" data-babel=\"false\" data-babel-preset-react=\"false\" data-babel-preset-ts=\"false\">\n<div class=\"snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden\">\n<pre class=\"snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>// Real example you can copy-paste and play with.\n// jsonplaceholder.typicode.com provides a dummy rest-api\n// for this sort of purpose.\nasync function doAsyncTask() {\n  const url = (\n    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/comments?' +\n    new URLSearchParams({ postId: 1 }).toString()\n  );\n\n  const result = await fetch(url)\n    .then(response =&gt; response.json());\n\n  console.log('Fetched from: ' + url);\n  console.log(result);\n}\n\ndoAsyncTask();</code></pre>\n</div>\n</div>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>If you are using/supporting...</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>IE:</strong> Internet Explored does not provide native support for URLSearchParams or fetch, but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/fetch-ie8\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Node:</strong> As of Node 18 there is native support for the fetch API (in version 17.5 it was behind the <code>--experimental-fetch</code> flag). In older versions, you can add the fetch API through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>. URLSearchParams comes with node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Node + TypeScript:</strong> If you're using node and typescript together you'll find that, due to some technical limitations, typescript does not offer type definitions for the global URLSearchParams. The simplest workaround is to just import it from the <code>url</code> module. See <a href=\"https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped/issues/34960\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">here</a> for more info.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"added 1240 characters in body","revision_guid":"269C0491-DECA-4C7A-8A1F-6B2A3E749869"},{"user":{"account_id":10438857,"reputation":13785,"user_id":7696223,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-m7rmWbqxL8s/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAo/GsqJGi8qLdI/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Scotty Jamison","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7696223/scotty-jamison"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1650500724,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":14,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise, modern approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams's toString()</a> function will convert the provided query args into a string. In JavaScript, when you concatenate an object with a string, that object's toString() function will automatically be called for you. You may prefer to be more explicit about what's happening here, and choose to call <code>.toString()</code> yourself before concatenating the two values together, like this: <code>fetch('https://...' + new URLSearchParams(...).toString())</code></p>\n<hr />\n<p>Some more complete examples:</p>\n<p><div class=\"snippet\" data-lang=\"js\" data-hide=\"true\" data-console=\"true\" data-babel=\"false\" data-babel-preset-react=\"false\" data-babel-preset-ts=\"false\">\n<div class=\"snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden\">\n<pre class=\"snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>// Logs out: http://example.com?query=Hello+World%21&amp;x=2\nconsole.log(\n  'http://example.com?' +\n  new URLSearchParams({\n    query: 'Hello World!',\n    x: 2,\n  })\n);\n\n// Also logs out: http://example.com?query=Hello+World%21&amp;x=2\nconsole.log(\n  'http://example.com?' +\n  new URLSearchParams({\n    query: 'Hello World!',\n    x: 2,\n  }).toString()\n);\n\n// Real example you can copy-paste and play with.\n// jsonplaceholder.typicode.com provides a dummy rest-api\n// for this sort of purpose.\nasync function doAsyncTask() {\n  const url = (\n    'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/comments?' +\n    new URLSearchParams({ postId: 1 }).toString()\n  );\n\n  console.log('Fetching from: ' + url)\n\n  const result = await fetch(url)\n    .then(response =&gt; response.json());\n\n  console.log(result);\n}\n\ndoAsyncTask();</code></pre>\n</div>\n</div>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Special instructions for different platforms/tools:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><strong>IE:</strong> Internet Explored does not provide native support for URLSearchParams or fetch, but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/fetch-ie8\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Node:</strong> As of Node 18 there is native support for the fetch API (in version 17.5 it was behind the <code>--experimental-fetch</code> flag). In older versions, you can add the fetch API through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>. URLSearchParams comes with node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Node+TypeScript:</strong> If you're using node and typescript together you'll find that, due to some technical limitations, typescript does not offer type definitions for the global URLSearchParams. The simplest workaround is to just import it from the <code>url</code> module. See <a href=\"https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped/issues/34960\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">here</a> for more info.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"added 1240 characters in body","revision_guid":"4B3E38B3-4A0E-4EB7-84A0-D61C723C1858"},{"user":{"account_id":10438857,"reputation":13785,"user_id":7696223,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-m7rmWbqxL8s/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAo/GsqJGi8qLdI/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Scotty Jamison","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7696223/scotty-jamison"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1648648538,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":13,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise, modern approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams's toString()</a> function will convert the provided query args into a string. In JavaScript, when you concatenate an object with a string, that object's toString() function will automatically be called for you. You may prefer to be more explicit about what's happening here, and choose to call .toString() yourself before concatenating the two values together, like this: <code>fetch('https://...' + new URLSearchParams(...).toString())</code></p>\n<hr />\n<p>IE does not support URLSearchParams (or fetch), but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/fetch-ie8\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available</a>.</p>\n<p>If using node, you can add the fetch API through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>. Alternatively, there's currently <a href=\"https://nodejs.org/en/blog/release/v17.5.0/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">experimental support for it</a> in version 17.5 if you run node with the <code>--experimental-fetch</code> flag. URLSearchParams comes with node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code>.</p>\n<p>If you're using node and typescript together, you'll find that, due to some technical limitations, typescript does not offer type definitions for the global URLSearchParams. The simplist workaround is to just import it from the <code>url</code> module. See <a href=\"https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped/issues/34960\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">here</a> for more info.</p>\n","comment":"added 2 characters in body","revision_guid":"7CAD233C-A974-4723-9C4E-181A813F85AE"},{"user":{"account_id":151321,"reputation":28128,"user_id":365102,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/4cFxJ9Lj.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Mateen Ulhaq","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/365102/mateen-ulhaq"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1648550948,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":44,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<h2>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h2>\n<p>For dictionaries <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>, their shallowly-merged dictionary <code>z</code> takes values from <code>y</code>, replacing those from <code>x</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>In Python 3.9.0 or greater (released 17 October 2020, <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0584/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>PEP-584</code></a>, <a href=\"https://bugs.python.org/issue36144\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">discussed here</a>):</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = x | y\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>In Python 3.5 or greater:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>In Python 2, (or 3.4 or lower) write a function:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    z = x.copy()   # start with keys and values of x\n    z.update(y)    # modifies z with keys and values of y\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Explanation</h3>\n<p>Say you have two dictionaries and you want to merge them into a new dictionary without altering the original dictionaries:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The desired result is to get a new dictionary (<code>z</code>) with the values merged, and the second dictionary's values overwriting those from the first.</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A new syntax for this, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a> and <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available as of Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression.</p>\n<p>Note that we can merge in with literal notation as well:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = {**x, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into the <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, you may wish to do this in a backward-compatible way. The classically Pythonic way, available in Python 2 and Python 3.0-3.4, is to do this as a two-step process:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>In both approaches, <code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>b</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<h2>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a <em>single expression</em></h2>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5 or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a <em>single expression</em>, the most performant while the correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Given two dictionaries, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an arbitrary number of dictionaries, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    Given any number of dictionaries, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key-value pairs in latter dictionaries.\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dictionaries. e.g. given dictionaries <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key-value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dictionaries <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h2>Critiques of Other Answers</h2>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the formerly accepted answer:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example). Even if your values are hashable, <strong>since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where <code>y</code> should have precedence, but instead the value from <code>x</code> is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor and is very fast and memory-efficient (even slightly more so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic.</p>\n<p>Here's an example of the usage being <a href=\"https://code.djangoproject.com/attachment/ticket/13357/django-pypy.2.diff\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">remediated in django</a>.</p>\n<p>Dictionaries are intended to take hashable keys (e.g. <code>frozenset</code>s or tuples), but <strong>this method fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally, I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is my understanding (as well as the understanding of the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099485.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">creator of the language</a>) that the intended usage for <code>dict(**y)</code> is for creating dictionaries for readability purposes, e.g.:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>dict(a=1, b=10, c=11)\n</code></pre>\n<p>instead of</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>{'a': 1, 'b': 10, 'c': 11}\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Response to comments</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Despite what Guido says, <code>dict(x, **y)</code> is in line with the dict specification, which btw. works for both Python 2 and 3. The fact that this only works for string keys is a direct consequence of how keyword parameters work and not a short-coming of dict. Nor is using the ** operator in this place an abuse of the mechanism, in fact, ** was designed precisely to pass dictionaries as keywords.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Again, it doesn't work for 3 when keys are not strings. The implicit calling contract is that namespaces take ordinary dictionaries, while users must only pass keyword arguments that are strings. All other callables enforced it. <code>dict</code> broke this consistency in Python 2:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; foo(**{('a', 'b'): None})\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: foo() keywords must be strings\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(**{('a', 'b'): None})\n{('a', 'b'): None}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This inconsistency was bad given other implementations of Python (PyPy, Jython, IronPython). Thus it was fixed in Python 3, as this usage could be a breaking change.</p>\n<p>I submit to you that it is malicious incompetence to intentionally write code that only works in one version of a language or that only works given certain arbitrary constraints.</p>\n<p>More comments:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>dict(x.items() + y.items())</code> is still the most readable solution for Python 2. Readability counts.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>My response: <code>merge_two_dicts(x, y)</code> actually seems much clearer to me, if we're actually concerned about readability. And it is not forward compatible, as Python 2 is increasingly deprecated.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>{**x, **y}</code> does not seem to handle nested dictionaries. the contents of nested keys are simply overwritten, not merged [...] I ended up being burnt by these answers that do not merge recursively and I was surprised no one mentioned it. In my interpretation of the word &quot;merging&quot; these answers describe &quot;updating one dict with another&quot;, and not merging.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes. I must refer you back to the question, which is asking for a <em>shallow</em> merge of <em><strong>two</strong></em> dictionaries, with the first's values being overwritten by the second's - in a single expression.</p>\n<p>Assuming two dictionaries of dictionaries, one might recursively merge them in a single function, but you should be careful not to modify the dictionaries from either source, and the surest way to avoid that is to make a copy when assigning values. As keys must be hashable and are usually therefore immutable, it is pointless to copy them:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>from copy import deepcopy\n\ndef dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y):\n    z = {}\n    overlapping_keys = x.keys() &amp; y.keys()\n    for key in overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = dict_of_dicts_merge(x[key], y[key])\n    for key in x.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(x[key])\n    for key in y.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(y[key])\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>Usage:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a':{1:{}}, 'b': {2:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b':{10:{}}, 'c': {11:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y)\n{'b': {2: {}, 10: {}}, 'a': {1: {}}, 'c': {11: {}}}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Coming up with contingencies for other value types is far beyond the scope of this question, so I will point you at <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/a/24088493/541136\">my answer to the canonical question on a &quot;Dictionaries of dictionaries merge&quot;</a>.</p>\n<h2>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h2>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dictionaries have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dictionaries manually inside a <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0274/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">dict comprehension</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in Python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()) # iteritems in Python 2\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>from itertools import chain\nz = dict(chain(x.items(), y.items())) # iteritems in Python 2\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Performance Analysis</h2>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly. (Self-contained so you can copy and paste yourself.)</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>from timeit import repeat\nfrom itertools import chain\n\nx = dict.fromkeys('abcdefg')\ny = dict.fromkeys('efghijk')\n\ndef merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n\nmin(repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\nmin(repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\nmin(repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()}))\nmin(repeat(lambda: dict(chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\nmin(repeat(lambda: dict(item for d in (x, y) for item in d.items())))\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.8.1, NixOS:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n1.0804965235292912\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n1.636518670246005\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()}))\n3.1779992282390594\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: dict(chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.740647904574871\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: dict(item for d in (x, y) for item in d.items())))\n4.266070580109954\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"lang-shell prettyprint-override\"><code>$ uname -a\nLinux nixos 4.19.113 #1-NixOS SMP Wed Mar 25 07:06:15 UTC 2020 x86_64 GNU/Linux\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Resources on Dictionaries</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/327311/how-are-pythons-built-in-dictionaries-implemented/44509302#44509302\">My explanation of Python's <strong>dictionary implementation</strong>, updated for 3.6.</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1024847/add-new-keys-to-a-dictionary/27208535#27208535\">Answer on how to add new keys to a dictionary</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/209840/map-two-lists-into-a-dictionary-in-python/33737067#33737067\">Mapping two lists into a dictionary</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#dictionaries\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The official Python docs on dictionaries</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=66P5FMkWoVU\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The Dictionary Even Mightier</a> - talk by Brandon Rhodes at Pycon 2017</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=npw4s1QTmPg\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Modern Python Dictionaries, A Confluence of Great Ideas</a> - talk by Raymond Hettinger at Pycon 2017</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"Reword to separate &quot;`x` and `y`, `z`&quot; for clarity. Also make title format to one line.","revision_guid":"09FCB149-C84E-4AF3-B9DB-8F786BDA3EDA"},{"user":{"account_id":10438857,"reputation":13785,"user_id":7696223,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-m7rmWbqxL8s/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAo/GsqJGi8qLdI/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Scotty Jamison","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7696223/scotty-jamison"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1646689274,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":12,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise, modern approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams's toString()</a> function will convert the provided query args into a string. In JavaScript, when you concatenate an object with a string, that object's toString() function will automatically be called for you. You may prefer to be more explicit about what's happening here, and choose to call .toString() yourself before concatenating the two values together, like this: <code>fetch('https://...' + new URLSearchParams(...).toString())</code></p>\n<hr />\n<p>IE does not support URLSearchParams (or fetch), but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/fetch-ie8\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available</a>.</p>\n<p>If using node, you can add the fetch API through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>. Alternatively, there's currently <a href=\"https://nodejs.org/en/blog/release/v17.5.0/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">experimental support for it</a> in version 17.5 if you run node with the <code>--experimental-fetch</code> flag. URLSearchParams comes with node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code>.</p>\n<p>If you're using node and typescript together, you'll find that, due to some technical limitations, typescript does not offer type definitions for the global URLSearchParams. The simplist workaround is to just import it from the URL module. See <a href=\"https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped/issues/34960\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">here</a> for more info.</p>\n","comment":"added 188 characters in body","revision_guid":"A9DDF143-16DE-4B48-B7B2-EF5CF0DA1344"},{"user":{"account_id":51914,"reputation":69254,"user_id":155137,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/00f9d78517e7e17837e0e7ac227305ca?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Martijn Courteaux","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/155137/martijn-courteaux"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1636275265,"post_id":53954986,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":16,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h3>How to deal with <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> in Pandas?</h3>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This post is meant for readers who,</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Would like to understand what this warning means</li>\n<li>Would like to understand different ways of suppressing this warning</li>\n<li>Would like to understand how to improve their code and follow good practices to avoid this warning in the future.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Setup</strong></p>\n<pre><code>np.random.seed(0)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(np.random.choice(10, (3, 5)), columns=list('ABCDE'))\ndf\n   A  B  C  D  E\n0  5  0  3  3  7\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<h1>What is the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>?</h1>\n<p>To know how to deal with this warning, it is important to understand what it means and why it is raised in the first place.</p>\n<p>When filtering DataFrames, it is possible slice/index a frame to return either a <em>view</em>, or a <em>copy</em>, depending on the internal layout and various implementation details. A &quot;view&quot; is, as the term suggests, a view into the original data, so modifying the view may modify the original object. On the other hand, a &quot;copy&quot; is a replication of data from the original, and modifying the copy has no effect on the original.</p>\n<p>As mentioned by other answers, the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> was created to flag &quot;chained assignment&quot; operations. Consider <code>df</code> in the setup above. Suppose you would like to select all values in column &quot;B&quot; where values in column &quot;A&quot; is &gt; 5. Pandas allows you to do this in different ways, some more correct than others. For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B']\n \n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B']\n\n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>These return the same result, so if you are only reading these values, it makes no difference. So, what is the issue? The problem with chained assignment, is that it is generally difficult to predict whether a view or a copy is returned, <strong>so this largely becomes an issue when you are attempting to assign values back.</strong> To build on the earlier example, consider how this code is executed by the interpreter:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__setitem__((df.A &gt; 5, 'B'), 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>With a single <code>__setitem__</code> call to <code>df</code>. OTOH, consider this code:</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__getitem__(df.A &gt; 5).__setitem__('B', 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now, depending on whether <code>__getitem__</code> returned a view or a copy, the <code>__setitem__</code> operation <strong>may not work</strong>.</p>\n<p>In general, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.loc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>loc</code></a> for label-based assignment, and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iloc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iloc</code></a> for integer/positional based assignment, as the spec guarantees that they always operate on the original. Additionally, for setting a single cell, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.at.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>at</code></a> and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iat.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iat</code></a>.</p>\n<p>More can be found in the <a href=\"http://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/indexing.html#returning-a-view-versus-a-copy\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">documentation</a>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Note</strong><br />\nAll boolean indexing operations done with <code>loc</code> can also be done with <code>iloc</code>. The only difference is that <code>iloc</code> expects either\nintegers/positions for index or a numpy array of boolean values, and\ninteger/position indexes for the columns.</p>\n<p>For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written nas</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[(df.A &gt; 5).values, 1] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'A'] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written as</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[1, 0] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>And so on.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h1>Just tell me how to suppress the warning!</h1>\n<p>Consider a simple operation on the &quot;A&quot; column of <code>df</code>. Selecting &quot;A&quot; and dividing by 2 will raise the warning, but the operation will work.</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']]\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n/Library/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.6/lib/python3.6/site-packages/IPython/__main__.py:1: SettingWithCopyWarning: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n\ndf2\n     A\n0  2.5\n1  4.5\n2  3.5\n</code></pre>\n<p>There are a couple ways of directly silencing this warning:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p>(recommended) <strong>Use <code>loc</code> to slice subsets</strong>:</p>\n<pre><code> df2 = df.loc[:, ['A']]\n df2['A'] /= 2     # Does not raise \n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Change <code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment</code></strong><br />\nCan be set to <code>None</code>, <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code>, or <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code>. <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code> is the default. <code>None</code> will suppress the warning entirely, and <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code> will throw a <code>SettingWithCopyError</code>, preventing the operation from going through.</p>\n<pre><code> pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = None\n df2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Make a <code>deepcopy</code></strong></p>\n<pre><code> df2 = df[['A']].copy(deep=True)\n df2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/20625582/how-to-deal-with-settingwithcopywarning-in-pandas#comment62589711_20625582\">@Peter Cotton</a> in the comments, came up with a nice way of non-intrusively changing the mode (modified from <a href=\"https://gist.github.com/notbanker/2be3ed34539c86e22ffdd88fd95ad8bc\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this gist</a>) using a context manager, to set the mode only as long as it is required, and the reset it back to the original state when finished.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>class ChainedAssignent:\n    def __init__(self, chained=None):\n        acceptable = [None, 'warn', 'raise']\n        assert chained in acceptable, &quot;chained must be in &quot; + str(acceptable)\n        self.swcw = chained\n\n    def __enter__(self):\n        self.saved_swcw = pd.options.mode.chained_assignment\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.swcw\n        return self\n\n    def __exit__(self, *args):\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.saved_swcw\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The usage is as follows:</p>\n<pre><code># some code here\nwith ChainedAssignent():\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n# more code follows\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or, to raise the exception</p>\n<pre><code>with ChainedAssignent(chained='raise'):\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n\nSettingWithCopyError: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<h1>The &quot;XY Problem&quot;: What am I doing wrong?</h1>\n<p>A lot of the time, users attempt to look for ways of suppressing this exception without fully understanding why it was raised in the first place. This is a good example of an <a href=\"https://meta.stackexchange.com/q/66377\">XY problem</a>, where users attempt to solve a problem &quot;Y&quot; that is actually a symptom of a deeper rooted problem &quot;X&quot;. Questions will be raised based on common problems that encounter this warning, and solutions will then be presented.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 1</strong><br />\nI have a DataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>df\n       A  B  C  D  E\n    0  5  0  3  3  7\n    1  9  3  5  2  4\n    2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I want to assign values in col &quot;A&quot; &gt; 5 to 1000. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>      A  B  C  D  E\n0     5  0  3  3  7\n1  1000  3  5  2  4\n2  1000  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Wrong way to do this:</p>\n<pre><code>df.A[df.A &gt; 5] = 1000         # works, because df.A returns a view\ndf[df.A &gt; 5]['A'] = 1000      # does not work\ndf.loc[df.A &gt; 5]['A'] = 1000   # does not work\n</code></pre>\n<p>Right way using <code>loc</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'A'] = 1000\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 2<sup>1</sup></strong><br />\nI am trying to set the value in cell (1, 'D') to 12345. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C      D  E\n0  5  0  3      3  7\n1  9  3  5  12345  4\n2  7  6  8      8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I have tried different ways of accessing this cell, such as\n<code>df['D'][1]</code>. What is the best way to do this?</p>\n<p><sub>1. This question isn't specifically related to the warning, but\nit is good to understand how to do this particular operation correctly\nso as to avoid situations where the warning could potentially arise in\nfuture.</sub></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You can use any of the following methods to do this.</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iloc[1, 3] = 12345\ndf.at[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iat[1, 3] = 12345\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 3</strong><br />\nI am trying to subset values based on some condition. I have a\nDataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I would like to assign values in &quot;D&quot; to 123 such that &quot;C&quot; == 5. I\ntried</p>\n<pre><code>df2.loc[df2.C == 5, 'D'] = 123\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which seems fine but I am <em>still</em> getting the\n<code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>! How do I fix this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is actually probably because of code higher up in your pipeline. Did you create <code>df2</code> from something larger, like</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>? In this case, boolean indexing will return a view, so <code>df2</code> will reference the original. What you'd need to do is assign <code>df2</code> to a <em>copy</em>:</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5].copy()\n# Or,\n# df2 = df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, :]\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 4</strong><br />\nI'm trying to drop column &quot;C&quot; in-place from</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>But using</p>\n<pre><code>df2.drop('C', axis=1, inplace=True)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Throws <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>. Why is this happening?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is because <code>df2</code> must have been created as a view from some other slicing operation, such as</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>The solution here is to either make a <code>copy()</code> of <code>df</code>, or use <code>loc</code>, as before.</p>\n\n","comment":"Fixed a quote.","revision_guid":"04717136-A7D6-4CD6-A98C-74CEA1BF0852"},{"user":{"account_id":7040330,"reputation":133,"user_id":5924929,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/CeweI.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Bernat","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/5924929/bernat"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1636196510,"post_id":53954986,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":15,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h3>How to deal with <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> in Pandas?</h3>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This post is meant for readers who,</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Would like to understand what this warning means</li>\n<li>Would like to understand different ways of suppressing this warning</li>\n<li>Would like to understand how to improve their code and follow good practices to avoid this warning in the future.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Setup</strong></p>\n<pre><code>np.random.seed(0)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(np.random.choice(10, (3, 5)), columns=list('ABCDE'))\ndf\n   A  B  C  D  E\n0  5  0  3  3  7\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<h1>What is the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>?</h1>\n<p>To know how to deal with this warning, it is important to understand what it means and why it is raised in the first place.</p>\n<p>When filtering DataFrames, it is possible slice/index a frame to return either a <em>view</em>, or a <em>copy</em>, depending on the internal layout and various implementation details. A &quot;view&quot; is, as the term suggests, a view into the original data, so modifying the view may modify the original object. On the other hand, a &quot;copy&quot; is a replication of data from the original, and modifying the copy has no effect on the original.</p>\n<p>As mentioned by other answers, the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> was created to flag &quot;chained assignment&quot; operations. Consider <code>df</code> in the setup above. Suppose you would like to select all values in column &quot;B&quot; where values in column &quot;A&quot; is &gt; 5. Pandas allows you to do this in different ways, some more correct than others. For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B']\n \n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B']\n\n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>These return the same result, so if you are only reading these values, it makes no difference. So, what is the issue? The problem with chained assignment, is that it is generally difficult to predict whether a view or a copy is returned, <strong>so this largely becomes an issue when you are attempting to assign values back.</strong> To build on the earlier example, consider how this code is executed by the interpreter:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__setitem__((df.A &gt; 5, 'B'), 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>With a single <code>__setitem__</code> call to <code>df</code>. OTOH, consider this code:</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__getitem__(df.A &gt; 5).__setitem__('B&quot;, 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now, depending on whether <code>__getitem__</code> returned a view or a copy, the <code>__setitem__</code> operation <strong>may not work</strong>.</p>\n<p>In general, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.loc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>loc</code></a> for label-based assignment, and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iloc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iloc</code></a> for integer/positional based assignment, as the spec guarantees that they always operate on the original. Additionally, for setting a single cell, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.at.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>at</code></a> and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iat.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iat</code></a>.</p>\n<p>More can be found in the <a href=\"http://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/indexing.html#returning-a-view-versus-a-copy\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">documentation</a>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Note</strong><br />\nAll boolean indexing operations done with <code>loc</code> can also be done with <code>iloc</code>. The only difference is that <code>iloc</code> expects either\nintegers/positions for index or a numpy array of boolean values, and\ninteger/position indexes for the columns.</p>\n<p>For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written nas</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[(df.A &gt; 5).values, 1] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'A'] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written as</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[1, 0] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>And so on.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h1>Just tell me how to suppress the warning!</h1>\n<p>Consider a simple operation on the &quot;A&quot; column of <code>df</code>. Selecting &quot;A&quot; and dividing by 2 will raise the warning, but the operation will work.</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']]\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n/Library/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.6/lib/python3.6/site-packages/IPython/__main__.py:1: SettingWithCopyWarning: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n\ndf2\n     A\n0  2.5\n1  4.5\n2  3.5\n</code></pre>\n<p>There are a couple ways of directly silencing this warning:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p>(recommended) <strong>Use <code>loc</code> to slice subsets</strong>:</p>\n<pre><code> df2 = df.loc[:, ['A']]\n df2['A'] /= 2     # Does not raise \n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Change <code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment</code></strong><br />\nCan be set to <code>None</code>, <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code>, or <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code>. <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code> is the default. <code>None</code> will suppress the warning entirely, and <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code> will throw a <code>SettingWithCopyError</code>, preventing the operation from going through.</p>\n<pre><code> pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = None\n df2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Make a <code>deepcopy</code></strong></p>\n<pre><code> df2 = df[['A']].copy(deep=True)\n df2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/20625582/how-to-deal-with-settingwithcopywarning-in-pandas#comment62589711_20625582\">@Peter Cotton</a> in the comments, came up with a nice way of non-intrusively changing the mode (modified from <a href=\"https://gist.github.com/notbanker/2be3ed34539c86e22ffdd88fd95ad8bc\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this gist</a>) using a context manager, to set the mode only as long as it is required, and the reset it back to the original state when finished.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>class ChainedAssignent:\n    def __init__(self, chained=None):\n        acceptable = [None, 'warn', 'raise']\n        assert chained in acceptable, &quot;chained must be in &quot; + str(acceptable)\n        self.swcw = chained\n\n    def __enter__(self):\n        self.saved_swcw = pd.options.mode.chained_assignment\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.swcw\n        return self\n\n    def __exit__(self, *args):\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.saved_swcw\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The usage is as follows:</p>\n<pre><code># some code here\nwith ChainedAssignent():\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n# more code follows\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or, to raise the exception</p>\n<pre><code>with ChainedAssignent(chained='raise'):\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n\nSettingWithCopyError: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<h1>The &quot;XY Problem&quot;: What am I doing wrong?</h1>\n<p>A lot of the time, users attempt to look for ways of suppressing this exception without fully understanding why it was raised in the first place. This is a good example of an <a href=\"https://meta.stackexchange.com/q/66377\">XY problem</a>, where users attempt to solve a problem &quot;Y&quot; that is actually a symptom of a deeper rooted problem &quot;X&quot;. Questions will be raised based on common problems that encounter this warning, and solutions will then be presented.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 1</strong><br />\nI have a DataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>df\n       A  B  C  D  E\n    0  5  0  3  3  7\n    1  9  3  5  2  4\n    2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I want to assign values in col &quot;A&quot; &gt; 5 to 1000. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>      A  B  C  D  E\n0     5  0  3  3  7\n1  1000  3  5  2  4\n2  1000  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Wrong way to do this:</p>\n<pre><code>df.A[df.A &gt; 5] = 1000         # works, because df.A returns a view\ndf[df.A &gt; 5]['A'] = 1000      # does not work\ndf.loc[df.A &gt; 5]['A'] = 1000   # does not work\n</code></pre>\n<p>Right way using <code>loc</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'A'] = 1000\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 2<sup>1</sup></strong><br />\nI am trying to set the value in cell (1, 'D') to 12345. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C      D  E\n0  5  0  3      3  7\n1  9  3  5  12345  4\n2  7  6  8      8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I have tried different ways of accessing this cell, such as\n<code>df['D'][1]</code>. What is the best way to do this?</p>\n<p><sub>1. This question isn't specifically related to the warning, but\nit is good to understand how to do this particular operation correctly\nso as to avoid situations where the warning could potentially arise in\nfuture.</sub></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You can use any of the following methods to do this.</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iloc[1, 3] = 12345\ndf.at[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iat[1, 3] = 12345\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 3</strong><br />\nI am trying to subset values based on some condition. I have a\nDataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I would like to assign values in &quot;D&quot; to 123 such that &quot;C&quot; == 5. I\ntried</p>\n<pre><code>df2.loc[df2.C == 5, 'D'] = 123\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which seems fine but I am <em>still</em> getting the\n<code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>! How do I fix this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is actually probably because of code higher up in your pipeline. Did you create <code>df2</code> from something larger, like</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>? In this case, boolean indexing will return a view, so <code>df2</code> will reference the original. What you'd need to do is assign <code>df2</code> to a <em>copy</em>:</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5].copy()\n# Or,\n# df2 = df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, :]\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 4</strong><br />\nI'm trying to drop column &quot;C&quot; in-place from</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>But using</p>\n<pre><code>df2.drop('C', axis=1, inplace=True)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Throws <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>. Why is this happening?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is because <code>df2</code> must have been created as a view from some other slicing operation, such as</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>The solution here is to either make a <code>copy()</code> of <code>df</code>, or use <code>loc</code>, as before.</p>\n\n","comment":"I suppose this line is supposed not to work because the column reference is not inside the .loc[ ] method, not because it lacks the required &quot;&gt;&quot; symbol the rest of the cases (right or wrong) have. Sorry for the html comment at the end, just to trick the &quot;at least change 6 characters&quot; rule for edit.","revision_guid":"6E6AE969-3E23-49D3-B868-294B96C31A7B"},{"user":{"account_id":10438857,"reputation":13785,"user_id":7696223,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-m7rmWbqxL8s/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAo/GsqJGi8qLdI/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Scotty Jamison","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7696223/scotty-jamison"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1634328001,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":11,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise, modern approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams's toString()</a> function will convert the provided query args into a string. In JavaScript, when you concatenate an object with a string, that object's toString() function will automatically be called for you. You may prefer to be more explicit about what's happening here, and choose to call .toString() yourself before concatenating the two values together, like this: <code>fetch('https://...' + new URLSearchParams(...).toString())</code></p>\n<hr />\n<p>IE does not support URLSearchParams (or fetch), but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/fetch-ie8\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available</a>.</p>\n<p>If using node, you can add the fetch API through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>. URLSearchParams comes with node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code>.</p>\n<p>If you're using node and typescript together, you'll find that, due to some technical limitations, typescript does not offer type definitions for the global URLSearchParams. The simplist workaround is to just import it from the URL module. See <a href=\"https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped/issues/34960\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">here</a> for more info.</p>\n","comment":"added 173 characters in body","revision_guid":"C59FA0C8-2340-480A-B9F3-DFD95E4E1DC8"},{"user":{"account_id":10438857,"reputation":13785,"user_id":7696223,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-m7rmWbqxL8s/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAo/GsqJGi8qLdI/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Scotty Jamison","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7696223/scotty-jamison"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1630158585,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":10,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise, modern approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams's</a> toString() function will convert the query args into a string that can be appended onto the URL. In this example, toString() is called implicitly when it gets concatenated with the URL. You may wish to explicitly call toString() to show your intentions.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>IE does not support URLSearchParams (or fetch), but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/fetch-ie8\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available</a>.</p>\n<p>If using node, you can add the fetch API through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>. URLSearchParams comes with node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code>.</p>\n<p>If you're using node and typescript together, you'll find that, due to some technical limitations, typescript does not offer type definitions for the global URLSearchParams. The simplist workaround is to just import it from the URL module. See <a href=\"https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped/issues/34960\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">here</a> for more info.</p>\n","comment":"Added explanation on how to use it with typescript","revision_guid":"250E6D9C-A9B4-4E77-96AD-BE996C433148"},{"user":{"account_id":10438857,"reputation":13785,"user_id":7696223,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-m7rmWbqxL8s/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAo/GsqJGi8qLdI/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Scotty Jamison","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7696223/scotty-jamison"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1628538967,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":9,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise, modern approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams's</a> toString() function will convert the query args into a string that can be appended onto the URL. In this example, toString() is called implicitly when it gets concatenated with the URL. You may wish to explicitly call toString() to show your intentions.</p>\n<p>IE does not support URLSearchParams (or fetch), but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/fetch-ie8\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available</a>.</p>\n<p>If using node, you can add the fetch API through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>. URLSearchParams comes with node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code>.</p>\n","comment":"Took out &quot;ES6 approach&quot;, the example really had little to do with EcmaScript version 6, and more to do with semi-recent additions to the browser APIs.","revision_guid":"FC749989-72E0-4FEF-B363-C5680E53D6A0"},{"user":{"user_type":"does_not_exist","display_name":"user16442705"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1626773614,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":43,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h2>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h2>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For dictionaries <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>, <code>z</code> becomes a shallowly-merged dictionary with values from <code>y</code> replacing those from <code>x</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>In Python 3.9.0 or greater (released 17 October 2020): <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0584/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP-584</a>, <a href=\"https://bugs.python.org/issue36144\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">discussed here</a>, was implemented and provides the simplest method:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = x | y          # NOTE: 3.9+ ONLY\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>In Python 3.5 or greater:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>In Python 2, (or 3.4 or lower) write a function:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    z = x.copy()   # start with keys and values of x\n    z.update(y)    # modifies z with keys and values of y\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Explanation</h3>\n<p>Say you have two dictionaries and you want to merge them into a new dictionary without altering the original dictionaries:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The desired result is to get a new dictionary (<code>z</code>) with the values merged, and the second dictionary's values overwriting those from the first.</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A new syntax for this, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a> and <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available as of Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression.</p>\n<p>Note that we can merge in with literal notation as well:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = {**x, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into the <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, you may wish to do this in a backward-compatible way. The classically Pythonic way, available in Python 2 and Python 3.0-3.4, is to do this as a two-step process:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>In both approaches, <code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>b</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<h2>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a <em>single expression</em></h2>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5 or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a <em>single expression</em>, the most performant while the correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Given two dictionaries, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an arbitrary number of dictionaries, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    Given any number of dictionaries, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key-value pairs in latter dictionaries.\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dictionaries. e.g. given dictionaries <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key-value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dictionaries <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h2>Critiques of Other Answers</h2>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the formerly accepted answer:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example). Even if your values are hashable, <strong>since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where <code>y</code> should have precedence, but instead the value from <code>x</code> is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor and is very fast and memory-efficient (even slightly more so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic.</p>\n<p>Here's an example of the usage being <a href=\"https://code.djangoproject.com/attachment/ticket/13357/django-pypy.2.diff\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">remediated in django</a>.</p>\n<p>Dictionaries are intended to take hashable keys (e.g. <code>frozenset</code>s or tuples), but <strong>this method fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally, I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is my understanding (as well as the understanding of the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099485.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">creator of the language</a>) that the intended usage for <code>dict(**y)</code> is for creating dictionaries for readability purposes, e.g.:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>dict(a=1, b=10, c=11)\n</code></pre>\n<p>instead of</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>{'a': 1, 'b': 10, 'c': 11}\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Response to comments</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Despite what Guido says, <code>dict(x, **y)</code> is in line with the dict specification, which btw. works for both Python 2 and 3. The fact that this only works for string keys is a direct consequence of how keyword parameters work and not a short-coming of dict. Nor is using the ** operator in this place an abuse of the mechanism, in fact, ** was designed precisely to pass dictionaries as keywords.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Again, it doesn't work for 3 when keys are not strings. The implicit calling contract is that namespaces take ordinary dictionaries, while users must only pass keyword arguments that are strings. All other callables enforced it. <code>dict</code> broke this consistency in Python 2:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; foo(**{('a', 'b'): None})\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: foo() keywords must be strings\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(**{('a', 'b'): None})\n{('a', 'b'): None}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This inconsistency was bad given other implementations of Python (PyPy, Jython, IronPython). Thus it was fixed in Python 3, as this usage could be a breaking change.</p>\n<p>I submit to you that it is malicious incompetence to intentionally write code that only works in one version of a language or that only works given certain arbitrary constraints.</p>\n<p>More comments:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>dict(x.items() + y.items())</code> is still the most readable solution for Python 2. Readability counts.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>My response: <code>merge_two_dicts(x, y)</code> actually seems much clearer to me, if we're actually concerned about readability. And it is not forward compatible, as Python 2 is increasingly deprecated.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>{**x, **y}</code> does not seem to handle nested dictionaries. the contents of nested keys are simply overwritten, not merged [...] I ended up being burnt by these answers that do not merge recursively and I was surprised no one mentioned it. In my interpretation of the word &quot;merging&quot; these answers describe &quot;updating one dict with another&quot;, and not merging.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes. I must refer you back to the question, which is asking for a <em>shallow</em> merge of <em><strong>two</strong></em> dictionaries, with the first's values being overwritten by the second's - in a single expression.</p>\n<p>Assuming two dictionaries of dictionaries, one might recursively merge them in a single function, but you should be careful not to modify the dictionaries from either source, and the surest way to avoid that is to make a copy when assigning values. As keys must be hashable and are usually therefore immutable, it is pointless to copy them:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>from copy import deepcopy\n\ndef dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y):\n    z = {}\n    overlapping_keys = x.keys() &amp; y.keys()\n    for key in overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = dict_of_dicts_merge(x[key], y[key])\n    for key in x.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(x[key])\n    for key in y.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(y[key])\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>Usage:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a':{1:{}}, 'b': {2:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b':{10:{}}, 'c': {11:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y)\n{'b': {2: {}, 10: {}}, 'a': {1: {}}, 'c': {11: {}}}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Coming up with contingencies for other value types is far beyond the scope of this question, so I will point you at <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/a/24088493/541136\">my answer to the canonical question on a &quot;Dictionaries of dictionaries merge&quot;</a>.</p>\n<h2>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h2>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dictionaries have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dictionaries manually inside a <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0274/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">dict comprehension</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in Python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()) # iteritems in Python 2\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>from itertools import chain\nz = dict(chain(x.items(), y.items())) # iteritems in Python 2\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Performance Analysis</h2>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly. (Self-contained so you can copy and paste yourself.)</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>from timeit import repeat\nfrom itertools import chain\n\nx = dict.fromkeys('abcdefg')\ny = dict.fromkeys('efghijk')\n\ndef merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n\nmin(repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\nmin(repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\nmin(repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()}))\nmin(repeat(lambda: dict(chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\nmin(repeat(lambda: dict(item for d in (x, y) for item in d.items())))\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.8.1, NixOS:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n1.0804965235292912\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n1.636518670246005\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()}))\n3.1779992282390594\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: dict(chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.740647904574871\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: dict(item for d in (x, y) for item in d.items())))\n4.266070580109954\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"lang-shell prettyprint-override\"><code>$ uname -a\nLinux nixos 4.19.113 #1-NixOS SMP Wed Mar 25 07:06:15 UTC 2020 x86_64 GNU/Linux\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Resources on Dictionaries</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/327311/how-are-pythons-built-in-dictionaries-implemented/44509302#44509302\">My explanation of Python's <strong>dictionary implementation</strong>, updated for 3.6.</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1024847/add-new-keys-to-a-dictionary/27208535#27208535\">Answer on how to add new keys to a dictionary</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/209840/map-two-lists-into-a-dictionary-in-python/33737067#33737067\">Mapping two lists into a dictionary</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#dictionaries\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The official Python docs on dictionaries</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=66P5FMkWoVU\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The Dictionary Even Mightier</a> - talk by Brandon Rhodes at Pycon 2017</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=npw4s1QTmPg\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Modern Python Dictionaries, A Confluence of Great Ideas</a> - talk by Raymond Hettinger at Pycon 2017</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"Formatting, minor tweaks","revision_guid":"9930EA11-02A7-439A-A266-2738A43E4219"},{"user":{"account_id":10438857,"reputation":13785,"user_id":7696223,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-m7rmWbqxL8s/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAo/GsqJGi8qLdI/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Scotty Jamison","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7696223/scotty-jamison"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1619798531,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":8,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise ES6 approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams's</a> toString() function will convert the query args into a string that can be appended onto the URL. In this example, toString() is called implicitly when it gets concatenated with the URL. You may wish to explicitly call toString() to show your intentions.</p>\n<p>IE does not support URLSearchParams (or fetch), but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/fetch-ie8\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available</a>.</p>\n<p>If using node, you can add the fetch API through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>. URLSearchParams comes with node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code>.</p>\n","comment":"deleted 7 characters in body","revision_guid":"6A89E39E-E6C2-44CF-B42A-5B13D9B7A447"},{"user":{"account_id":12466567,"reputation":131,"user_id":9076272,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh5.googleusercontent.com/-TZVB7TpTZKY/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAACc/aM7EoYfAkrk/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Lina Achaji","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/9076272/lina-achaji"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1616089263,"post_id":66696341,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":1,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>As suggested in the last comment, we can use the class introduced by <a href=\"https://nlp.seas.harvard.edu/2018/04/03/attention.html#optimizer\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">https://nlp.seas.harvard.edu/2018/04/03/attention.html#optimizer</a>. But this answer will give an error unless we define a function to update the state_dict.</p>\n<p>So here's the full Scheduler:</p>\n<pre><code>class NoamOpt:\n    &quot;Optim wrapper that implements rate.&quot;\n    def __init__(self, model_size, warmup, optimizer):\n        self.optimizer = optimizer\n        self._step = 0\n        self.warmup = warmup\n        self.model_size = model_size\n        self._rate = 0\n    \n    def state_dict(self):\n        &quot;&quot;&quot;Returns the state of the warmup scheduler as a :class:`dict`.\n        It contains an entry for every variable in self.__dict__ which\n        is not the optimizer.\n        &quot;&quot;&quot;\n        return {key: value for key, value in self.__dict__.items() if key != 'optimizer'}\n    \n    def load_state_dict(self, state_dict):\n        &quot;&quot;&quot;Loads the warmup scheduler's state.\n        Arguments:\n            state_dict (dict): warmup scheduler state. Should be an object returned\n                from a call to :meth:`state_dict`.\n        &quot;&quot;&quot;\n        self.__dict__.update(state_dict) \n        \n    def step(self):\n        &quot;Update parameters and rate&quot;\n        self._step += 1\n        rate = self.rate()\n        for p in self.optimizer.param_groups:\n            p['lr'] = rate\n        self._rate = rate\n        self.optimizer.step()\n        \n    def rate(self, step = None):\n        &quot;Implement `lrate` above&quot;\n        if step is None:\n            step = self._step\n        return (self.model_size ** (-0.5) *\n            min(step ** (-0.5), step * self.warmup ** (-1.5))) \n</code></pre>\n<p>Later, to use it inside the training loop:</p>\n<pre><code>optimizer = NoamOpt(input_opts['d_model'], 500,\n            torch.optim.Adam(model.parameters(), lr=0, betas=(0.9, 0.98), eps=1e-9))\n</code></pre>\n<p>.\n.\n.</p>\n<pre><code>optimizer.step()\n</code></pre>\n","revision_guid":"442B0D4B-0F24-479B-9645-8F68267AEED9"},{"user":{"account_id":9550226,"reputation":41,"user_id":15215464,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/9b6b7a36d6549765907f9aa72a1ff34c?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Hannes82","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/15215464/hannes82"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1613410317,"post_id":66212699,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":1,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<pre><code>class NoamOpt:\n&quot;Optim wrapper that implements rate.&quot;\ndef __init__(self, model_size, factor, warmup, optimizer):\n    self.optimizer = optimizer\n    self._step = 0\n    self.warmup = warmup\n    self.factor = factor\n    self.model_size = model_size\n    self._rate = 0\n    \ndef step(self):\n    &quot;Update parameters and rate&quot;\n    self._step += 1\n    rate = self.rate()\n    for p in self.optimizer.param_groups:\n        p['lr'] = rate\n    self._rate = rate\n    self.optimizer.step()\n    \ndef rate(self, step = None):\n    &quot;Implement `lrate` above&quot;\n    if step is None:\n        step = self._step\n    return self.factor * \\\n        (self.model_size ** (-0.5) *\n        min(step ** (-0.5), step * self.warmup ** (-1.5)))\n    \ndef get_std_opt(model):\n    return NoamOpt(model.src_embed[0].d_model, 2, 4000,torch.optim.Adam(model.parameters(), lr=0, betas=(0.9, 0.98), eps=1e-9))\n</code></pre>\n<p>As in: <a href=\"https://nlp.seas.harvard.edu/2018/04/03/attention.html#optimizer\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">https://nlp.seas.harvard.edu/2018/04/03/attention.html#optimizer</a></p>\n","revision_guid":"5D69F007-BD70-4CD5-9269-8344E02A9236"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1608299846,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":42,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h2>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h2>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For dictionaries <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>, <code>z</code> becomes a shallowly merged dictionary with values from <code>y</code> replacing those from <code>x</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>In Python 3.9.0 or greater (released 17 October 2020): <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0584/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP-584</a>, <a href=\"https://bugs.python.org/issue36144\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">discussed here</a>, was implemented and provides the simplest method:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = x | y          # NOTE: 3.9+ ONLY\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>In Python 3.5 or greater:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>In Python 2, (or 3.4 or lower) write a function:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    z = x.copy()   # start with x's keys and values\n    z.update(y)    # modifies z with y's keys and values &amp; returns None\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Explanation</h3>\n<p>Say you have two dictionaries and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dictionaries:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The desired result is to get a new dictionary (<code>z</code>) with the values merged, and the second dictionary's values overwriting those from the first.</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A new syntax for this, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a> and <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available as of Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression.</p>\n<p>Note that we can merge in with literal notation as well:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = {**x, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, you may wish to do this in a backward-compatible way. The classically Pythonic way, available in Python 2 and Python 3.0-3.4, is to do this as a two-step process:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>In both approaches, <code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<h2>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a <em>single expression</em></h2>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5 or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a <em>single expression</em>, the most performant while the correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Given two dictionaries, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dictionaries, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    Given any number of dictionaries, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key-value pairs in latter dictionaries.\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dictionaries. e.g. given dictionaries <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key-value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dictionaries <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h2>Critiques of Other Answers</h2>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the formerly accepted answer:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example). Even if your values are hashable, <strong>since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor and is very fast and memory-efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic.</p>\n<p>Here's an example of the usage being <a href=\"https://code.djangoproject.com/attachment/ticket/13357/django-pypy.2.diff\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">remediated in django</a>.</p>\n<p>Dictionaries are intended to take hashable keys (e.g. frozensets or tuples), but <strong>this method fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally, I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is my understanding (as well as the understanding of the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099485.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">creator of the language</a>) that the intended usage for <code>dict(**y)</code> is for creating dictionaries for readability purposes, e.g.:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>dict(a=1, b=10, c=11)\n</code></pre>\n<p>instead of</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>{'a': 1, 'b': 10, 'c': 11}\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Response to comments</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Despite what Guido says, <code>dict(x, **y)</code> is in line with the dict specification, which btw. works for both Python 2 and 3. The fact that this only works for string keys is a direct consequence of how keyword parameters work and not a short-coming of dict. Nor is using the ** operator in this place an abuse of the mechanism, in fact, ** was designed precisely to pass dictionaries as keywords.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Again, it doesn't work for 3 when keys are non-strings. The implicit calling contract is that namespaces take ordinary dictionaries, while users must only pass keyword arguments that are strings. All other callables enforced it. <code>dict</code> broke this consistency in Python 2:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; foo(**{('a', 'b'): None})\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: foo() keywords must be strings\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(**{('a', 'b'): None})\n{('a', 'b'): None}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This inconsistency was bad given other implementations of Python (Pypy, Jython, IronPython). Thus it was fixed in Python 3, as this usage could be a breaking change.</p>\n<p>I submit to you that it is malicious incompetence to intentionally write code that only works in one version of a language or that only works given certain arbitrary constraints.</p>\n<p>More comments:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>dict(x.items() + y.items())</code> is still the most readable solution for Python 2. Readability counts.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>My response: <code>merge_two_dicts(x, y)</code> actually seems much clearer to me, if we're actually concerned about readability. And it is not forward compatible, as Python 2 is increasingly deprecated.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>{**x, **y}</code> does not seem to handle nested dictionaries. the contents of nested keys are simply overwritten, not merged [...] I ended up being burnt by these answers that do not merge recursively and I was surprised no one mentioned it. In my interpretation of the word &quot;merging&quot; these answers describe &quot;updating one dict with another&quot;, and not merging.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes. I must refer you back to the question, which is asking for a <em>shallow</em> merge of <em><strong>two</strong></em> dictionaries, with the first's values being overwritten by the second's - in a single expression.</p>\n<p>Assuming two dictionaries of dictionaries, one might recursively merge them in a single function, but you should be careful not to modify the dictionaries from either source, and the surest way to avoid that is to make a copy when assigning values. As keys must be hashable and are usually therefore immutable, it is pointless to copy them:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>from copy import deepcopy\n\ndef dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y):\n    z = {}\n    overlapping_keys = x.keys() &amp; y.keys()\n    for key in overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = dict_of_dicts_merge(x[key], y[key])\n    for key in x.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(x[key])\n    for key in y.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(y[key])\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>Usage:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a':{1:{}}, 'b': {2:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b':{10:{}}, 'c': {11:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y)\n{'b': {2: {}, 10: {}}, 'a': {1: {}}, 'c': {11: {}}}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Coming up with contingencies for other value types is far beyond the scope of this question, so I will point you at <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/a/24088493/541136\">my answer to the canonical question on a &quot;Dictionaries of dictionaries merge&quot;</a>.</p>\n<h2>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h2>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dictionaries have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dictionaries manually inside a <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0274/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">dict comprehension</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()) # iteritems in Python 2\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>from itertools import chain\nz = dict(chain(x.items(), y.items())) # iteritems in Python 2\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Performance Analysis</h2>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly. (Self-contained so you can copy and paste yourself.)</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>from timeit import repeat\nfrom itertools import chain\n\nx = dict.fromkeys('abcdefg')\ny = dict.fromkeys('efghijk')\n\ndef merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n\nmin(repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\nmin(repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\nmin(repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()}))\nmin(repeat(lambda: dict(chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\nmin(repeat(lambda: dict(item for d in (x, y) for item in d.items())))\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.8.1, NixOS:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n1.0804965235292912\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n1.636518670246005\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()}))\n3.1779992282390594\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: dict(chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.740647904574871\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: dict(item for d in (x, y) for item in d.items())))\n4.266070580109954\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"lang-shell prettyprint-override\"><code>$ uname -a\nLinux nixos 4.19.113 #1-NixOS SMP Wed Mar 25 07:06:15 UTC 2020 x86_64 GNU/Linux\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Resources on Dictionaries</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/327311/how-are-pythons-built-in-dictionaries-implemented/44509302#44509302\">My explanation of Python's <strong>dictionary implementation</strong>, updated for 3.6.</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1024847/add-new-keys-to-a-dictionary/27208535#27208535\">Answer on how to add new keys to a dictionary</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/209840/map-two-lists-into-a-dictionary-in-python/33737067#33737067\">Mapping two lists into a dictionary</a></li>\n<li>The official Python <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#dictionaries\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">docs on dictionaries</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=66P5FMkWoVU\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The Dictionary Even Mightier</a> - talk by Brandon Rhodes at Pycon 2017</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=npw4s1QTmPg\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Modern Python Dictionaries, A Confluence of Great Ideas</a> - talk by Raymond Hettinger at Pycon 2017</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"probably shouldn&#39;t be fixing grammar on quotes, but in this case, the grammar fix was wrong, it is &quot;the fact... is&quot; not &quot;the fact... are&quot;.","revision_guid":"F3111230-08E5-495E-9154-3B92F8011979"},{"user":{"account_id":18009677,"reputation":228,"user_id":13089670,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/CEGYS.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Samuel Sorial","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/13089670/samuel-sorial"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1608299714,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":41,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h2>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h2>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For dictionaries <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>, <code>z</code> becomes a shallowly merged dictionary with values from <code>y</code> replacing those from <code>x</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>In Python 3.9.0 or greater (released 17 October 2020): <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0584/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP-584</a>, <a href=\"https://bugs.python.org/issue36144\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">discussed here</a>, was implemented and provides the simplest method:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = x | y          # NOTE: 3.9+ ONLY\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>In Python 3.5 or greater:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>In Python 2, (or 3.4 or lower) write a function:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    z = x.copy()   # start with x's keys and values\n    z.update(y)    # modifies z with y's keys and values &amp; returns None\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Explanation</h3>\n<p>Say you have two dictionaries and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dictionaries:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The desired result is to get a new dictionary (<code>z</code>) with the values merged, and the second dictionary's values overwriting those from the first.</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A new syntax for this, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a> and <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available as of Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression.</p>\n<p>Note that we can merge in with literal notation as well:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = {**x, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, you may wish to do this in a backward-compatible way. The classically Pythonic way, available in Python 2 and Python 3.0-3.4, is to do this as a two-step process:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>In both approaches, <code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<h2>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a <em>single expression</em></h2>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5 or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a <em>single expression</em>, the most performant while the correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Given two dictionaries, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dictionaries, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    Given any number of dictionaries, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key-value pairs in latter dictionaries.\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dictionaries. e.g. given dictionaries <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key-value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dictionaries <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h2>Critiques of Other Answers</h2>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the formerly accepted answer:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example). Even if your values are hashable, <strong>since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor and is very fast and memory-efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic.</p>\n<p>Here's an example of the usage being <a href=\"https://code.djangoproject.com/attachment/ticket/13357/django-pypy.2.diff\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">remediated in django</a>.</p>\n<p>Dictionaries are intended to take hashable keys (e.g. frozensets or tuples), but <strong>this method fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally, I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is my understanding (as well as the understanding of the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099485.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">creator of the language</a>) that the intended usage for <code>dict(**y)</code> is for creating dictionaries for readability purposes, e.g.:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>dict(a=1, b=10, c=11)\n</code></pre>\n<p>instead of</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>{'a': 1, 'b': 10, 'c': 11}\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Response to comments</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Despite what Guido says, <code>dict(x, **y)</code> is in line with the dict specification, which btw. works for both Python 2 and 3. The fact that this only works for string keys are a direct consequence of how keyword parameters work and not a short-coming of dict. Nor is using the ** operator in this place an abuse of the mechanism, in fact, ** was designed precisely to pass dictionaries as keywords.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Again, it doesn't work for 3 when keys are non-strings. The implicit calling contract is that namespaces take ordinary dictionaries, while users must only pass keyword arguments that are strings. All other callables enforced it. <code>dict</code> broke this consistency in Python 2:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; foo(**{('a', 'b'): None})\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: foo() keywords must be strings\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(**{('a', 'b'): None})\n{('a', 'b'): None}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This inconsistency was bad given other implementations of Python (Pypy, Jython, IronPython). Thus it was fixed in Python 3, as this usage could be a breaking change.</p>\n<p>I submit to you that it is malicious incompetence to intentionally write code that only works in one version of a language or that only works given certain arbitrary constraints.</p>\n<p>More comments:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>dict(x.items() + y.items())</code> is still the most readable solution for Python 2. Readability counts.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>My response: <code>merge_two_dicts(x, y)</code> actually seems much clearer to me, if we're actually concerned about readability. And it is not forward compatible, as Python 2 is increasingly deprecated.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>{**x, **y}</code> does not seem to handle nested dictionaries. the contents of nested keys are simply overwritten, not merged [...] I ended up being burnt by these answers that do not merge recursively and I was surprised no one mentioned it. In my interpretation of the word &quot;merging&quot; these answers describe &quot;updating one dict with another&quot;, and not merging.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes. I must refer you back to the question, which is asking for a <em>shallow</em> merge of <em><strong>two</strong></em> dictionaries, with the first's values being overwritten by the second's - in a single expression.</p>\n<p>Assuming two dictionaries of dictionaries, one might recursively merge them in a single function, but you should be careful not to modify the dictionaries from either source, and the surest way to avoid that is to make a copy when assigning values. As keys must be hashable and are usually therefore immutable, it is pointless to copy them:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>from copy import deepcopy\n\ndef dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y):\n    z = {}\n    overlapping_keys = x.keys() &amp; y.keys()\n    for key in overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = dict_of_dicts_merge(x[key], y[key])\n    for key in x.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(x[key])\n    for key in y.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(y[key])\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>Usage:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a':{1:{}}, 'b': {2:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b':{10:{}}, 'c': {11:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y)\n{'b': {2: {}, 10: {}}, 'a': {1: {}}, 'c': {11: {}}}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Coming up with contingencies for other value types is far beyond the scope of this question, so I will point you at <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/a/24088493/541136\">my answer to the canonical question on a &quot;Dictionaries of dictionaries merge&quot;</a>.</p>\n<h2>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h2>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dictionaries have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dictionaries manually inside a <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0274/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">dict comprehension</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()) # iteritems in Python 2\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>from itertools import chain\nz = dict(chain(x.items(), y.items())) # iteritems in Python 2\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Performance Analysis</h2>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly. (Self-contained so you can copy and paste yourself.)</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>from timeit import repeat\nfrom itertools import chain\n\nx = dict.fromkeys('abcdefg')\ny = dict.fromkeys('efghijk')\n\ndef merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n\nmin(repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\nmin(repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\nmin(repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()}))\nmin(repeat(lambda: dict(chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\nmin(repeat(lambda: dict(item for d in (x, y) for item in d.items())))\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.8.1, NixOS:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n1.0804965235292912\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n1.636518670246005\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()}))\n3.1779992282390594\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: dict(chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.740647904574871\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: dict(item for d in (x, y) for item in d.items())))\n4.266070580109954\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"lang-shell prettyprint-override\"><code>$ uname -a\nLinux nixos 4.19.113 #1-NixOS SMP Wed Mar 25 07:06:15 UTC 2020 x86_64 GNU/Linux\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Resources on Dictionaries</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/327311/how-are-pythons-built-in-dictionaries-implemented/44509302#44509302\">My explanation of Python's <strong>dictionary implementation</strong>, updated for 3.6.</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1024847/add-new-keys-to-a-dictionary/27208535#27208535\">Answer on how to add new keys to a dictionary</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/209840/map-two-lists-into-a-dictionary-in-python/33737067#33737067\">Mapping two lists into a dictionary</a></li>\n<li>The official Python <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#dictionaries\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">docs on dictionaries</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=66P5FMkWoVU\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The Dictionary Even Mightier</a> - talk by Brandon Rhodes at Pycon 2017</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=npw4s1QTmPg\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Modern Python Dictionaries, A Confluence of Great Ideas</a> - talk by Raymond Hettinger at Pycon 2017</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"Grammar improvements","revision_guid":"516E2AA0-7B02-4B23-9D68-BBCBE3E376FA"},{"user":{"account_id":6321039,"reputation":407685,"user_id":4909087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Zcszo.png?s=256","display_name":"coldspeed95","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/4909087/coldspeed95"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1608292808,"post_id":53954986,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":14,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h3>How to deal with <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> in Pandas?</h3>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This post is meant for readers who,</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Would like to understand what this warning means</li>\n<li>Would like to understand different ways of suppressing this warning</li>\n<li>Would like to understand how to improve their code and follow good practices to avoid this warning in the future.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Setup</strong></p>\n<pre><code>np.random.seed(0)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(np.random.choice(10, (3, 5)), columns=list('ABCDE'))\ndf\n   A  B  C  D  E\n0  5  0  3  3  7\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<h1>What is the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>?</h1>\n<p>To know how to deal with this warning, it is important to understand what it means and why it is raised in the first place.</p>\n<p>When filtering DataFrames, it is possible slice/index a frame to return either a <em>view</em>, or a <em>copy</em>, depending on the internal layout and various implementation details. A &quot;view&quot; is, as the term suggests, a view into the original data, so modifying the view may modify the original object. On the other hand, a &quot;copy&quot; is a replication of data from the original, and modifying the copy has no effect on the original.</p>\n<p>As mentioned by other answers, the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> was created to flag &quot;chained assignment&quot; operations. Consider <code>df</code> in the setup above. Suppose you would like to select all values in column &quot;B&quot; where values in column &quot;A&quot; is &gt; 5. Pandas allows you to do this in different ways, some more correct than others. For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B']\n \n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B']\n\n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>These return the same result, so if you are only reading these values, it makes no difference. So, what is the issue? The problem with chained assignment, is that it is generally difficult to predict whether a view or a copy is returned, <strong>so this largely becomes an issue when you are attempting to assign values back.</strong> To build on the earlier example, consider how this code is executed by the interpreter:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__setitem__((df.A &gt; 5, 'B'), 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>With a single <code>__setitem__</code> call to <code>df</code>. OTOH, consider this code:</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__getitem__(df.A &gt; 5).__setitem__('B&quot;, 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now, depending on whether <code>__getitem__</code> returned a view or a copy, the <code>__setitem__</code> operation <strong>may not work</strong>.</p>\n<p>In general, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.loc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>loc</code></a> for label-based assignment, and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iloc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iloc</code></a> for integer/positional based assignment, as the spec guarantees that they always operate on the original. Additionally, for setting a single cell, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.at.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>at</code></a> and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iat.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iat</code></a>.</p>\n<p>More can be found in the <a href=\"http://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/indexing.html#returning-a-view-versus-a-copy\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">documentation</a>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Note</strong><br />\nAll boolean indexing operations done with <code>loc</code> can also be done with <code>iloc</code>. The only difference is that <code>iloc</code> expects either\nintegers/positions for index or a numpy array of boolean values, and\ninteger/position indexes for the columns.</p>\n<p>For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written nas</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[(df.A &gt; 5).values, 1] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'A'] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written as</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[1, 0] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>And so on.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h1>Just tell me how to suppress the warning!</h1>\n<p>Consider a simple operation on the &quot;A&quot; column of <code>df</code>. Selecting &quot;A&quot; and dividing by 2 will raise the warning, but the operation will work.</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']]\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n/Library/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.6/lib/python3.6/site-packages/IPython/__main__.py:1: SettingWithCopyWarning: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n\ndf2\n     A\n0  2.5\n1  4.5\n2  3.5\n</code></pre>\n<p>There are a couple ways of directly silencing this warning:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p>(recommended) <strong>Use <code>loc</code> to slice subsets</strong>:</p>\n<pre><code> df2 = df.loc[:, ['A']]\n df2['A'] /= 2     # Does not raise \n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Change <code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment</code></strong><br />\nCan be set to <code>None</code>, <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code>, or <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code>. <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code> is the default. <code>None</code> will suppress the warning entirely, and <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code> will throw a <code>SettingWithCopyError</code>, preventing the operation from going through.</p>\n<pre><code> pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = None\n df2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Make a <code>deepcopy</code></strong></p>\n<pre><code> df2 = df[['A']].copy(deep=True)\n df2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/20625582/how-to-deal-with-settingwithcopywarning-in-pandas#comment62589711_20625582\">@Peter Cotton</a> in the comments, came up with a nice way of non-intrusively changing the mode (modified from <a href=\"https://gist.github.com/notbanker/2be3ed34539c86e22ffdd88fd95ad8bc\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this gist</a>) using a context manager, to set the mode only as long as it is required, and the reset it back to the original state when finished.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>class ChainedAssignent:\n    def __init__(self, chained=None):\n        acceptable = [None, 'warn', 'raise']\n        assert chained in acceptable, &quot;chained must be in &quot; + str(acceptable)\n        self.swcw = chained\n\n    def __enter__(self):\n        self.saved_swcw = pd.options.mode.chained_assignment\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.swcw\n        return self\n\n    def __exit__(self, *args):\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.saved_swcw\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The usage is as follows:</p>\n<pre><code># some code here\nwith ChainedAssignent():\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n# more code follows\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or, to raise the exception</p>\n<pre><code>with ChainedAssignent(chained='raise'):\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n\nSettingWithCopyError: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<h1>The &quot;XY Problem&quot;: What am I doing wrong?</h1>\n<p>A lot of the time, users attempt to look for ways of suppressing this exception without fully understanding why it was raised in the first place. This is a good example of an <a href=\"https://meta.stackexchange.com/q/66377\">XY problem</a>, where users attempt to solve a problem &quot;Y&quot; that is actually a symptom of a deeper rooted problem &quot;X&quot;. Questions will be raised based on common problems that encounter this warning, and solutions will then be presented.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 1</strong><br />\nI have a DataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>df\n       A  B  C  D  E\n    0  5  0  3  3  7\n    1  9  3  5  2  4\n    2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I want to assign values in col &quot;A&quot; &gt; 5 to 1000. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>      A  B  C  D  E\n0     5  0  3  3  7\n1  1000  3  5  2  4\n2  1000  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Wrong way to do this:</p>\n<pre><code>df.A[df.A &gt; 5] = 1000         # works, because df.A returns a view\ndf[df.A &gt; 5]['A'] = 1000      # does not work\ndf.loc[df.A  5]['A'] = 1000   # does not work\n</code></pre>\n<p>Right way using <code>loc</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'A'] = 1000\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 2<sup>1</sup></strong><br />\nI am trying to set the value in cell (1, 'D') to 12345. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C      D  E\n0  5  0  3      3  7\n1  9  3  5  12345  4\n2  7  6  8      8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I have tried different ways of accessing this cell, such as\n<code>df['D'][1]</code>. What is the best way to do this?</p>\n<p><sub>1. This question isn't specifically related to the warning, but\nit is good to understand how to do this particular operation correctly\nso as to avoid situations where the warning could potentially arise in\nfuture.</sub></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You can use any of the following methods to do this.</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iloc[1, 3] = 12345\ndf.at[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iat[1, 3] = 12345\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 3</strong><br />\nI am trying to subset values based on some condition. I have a\nDataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I would like to assign values in &quot;D&quot; to 123 such that &quot;C&quot; == 5. I\ntried</p>\n<pre><code>df2.loc[df2.C == 5, 'D'] = 123\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which seems fine but I am <em>still</em> getting the\n<code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>! How do I fix this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is actually probably because of code higher up in your pipeline. Did you create <code>df2</code> from something larger, like</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>? In this case, boolean indexing will return a view, so <code>df2</code> will reference the original. What you'd need to do is assign <code>df2</code> to a <em>copy</em>:</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5].copy()\n# Or,\n# df2 = df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, :]\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 4</strong><br />\nI'm trying to drop column &quot;C&quot; in-place from</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>But using</p>\n<pre><code>df2.drop('C', axis=1, inplace=True)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Throws <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>. Why is this happening?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is because <code>df2</code> must have been created as a view from some other slicing operation, such as</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>The solution here is to either make a <code>copy()</code> of <code>df</code>, or use <code>loc</code>, as before.</p>\n","comment":"added 135 characters in body","revision_guid":"88BDA221-D29F-4A33-90D2-CBB65EA99633"},{"user":{"account_id":10273952,"reputation":1696,"user_id":7581507,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/30ff67710c5c3561f96b435f5d040b34?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Alonme","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7581507/alonme"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1607523051,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":40,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h2>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h2>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For dictionaries <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>, <code>z</code> becomes a shallowly merged dictionary with values from <code>y</code> replacing those from <code>x</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>In Python 3.9.0 or greater (released 17 October 2020): <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0584/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP-584</a>, <a href=\"https://bugs.python.org/issue36144\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">discussed here</a>, was implemented and provides the simplest method:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = x | y          # NOTE: 3.9+ ONLY\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>In Python 3.5 or greater:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>In Python 2, (or 3.4 or lower) write a function:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    z = x.copy()   # start with x's keys and values\n    z.update(y)    # modifies z with y's keys and values &amp; returns None\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Explanation</h3>\n<p>Say you have two dictionaries and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dictionaries:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The desired result is to get a new dictionary (<code>z</code>) with the values merged, and the second dictionary's values overwriting those from the first.</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A new syntax for this, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a> and <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available as of Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression.</p>\n<p>Note that we can merge in with literal notation as well:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = {**x, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, you may wish to do this in a backwards compatible way. The classically Pythonic way, available in Python 2 and Python 3.0-3.4, is to do this as a two-step process:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>In both approaches, <code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<h2>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a <em>single expression</em></h2>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5, or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a <em>single expression</em>, the most performant while correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Given two dictionaries, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dictionaries, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    Given any number of dictionaries, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dictionaries.\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dictionaries. e.g. given dictionaries <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dictionaries <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h2>Critiques of Other Answers</h2>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the formerly accepted answer:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example). Even if your values are hashable, <strong>since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic.</p>\n<p>Here's an example of the usage being <a href=\"https://code.djangoproject.com/attachment/ticket/13357/django-pypy.2.diff\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">remediated in django</a>.</p>\n<p>Dictionaries are intended to take hashable keys (e.g. frozensets or tuples), but <strong>this method fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is my understanding (as well as the understanding of the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099485.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">creator of the language</a>) that the intended usage for <code>dict(**y)</code> is for creating dictionaries for readability purposes, e.g.:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>dict(a=1, b=10, c=11)\n</code></pre>\n<p>instead of</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>{'a': 1, 'b': 10, 'c': 11}\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Response to comments</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Despite what Guido says, <code>dict(x, **y)</code> is in line with the dict specification, which btw. works for both Python 2 and 3. The fact that this only works for string keys is a direct consequence of how keyword parameters work and not a short-comming of dict. Nor is using the ** operator in this place an abuse of the mechanism, in fact ** was designed precisely to pass dictionaries as keywords.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Again, it doesn't work for 3 when keys are non-strings. The implicit calling contract is that namespaces take ordinary dictionaries, while users must only pass keyword arguments that are strings. All other callables enforced it. <code>dict</code> broke this consistency in Python 2:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; foo(**{('a', 'b'): None})\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: foo() keywords must be strings\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(**{('a', 'b'): None})\n{('a', 'b'): None}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This inconsistency was bad given other implementations of Python (Pypy, Jython, IronPython). Thus it was fixed in Python 3, as this usage could be a breaking change.</p>\n<p>I submit to you that it is malicious incompetence to intentionally write code that only works in one version of a language or that only works given certain arbitrary constraints.</p>\n<p>More comments:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>dict(x.items() + y.items())</code> is still the most readable solution for Python 2. Readability counts.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>My response: <code>merge_two_dicts(x, y)</code> actually seems much clearer to me, if we're actually concerned about readability. And it is not forward compatible, as Python 2 is increasingly deprecated.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>{**x, **y}</code> does not seem to handle nested dictionaries. the contents of nested keys are simply overwritten, not merged [...] I ended up being burnt by these answers that do not merge recursively and I was surprised no one mentioned it. In my interpretation of the word &quot;merging&quot; these answers describe &quot;updating one dict with another&quot;, and not merging.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes. I must refer you back to the question, which is asking for a <em>shallow</em> merge of <em><strong>two</strong></em> dictionaries, with the first's values being overwritten by the second's - in a single expression.</p>\n<p>Assuming two dictionary of dictionaries, one might recursively merge them in a single function, but you should be careful not to modify the dictionaries from either source, and the surest way to avoid that is to make a copy when assigning values. As keys must be hashable and are usually therefore immutable, it is pointless to copy them:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>from copy import deepcopy\n\ndef dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y):\n    z = {}\n    overlapping_keys = x.keys() &amp; y.keys()\n    for key in overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = dict_of_dicts_merge(x[key], y[key])\n    for key in x.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(x[key])\n    for key in y.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(y[key])\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>Usage:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a':{1:{}}, 'b': {2:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b':{10:{}}, 'c': {11:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y)\n{'b': {2: {}, 10: {}}, 'a': {1: {}}, 'c': {11: {}}}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Coming up with contingencies for other value types is far beyond the scope of this question, so I will point you at <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/a/24088493/541136\">my answer to the canonical question on a &quot;Dictionaries of dictionaries merge&quot;</a>.</p>\n<h2>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h2>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dictionaries have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dictionaries manually inside a <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0274/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">dict comprehension</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()) # iteritems in Python 2\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>from itertools import chain\nz = dict(chain(x.items(), y.items())) # iteritems in Python 2\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Performance Analysis</h2>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly. (Self-contained so you can copy and paste yourself.)</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>from timeit import repeat\nfrom itertools import chain\n\nx = dict.fromkeys('abcdefg')\ny = dict.fromkeys('efghijk')\n\ndef merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n\nmin(repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\nmin(repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\nmin(repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()}))\nmin(repeat(lambda: dict(chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\nmin(repeat(lambda: dict(item for d in (x, y) for item in d.items())))\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.8.1, NixOS:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n1.0804965235292912\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n1.636518670246005\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()}))\n3.1779992282390594\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: dict(chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.740647904574871\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: dict(item for d in (x, y) for item in d.items())))\n4.266070580109954\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"lang-shell prettyprint-override\"><code>$ uname -a\nLinux nixos 4.19.113 #1-NixOS SMP Wed Mar 25 07:06:15 UTC 2020 x86_64 GNU/Linux\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Resources on Dictionaries</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/327311/how-are-pythons-built-in-dictionaries-implemented/44509302#44509302\">My explanation of Python's <strong>dictionary implementation</strong>, updated for 3.6.</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1024847/add-new-keys-to-a-dictionary/27208535#27208535\">Answer on how to add new keys to a dictionary</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/209840/map-two-lists-into-a-dictionary-in-python/33737067#33737067\">Mapping two lists into a dictionary</a></li>\n<li>The official Python <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#dictionaries\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">docs on dictionaries</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=66P5FMkWoVU\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The Dictionary Even Mightier</a> - talk by Brandon Rhodes at Pycon 2017</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=npw4s1QTmPg\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Modern Python Dictionaries, A Confluence of Great Ideas</a> - talk by Raymond Hettinger at Pycon 2017</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"Move newest version to be the first - as it should be the preferred way","revision_guid":"23CEAD00-0687-4AD9-BBAA-32606C181713"},{"user":{"account_id":5735532,"reputation":196,"user_id":5511326,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/ewKUZ.png?s=256","display_name":"quassy","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/5511326/quassy"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1603029614,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":39,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h2>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h2>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For dictionaries <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>, <code>z</code> becomes a shallowly merged dictionary with values from <code>y</code> replacing those from <code>x</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In Python 3.5 or greater:\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li>In Python 2, (or 3.4 or lower) write a function:\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    z = x.copy()   # start with x's keys and values\n    z.update(y)    # modifies z with y's keys and values &amp; returns None\n    return z\n</code></pre>\nand now:\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li>In Python 3.9.0 or greater (released 17 October 2020): <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0584/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP-584</a>, <a href=\"https://bugs.python.org/issue36144\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">discussed here</a>, was implemented to further simplify this:\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = x | y          # NOTE: 3.9+ ONLY\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Explanation</h3>\n<p>Say you have two dictionaries and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dictionaries:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The desired result is to get a new dictionary (<code>z</code>) with the values merged, and the second dictionary's values overwriting those from the first.</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A new syntax for this, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a> and <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available as of Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression.</p>\n<p>Note that we can merge in with literal notation as well:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = {**x, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, you may wish to do this in a backwards compatible way. The classically Pythonic way, available in Python 2 and Python 3.0-3.4, is to do this as a two-step process:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>In both approaches, <code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<h2>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a <em>single expression</em></h2>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5, or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a <em>single expression</em>, the most performant while correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Given two dictionaries, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dictionaries, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    Given any number of dictionaries, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dictionaries.\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dictionaries. e.g. given dictionaries <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dictionaries <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h2>Critiques of Other Answers</h2>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the formerly accepted answer:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example). Even if your values are hashable, <strong>since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic.</p>\n<p>Here's an example of the usage being <a href=\"https://code.djangoproject.com/attachment/ticket/13357/django-pypy.2.diff\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">remediated in django</a>.</p>\n<p>Dictionaries are intended to take hashable keys (e.g. frozensets or tuples), but <strong>this method fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is my understanding (as well as the understanding of the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099485.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">creator of the language</a>) that the intended usage for <code>dict(**y)</code> is for creating dictionaries for readability purposes, e.g.:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>dict(a=1, b=10, c=11)\n</code></pre>\n<p>instead of</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>{'a': 1, 'b': 10, 'c': 11}\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Response to comments</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Despite what Guido says, <code>dict(x, **y)</code> is in line with the dict specification, which btw. works for both Python 2 and 3. The fact that this only works for string keys is a direct consequence of how keyword parameters work and not a short-comming of dict. Nor is using the ** operator in this place an abuse of the mechanism, in fact ** was designed precisely to pass dictionaries as keywords.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Again, it doesn't work for 3 when keys are non-strings. The implicit calling contract is that namespaces take ordinary dictionaries, while users must only pass keyword arguments that are strings. All other callables enforced it. <code>dict</code> broke this consistency in Python 2:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; foo(**{('a', 'b'): None})\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: foo() keywords must be strings\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(**{('a', 'b'): None})\n{('a', 'b'): None}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This inconsistency was bad given other implementations of Python (Pypy, Jython, IronPython). Thus it was fixed in Python 3, as this usage could be a breaking change.</p>\n<p>I submit to you that it is malicious incompetence to intentionally write code that only works in one version of a language or that only works given certain arbitrary constraints.</p>\n<p>More comments:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>dict(x.items() + y.items())</code> is still the most readable solution for Python 2. Readability counts.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>My response: <code>merge_two_dicts(x, y)</code> actually seems much clearer to me, if we're actually concerned about readability. And it is not forward compatible, as Python 2 is increasingly deprecated.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>{**x, **y}</code> does not seem to handle nested dictionaries. the contents of nested keys are simply overwritten, not merged [...] I ended up being burnt by these answers that do not merge recursively and I was surprised no one mentioned it. In my interpretation of the word &quot;merging&quot; these answers describe &quot;updating one dict with another&quot;, and not merging.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes. I must refer you back to the question, which is asking for a <em>shallow</em> merge of <em><strong>two</strong></em> dictionaries, with the first's values being overwritten by the second's - in a single expression.</p>\n<p>Assuming two dictionary of dictionaries, one might recursively merge them in a single function, but you should be careful not to modify the dictionaries from either source, and the surest way to avoid that is to make a copy when assigning values. As keys must be hashable and are usually therefore immutable, it is pointless to copy them:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>from copy import deepcopy\n\ndef dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y):\n    z = {}\n    overlapping_keys = x.keys() &amp; y.keys()\n    for key in overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = dict_of_dicts_merge(x[key], y[key])\n    for key in x.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(x[key])\n    for key in y.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(y[key])\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>Usage:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a':{1:{}}, 'b': {2:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b':{10:{}}, 'c': {11:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y)\n{'b': {2: {}, 10: {}}, 'a': {1: {}}, 'c': {11: {}}}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Coming up with contingencies for other value types is far beyond the scope of this question, so I will point you at <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/a/24088493/541136\">my answer to the canonical question on a &quot;Dictionaries of dictionaries merge&quot;</a>.</p>\n<h2>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h2>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dictionaries have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dictionaries manually inside a <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0274/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">dict comprehension</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()) # iteritems in Python 2\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>from itertools import chain\nz = dict(chain(x.items(), y.items())) # iteritems in Python 2\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Performance Analysis</h2>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly. (Self-contained so you can copy and paste yourself.)</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>from timeit import repeat\nfrom itertools import chain\n\nx = dict.fromkeys('abcdefg')\ny = dict.fromkeys('efghijk')\n\ndef merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n\nmin(repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\nmin(repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\nmin(repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()}))\nmin(repeat(lambda: dict(chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\nmin(repeat(lambda: dict(item for d in (x, y) for item in d.items())))\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.8.1, NixOS:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n1.0804965235292912\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n1.636518670246005\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()}))\n3.1779992282390594\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: dict(chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.740647904574871\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: dict(item for d in (x, y) for item in d.items())))\n4.266070580109954\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"lang-shell prettyprint-override\"><code>$ uname -a\nLinux nixos 4.19.113 #1-NixOS SMP Wed Mar 25 07:06:15 UTC 2020 x86_64 GNU/Linux\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Resources on Dictionaries</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/327311/how-are-pythons-built-in-dictionaries-implemented/44509302#44509302\">My explanation of Python's <strong>dictionary implementation</strong>, updated for 3.6.</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1024847/add-new-keys-to-a-dictionary/27208535#27208535\">Answer on how to add new keys to a dictionary</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/209840/map-two-lists-into-a-dictionary-in-python/33737067#33737067\">Mapping two lists into a dictionary</a></li>\n<li>The official Python <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#dictionaries\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">docs on dictionaries</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=66P5FMkWoVU\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The Dictionary Even Mightier</a> - talk by Brandon Rhodes at Pycon 2017</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=npw4s1QTmPg\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Modern Python Dictionaries, A Confluence of Great Ideas</a> - talk by Raymond Hettinger at Pycon 2017</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"Python 3.9 is released by now","revision_guid":"30EFFC08-548E-4125-95CE-BC425E31064E"},{"user":{"account_id":10438857,"reputation":13785,"user_id":7696223,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-m7rmWbqxL8s/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAo/GsqJGi8qLdI/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Scotty Jamison","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7696223/scotty-jamison"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1602361659,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":7,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise ES6 approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams's</a> toString() function will convert the query args into a string that can be appended onto the URL. In this example, toString() is called implicitly when it gets concatenated with the URL. You will likely want to call toString() explicitly to improve readability.</p>\n<p>IE does not support URLSearchParams (or fetch), but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/fetch-ie8\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available</a>.</p>\n<p>If using node, you can add the fetch API through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>. URLSearchParams comes with node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code>.</p>\n","comment":"Added a link to a fetch pollyfil","revision_guid":"16AE8536-20F5-4FB3-BAEF-057183409BF5"},{"user":{"account_id":1699549,"reputation":20791,"user_id":1560035,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/fe3aa4a1766a44d8f27db8a95cb573ed?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"TheoretiCAL","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/1560035/theoretical"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1601686496,"post_id":64178448,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":2,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>From <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/38987/how-do-i-merge-two-dictionaries-in-a-single-expression-in-python-taking-union-o\">Aaron Hall's answer</a>:</p>\n<pre><code>from copy import deepcopy\n\ndef dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y):\n    z = {}\n    overlapping_keys = x.keys() &amp; y.keys()\n    for key in overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = dict_of_dicts_merge(x[key], y[key])\n    for key in x.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(x[key])\n    for key in y.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(y[key])\n    return z\n\nresult = {}\nfor dict_elem in all_dicts:\n   dict_of_dicts_merge(result, dict_elem)\n\nreturn result\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"added 136 characters in body","revision_guid":"776F6AC8-4164-4EA7-AA33-5070B23C1B1C"},{"user":{"account_id":1699549,"reputation":20791,"user_id":1560035,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/fe3aa4a1766a44d8f27db8a95cb573ed?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"TheoretiCAL","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/1560035/theoretical"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1601674079,"post_id":64178448,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":1,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>From <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/38987/how-do-i-merge-two-dictionaries-in-a-single-expression-in-python-taking-union-o\">Aaron Hall's answer</a>:</p>\n<pre><code>from copy import deepcopy\n\ndef dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y):\n    z = {}\n    overlapping_keys = x.keys() &amp; y.keys()\n    for key in overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = dict_of_dicts_merge(x[key], y[key])\n    for key in x.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(x[key])\n    for key in y.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(y[key])\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n","revision_guid":"ACAFA55C-D783-4BD7-B021-F7CEBB87F9B8"},{"user":{"account_id":10438857,"reputation":13785,"user_id":7696223,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-m7rmWbqxL8s/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAo/GsqJGi8qLdI/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Scotty Jamison","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7696223/scotty-jamison"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1601487341,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":6,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise ES6 approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams's</a> toString() function will convert the query args into a string that can be appended onto the URL. In this example, toString() is called implicitly when it gets concatenated with the URL. You will likely want to call toString() explicitly to improve readability.</p>\n<p>IE does not support URLSearchParams (or fetch), but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> available.</p>\n<p>If using node, you can add the fetch API through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>. URLSearchParams comes with node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code>.</p>\n","comment":"added 11 characters in body","revision_guid":"DF0AFE65-38D1-4BD3-B82E-07AF066865CF"},{"user":{"account_id":10438857,"reputation":13785,"user_id":7696223,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-m7rmWbqxL8s/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAo/GsqJGi8qLdI/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Scotty Jamison","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7696223/scotty-jamison"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1598906539,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":5,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise ES6 approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams's</a> toString() function will convert the query args into a string that can be appended onto the URL. In this example, toString() is called implicitly when it gets concatenated with the URL. You will likely want to call toString() explicitly to improve readability.</p>\n<p>IE does not support URLSearchParams, but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> available.</p>\n<p>If using node, you can add the fetch API through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>. URLSearchParams comes with node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code>.</p>\n","comment":"added 75 characters in body","revision_guid":"8F7BC939-C918-4EC8-945D-A15EA1A275FE"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1598659706,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":38,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h2>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h2>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For dictionaries <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>, <code>z</code> becomes a shallowly merged dictionary with values from <code>y</code> replacing those from <code>x</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In Python 3.5 or greater:\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li>In Python 2, (or 3.4 or lower) write a function:\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    z = x.copy()   # start with x's keys and values\n    z.update(y)    # modifies z with y's keys and values &amp; returns None\n    return z\n</code></pre>\nand now:\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li>In Python 3.9.0a4 or greater (final release date approx October 2020): <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0584/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP-584</a>, <a href=\"https://bugs.python.org/issue36144\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">discussed here</a>, was implemented to further simplify this:\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = x | y          # NOTE: 3.9+ ONLY\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Explanation</h3>\n<p>Say you have two dictionaries and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dictionaries:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The desired result is to get a new dictionary (<code>z</code>) with the values merged, and the second dictionary's values overwriting those from the first.</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A new syntax for this, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a> and <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available as of Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression.</p>\n<p>Note that we can merge in with literal notation as well:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = {**x, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, you may wish to do this in a backwards compatible way. The classically Pythonic way, available in Python 2 and Python 3.0-3.4, is to do this as a two-step process:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>In both approaches, <code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<h2>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a <em>single expression</em></h2>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5, or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a <em>single expression</em>, the most performant while correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Given two dictionaries, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dictionaries, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    Given any number of dictionaries, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dictionaries.\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dictionaries. e.g. given dictionaries <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dictionaries <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h2>Critiques of Other Answers</h2>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the formerly accepted answer:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example). Even if your values are hashable, <strong>since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic.</p>\n<p>Here's an example of the usage being <a href=\"https://code.djangoproject.com/attachment/ticket/13357/django-pypy.2.diff\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">remediated in django</a>.</p>\n<p>Dictionaries are intended to take hashable keys (e.g. frozensets or tuples), but <strong>this method fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is my understanding (as well as the understanding of the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099485.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">creator of the language</a>) that the intended usage for <code>dict(**y)</code> is for creating dictionaries for readability purposes, e.g.:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>dict(a=1, b=10, c=11)\n</code></pre>\n<p>instead of</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>{'a': 1, 'b': 10, 'c': 11}\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Response to comments</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Despite what Guido says, <code>dict(x, **y)</code> is in line with the dict specification, which btw. works for both Python 2 and 3. The fact that this only works for string keys is a direct consequence of how keyword parameters work and not a short-comming of dict. Nor is using the ** operator in this place an abuse of the mechanism, in fact ** was designed precisely to pass dictionaries as keywords.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Again, it doesn't work for 3 when keys are non-strings. The implicit calling contract is that namespaces take ordinary dictionaries, while users must only pass keyword arguments that are strings. All other callables enforced it. <code>dict</code> broke this consistency in Python 2:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; foo(**{('a', 'b'): None})\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: foo() keywords must be strings\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(**{('a', 'b'): None})\n{('a', 'b'): None}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This inconsistency was bad given other implementations of Python (Pypy, Jython, IronPython). Thus it was fixed in Python 3, as this usage could be a breaking change.</p>\n<p>I submit to you that it is malicious incompetence to intentionally write code that only works in one version of a language or that only works given certain arbitrary constraints.</p>\n<p>More comments:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>dict(x.items() + y.items())</code> is still the most readable solution for Python 2. Readability counts.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>My response: <code>merge_two_dicts(x, y)</code> actually seems much clearer to me, if we're actually concerned about readability. And it is not forward compatible, as Python 2 is increasingly deprecated.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>{**x, **y}</code> does not seem to handle nested dictionaries. the contents of nested keys are simply overwritten, not merged [...] I ended up being burnt by these answers that do not merge recursively and I was surprised no one mentioned it. In my interpretation of the word &quot;merging&quot; these answers describe &quot;updating one dict with another&quot;, and not merging.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes. I must refer you back to the question, which is asking for a <em>shallow</em> merge of <em><strong>two</strong></em> dictionaries, with the first's values being overwritten by the second's - in a single expression.</p>\n<p>Assuming two dictionary of dictionaries, one might recursively merge them in a single function, but you should be careful not to modify the dictionaries from either source, and the surest way to avoid that is to make a copy when assigning values. As keys must be hashable and are usually therefore immutable, it is pointless to copy them:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>from copy import deepcopy\n\ndef dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y):\n    z = {}\n    overlapping_keys = x.keys() &amp; y.keys()\n    for key in overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = dict_of_dicts_merge(x[key], y[key])\n    for key in x.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(x[key])\n    for key in y.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(y[key])\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>Usage:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a':{1:{}}, 'b': {2:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b':{10:{}}, 'c': {11:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y)\n{'b': {2: {}, 10: {}}, 'a': {1: {}}, 'c': {11: {}}}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Coming up with contingencies for other value types is far beyond the scope of this question, so I will point you at <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/a/24088493/541136\">my answer to the canonical question on a &quot;Dictionaries of dictionaries merge&quot;</a>.</p>\n<h2>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h2>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dictionaries have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dictionaries manually inside a <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0274/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">dict comprehension</a>:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()) # iteritems in Python 2\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>from itertools import chain\nz = dict(chain(x.items(), y.items())) # iteritems in Python 2\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Performance Analysis</h2>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly. (Self-contained so you can copy and paste yourself.)</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>from timeit import repeat\nfrom itertools import chain\n\nx = dict.fromkeys('abcdefg')\ny = dict.fromkeys('efghijk')\n\ndef merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n\nmin(repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\nmin(repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\nmin(repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()}))\nmin(repeat(lambda: dict(chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\nmin(repeat(lambda: dict(item for d in (x, y) for item in d.items())))\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.8.1, NixOS:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n1.0804965235292912\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n1.636518670246005\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()}))\n3.1779992282390594\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: dict(chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.740647904574871\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(repeat(lambda: dict(item for d in (x, y) for item in d.items())))\n4.266070580109954\n</code></pre>\n<pre class=\"lang-shell prettyprint-override\"><code>$ uname -a\nLinux nixos 4.19.113 #1-NixOS SMP Wed Mar 25 07:06:15 UTC 2020 x86_64 GNU/Linux\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Resources on Dictionaries</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/327311/how-are-pythons-built-in-dictionaries-implemented/44509302#44509302\">My explanation of Python's <strong>dictionary implementation</strong>, updated for 3.6.</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1024847/add-new-keys-to-a-dictionary/27208535#27208535\">Answer on how to add new keys to a dictionary</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/209840/map-two-lists-into-a-dictionary-in-python/33737067#33737067\">Mapping two lists into a dictionary</a></li>\n<li>The official Python <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#dictionaries\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">docs on dictionaries</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=66P5FMkWoVU\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The Dictionary Even Mightier</a> - talk by Brandon Rhodes at Pycon 2017</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=npw4s1QTmPg\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Modern Python Dictionaries, A Confluence of Great Ideas</a> - talk by Raymond Hettinger at Pycon 2017</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"format with fences, improve copy-pasteability, update a bit of Python 2 specific code, remove Python 2 specific performance analysis, update perf analysis for 3.8","revision_guid":"DC0A34D7-6255-475F-94CA-A7EC2610650F"},{"user":{"account_id":10438857,"reputation":13785,"user_id":7696223,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-m7rmWbqxL8s/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAo/GsqJGi8qLdI/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Scotty Jamison","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7696223/scotty-jamison"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1598287506,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":4,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise ES6 approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams's</a> toString() function will convert the query args into a string that can be appended onto the URL. In this example, toString() is called implicitly when it gets concatenated with the URL.</p>\n<p>IE does not support URLSearchParams, but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> available.</p>\n<p>If using node, you can add the fetch API through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>. URLSearchParams comes with node, and can be found as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code>.</p>\n","comment":"deleted 26 characters in body","revision_guid":"9DDC3A72-1A7F-4F2C-B489-35DA78BCD289"},{"user":{"account_id":18002354,"reputation":28,"user_id":13084011,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/e8a856e384096cd0d44f1fbe73a561a3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"vladsuper5555","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/13084011/vladsuper5555"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1598180379,"post_id":63546158,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":1,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>You can solve for each case and then merge</p>\n<pre><code>from copy import deepcopy\n\ndef dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y):\n    z = {}\n    overlapping_keys = x.keys() &amp; y.keys()\n    for key in overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = dict_of_dicts_merge(x[key], y[key])\n    for key in x.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(x[key])\n    for key in y.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(y[key])\n    return z\n\nkey1 = &quot;A.B.C.D&quot;\ntext_to_be_inserted_for_key1 = &quot;Test1&quot;\nkey2 = &quot;A.B.C.E&quot;\ntext_to_be_inserted_for_key2 = &quot;Test2&quot;\n\ndict1 = {}\nnewdict = {}\nolddict = {}\nkeys_for_1 = key1.split(&quot;.&quot;)\nkeys_for_1.reverse()\n\n\nolddict[keys_for_1[0]] = text_to_be_inserted_for_key1\n\nfor i in range (1,len(keys_for_1)):\n    newdict = {}\n    newdict[keys_for_1[i]] = olddict\n    olddict = newdict\n\nsave1 = newdict \n\nnewdict = {}\nolddict = {}\nkeys_for_2 = key2.split(&quot;.&quot;)\nkeys_for_2.reverse()\nolddict[keys_for_2[0]] = text_to_be_inserted_for_key2\n\nfor i in range (1,len(keys_for_2)):\n    newdict = {}\n    newdict[keys_for_2[i]] = olddict\n    olddict = newdict \n\nsave2 = newdict\n\ndict1 = dict_of_dicts_merge(save1,save2)\nprint (dict1)\n</code></pre>\n","revision_guid":"6AE538FD-7530-480D-85CF-E560D99DF9E1"},{"user":{"account_id":10438857,"reputation":13785,"user_id":7696223,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-m7rmWbqxL8s/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAo/GsqJGi8qLdI/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Scotty Jamison","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7696223/scotty-jamison"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1597091322,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":3,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise ES6 approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams's</a> toString() function will convert the query args into a string that can be appended onto the URL. In this example, toString() is called implicitly when it gets concatenated with the URL.</p>\n<p>IE does not support URLSearchParams, but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> available.</p>\n<p>If using node, you can find URLSearchParams as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code> instead. The actual fetch API is not part of node and needs to be added through a package like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>.</p>\n","comment":"deleted 1 character in body","revision_guid":"3923F937-B944-4A9B-9427-3D4C1EB07262"},{"user":{"account_id":10438857,"reputation":13785,"user_id":7696223,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-m7rmWbqxL8s/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAo/GsqJGi8qLdI/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Scotty Jamison","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7696223/scotty-jamison"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1596036777,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":2,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise ES6 approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParams's</a> toString() function will convert the query args into a string that can be appended onto the URL. In this example, toString() is called implicitly when it gets concatenated with the URL.</p>\n<p>IE does not support URLSearchParams, but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> available.</p>\n<p>If using node, you can find URLSearchParams as a global object since version 10. In older version you can find it at <code>require('url').URLSearchParams</code> instead. The actual fetch API is not part of node and needs to be added through a package, like <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/node-fetch\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">node-fetch</a>.</p>\n","comment":"Explains how node supports this feature.","revision_guid":"4E9308B4-486C-416F-97A2-7F6A6B4A0662"},{"user":{"account_id":18596616,"reputation":27754,"user_id":13552470,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/oT1YI5NA.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Harvard Candidate","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/13552470/harvard-candidate"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1592883001,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":37,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h2>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h2>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For dictionaries <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>, <code>z</code> becomes a shallowly merged dictionary with values from <code>y</code> replacing those from <code>x</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>In Python 3.5 or greater:</p>\n<pre><code>  z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>In Python 2, (or 3.4 or lower) write a function:</p>\n<pre><code>  def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n      z = x.copy()   # start with x's keys and values\n      z.update(y)    # modifies z with y's keys and values &amp; returns None\n      return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre><code>  z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>In Python 3.9.0a4 or greater (final release date approx October 2020): <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0584/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP-584</a>, <a href=\"https://bugs.python.org/issue36144\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">discussed here</a>, was implemented to further simplify this:</p>\n<pre><code>  z = x | y          # NOTE: 3.9+ ONLY\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Explanation</h3>\n<p>Say you have two dictionaries and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dictionaries:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The desired result is to get a new dictionary (<code>z</code>) with the values merged, and the second dictionary's values overwriting those from the first.</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A new syntax for this, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a> and <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available as of Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression.</p>\n<p>Note that we can merge in with literal notation as well:</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, you may wish to do this in a backwards compatible way. The classically Pythonic way, available in Python 2 and Python 3.0-3.4, is to do this as a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>In both approaches, <code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<h2>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a <em>single expression</em></h2>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5, or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a <em>single expression</em>, the most performant while correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Given two dictionaries, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dictionaries, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    Given any number of dictionaries, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dictionaries.\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dictionaries. e.g. given dictionaries <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dictionaries <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h2>Critiques of Other Answers</h2>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the formerly accepted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example). Even if your values are hashable, <strong>since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic.</p>\n<p>Here's an example of the usage being <a href=\"https://code.djangoproject.com/attachment/ticket/13357/django-pypy.2.diff\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">remediated in django</a>.</p>\n<p>Dictionaries are intended to take hashable keys (e.g. frozensets or tuples), but <strong>this method fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is my understanding (as well as the understanding of the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099485.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">creator of the language</a>) that the intended usage for <code>dict(**y)</code> is for creating dictionaries for readability purposes, e.g.:</p>\n<pre><code>dict(a=1, b=10, c=11)\n</code></pre>\n<p>instead of</p>\n<pre><code>{'a': 1, 'b': 10, 'c': 11}\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Response to comments</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Despite what Guido says, <code>dict(x, **y)</code> is in line with the dict specification, which btw. works for both Python 2 and 3. The fact that this only works for string keys is a direct consequence of how keyword parameters work and not a short-comming of dict. Nor is using the ** operator in this place an abuse of the mechanism, in fact ** was designed precisely to pass dictionaries as keywords.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Again, it doesn't work for 3 when keys are non-strings. The implicit calling contract is that namespaces take ordinary dictionaries, while users must only pass keyword arguments that are strings. All other callables enforced it. <code>dict</code> broke this consistency in Python 2:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; foo(**{('a', 'b'): None})\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: foo() keywords must be strings\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(**{('a', 'b'): None})\n{('a', 'b'): None}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This inconsistency was bad given other implementations of Python (Pypy, Jython, IronPython). Thus it was fixed in Python 3, as this usage could be a breaking change.</p>\n<p>I submit to you that it is malicious incompetence to intentionally write code that only works in one version of a language or that only works given certain arbitrary constraints.</p>\n<p>More comments:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>dict(x.items() + y.items())</code> is still the most readable solution for Python 2. Readability counts.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>My response: <code>merge_two_dicts(x, y)</code> actually seems much clearer to me, if we're actually concerned about readability. And it is not forward compatible, as Python 2 is increasingly deprecated.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>{**x, **y}</code> does not seem to handle nested dictionaries. the contents of nested keys are simply overwritten, not merged [...] I ended up being burnt by these answers that do not merge recursively and I was surprised no one mentioned it. In my interpretation of the word &quot;merging&quot; these answers describe &quot;updating one dict with another&quot;, and not merging.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes. I must refer you back to the question, which is asking for a <em>shallow</em> merge of <em><strong>two</strong></em> dictionaries, with the first's values being overwritten by the second's - in a single expression.</p>\n<p>Assuming two dictionary of dictionaries, one might recursively merge them in a single function, but you should be careful not to modify the dictionaries from either source, and the surest way to avoid that is to make a copy when assigning values. As keys must be hashable and are usually therefore immutable, it is pointless to copy them:</p>\n<pre><code>from copy import deepcopy\n\ndef dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y):\n    z = {}\n    overlapping_keys = x.keys() &amp; y.keys()\n    for key in overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = dict_of_dicts_merge(x[key], y[key])\n    for key in x.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(x[key])\n    for key in y.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(y[key])\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>Usage:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a':{1:{}}, 'b': {2:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b':{10:{}}, 'c': {11:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y)\n{'b': {2: {}, 10: {}}, 'a': {1: {}}, 'c': {11: {}}}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Coming up with contingencies for other value types is far beyond the scope of this question, so I will point you at <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/a/24088493/541136\">my answer to the canonical question on a &quot;Dictionaries of dictionaries merge&quot;</a>.</p>\n<h2>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h2>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dictionaries have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dictionaries manually inside a <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0274/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">dict comprehension</a>:</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre><code>import itertools\nz = dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Performance Analysis</h2>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly.</p>\n<pre><code>import timeit\n</code></pre>\n<p>The following is done on Ubuntu 14.04</p>\n<p>In Python 2.7 (system Python):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.5726828575134277\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.163769006729126\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))))\n1.1614501476287842\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n2.2345519065856934\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.5 (deadsnakes PPA):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n0.4094954460160807\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.7881555100320838\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.4525277839857154\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.3143140770262107\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n3.2069112799945287\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Resources on Dictionaries</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/327311/how-are-pythons-built-in-dictionaries-implemented/44509302#44509302\">My explanation of Python's <strong>dictionary implementation</strong>, updated for 3.6.</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1024847/add-new-keys-to-a-dictionary/27208535#27208535\">Answer on how to add new keys to a dictionary</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/209840/map-two-lists-into-a-dictionary-in-python/33737067#33737067\">Mapping two lists into a dictionary</a></li>\n<li>The official Python <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#dictionaries\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">docs on dictionaries</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=66P5FMkWoVU\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The Dictionary Even Mightier</a> - talk by Brandon Rhodes at Pycon 2017</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=npw4s1QTmPg\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Modern Python Dictionaries, A Confluence of Great Ideas</a> - talk by Raymond Hettinger at Pycon 2017</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"Added dict comprehension link, and changed some dict to dictionary","revision_guid":"154AFC39-5AFE-4A9E-AC0F-046B3882CC55"},{"user":{"account_id":66296,"reputation":27035,"user_id":194586,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/1a71658d81f8a82a8122050f21bb86d3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Nick T","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/194586/nick-t"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1583335489,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":36,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h2>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h2>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For dictionaries <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>, <code>z</code> becomes a shallowly merged dictionary with values from <code>y</code> replacing those from <code>x</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>In Python 3.5 or greater:</p>\n<pre><code>  z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>In Python 2, (or 3.4 or lower) write a function:</p>\n<pre><code>  def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n      z = x.copy()   # start with x's keys and values\n      z.update(y)    # modifies z with y's keys and values &amp; returns None\n      return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre><code>  z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>In Python 3.9.0a4 or greater (final release date approx October 2020): <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0584/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP-584</a>, <a href=\"https://bugs.python.org/issue36144\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">discussed here</a>, was implemented to further simplify this:</p>\n<pre><code>  z = x | y          # NOTE: 3.9+ ONLY\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Explanation</h3>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The desired result is to get a new dictionary (<code>z</code>) with the values merged, and the second dict's values overwriting those from the first.</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A new syntax for this, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a> and <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available as of Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression.</p>\n<p>Note that we can merge in with literal notation as well:</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, you may wish to do this in a backwards compatible way. The classically Pythonic way, available in Python 2 and Python 3.0-3.4, is to do this as a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>In both approaches, <code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<h2>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a <em>single expression</em></h2>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5, or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a <em>single expression</em>, the most performant while correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h2>Critiques of Other Answers</h2>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the formerly accepted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example). Even if your values are hashable, <strong>since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic.</p>\n<p>Here's an example of the usage being <a href=\"https://code.djangoproject.com/attachment/ticket/13357/django-pypy.2.diff\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">remediated in django</a>.</p>\n<p>Dicts are intended to take hashable keys (e.g. frozensets or tuples), but <strong>this method fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is my understanding (as well as the understanding of the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099485.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">creator of the language</a>) that the intended usage for <code>dict(**y)</code> is for creating dicts for readability purposes, e.g.:</p>\n<pre><code>dict(a=1, b=10, c=11)\n</code></pre>\n<p>instead of</p>\n<pre><code>{'a': 1, 'b': 10, 'c': 11}\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Response to comments</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Despite what Guido says, <code>dict(x, **y)</code> is in line with the dict specification, which btw. works for both Python 2 and 3. The fact that this only works for string keys is a direct consequence of how keyword parameters work and not a short-comming of dict. Nor is using the ** operator in this place an abuse of the mechanism, in fact ** was designed precisely to pass dicts as keywords.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Again, it doesn't work for 3 when keys are non-strings. The implicit calling contract is that namespaces take ordinary dicts, while users must only pass keyword arguments that are strings. All other callables enforced it. <code>dict</code> broke this consistency in Python 2:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; foo(**{('a', 'b'): None})\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: foo() keywords must be strings\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(**{('a', 'b'): None})\n{('a', 'b'): None}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This inconsistency was bad given other implementations of Python (Pypy, Jython, IronPython). Thus it was fixed in Python 3, as this usage could be a breaking change.</p>\n<p>I submit to you that it is malicious incompetence to intentionally write code that only works in one version of a language or that only works given certain arbitrary constraints.</p>\n<p>More comments:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>dict(x.items() + y.items())</code> is still the most readable solution for Python 2. Readability counts.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>My response: <code>merge_two_dicts(x, y)</code> actually seems much clearer to me, if we're actually concerned about readability. And it is not forward compatible, as Python 2 is increasingly deprecated.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>{**x, **y}</code> does not seem to handle nested dictionaries. the contents of nested keys are simply overwritten, not merged [...] I ended up being burnt by these answers that do not merge recursively and I was surprised no one mentioned it. In my interpretation of the word &quot;merging&quot; these answers describe &quot;updating one dict with another&quot;, and not merging.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes. I must refer you back to the question, which is asking for a <em>shallow</em> merge of <em><strong>two</strong></em> dictionaries, with the first's values being overwritten by the second's - in a single expression.</p>\n<p>Assuming two dictionary of dictionaries, one might recursively merge them in a single function, but you should be careful not to modify the dicts from either source, and the surest way to avoid that is to make a copy when assigning values. As keys must be hashable and are usually therefore immutable, it is pointless to copy them:</p>\n<pre><code>from copy import deepcopy\n\ndef dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y):\n    z = {}\n    overlapping_keys = x.keys() &amp; y.keys()\n    for key in overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = dict_of_dicts_merge(x[key], y[key])\n    for key in x.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(x[key])\n    for key in y.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(y[key])\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>Usage:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a':{1:{}}, 'b': {2:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b':{10:{}}, 'c': {11:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y)\n{'b': {2: {}, 10: {}}, 'a': {1: {}}, 'c': {11: {}}}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Coming up with contingencies for other value types is far beyond the scope of this question, so I will point you at <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/a/24088493/541136\">my answer to the canonical question on a &quot;Dictionaries of dictionaries merge&quot;</a>.</p>\n<h2>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h2>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dicts manually inside a dict comprehension:</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre><code>import itertools\nz = dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Performance Analysis</h2>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly.</p>\n<pre><code>import timeit\n</code></pre>\n<p>The following is done on Ubuntu 14.04</p>\n<p>In Python 2.7 (system Python):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.5726828575134277\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.163769006729126\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))))\n1.1614501476287842\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n2.2345519065856934\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.5 (deadsnakes PPA):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n0.4094954460160807\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.7881555100320838\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.4525277839857154\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.3143140770262107\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n3.2069112799945287\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Resources on Dictionaries</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/327311/how-are-pythons-built-in-dictionaries-implemented/44509302#44509302\">My explanation of Python's <strong>dictionary implementation</strong>, updated for 3.6.</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1024847/add-new-keys-to-a-dictionary/27208535#27208535\">Answer on how to add new keys to a dictionary</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/209840/map-two-lists-into-a-dictionary-in-python/33737067#33737067\">Mapping two lists into a dictionary</a></li>\n<li>The official Python <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#dictionaries\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">docs on dictionaries</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=66P5FMkWoVU\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The Dictionary Even Mightier</a> - talk by Brandon Rhodes at Pycon 2017</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=npw4s1QTmPg\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Modern Python Dictionaries, A Confluence of Great Ideas</a> - talk by Raymond Hettinger at Pycon 2017</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"PEP-584 implemented circa 3.9.0a4","revision_guid":"B7DB4584-EFFE-40A5-84D6-52BF7E1D6D1B"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1582084945,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":35,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h2>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h2>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For dictionaries <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>, <code>z</code> becomes a shallowly merged dictionary with values from <code>y</code> replacing those from <code>x</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>In Python 3.5 or greater:</p>\n<pre><code>  z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>In Python 2, (or 3.4 or lower) write a function:</p>\n<pre><code>  def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n      z = x.copy()   # start with x's keys and values\n      z.update(y)    # modifies z with y's keys and values &amp; returns None\n      return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre><code>  z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note there is a <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0584/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">proposal (PEP 584)</a>, <a href=\"https://bugs.python.org/issue36144\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">discussed here</a>, to further simplify this in a future version of Python by giving <code>dict</code> a merging operator, expected to be <code>|</code>, which would allow for:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-python prettyprint-override\"><code>z = x | y        # &lt;- But this is not yet implemented.\n</code></pre>\n<h3>Explanation</h3>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The desired result is to get a new dictionary (<code>z</code>) with the values merged, and the second dict's values overwriting those from the first.</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A new syntax for this, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a> and <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available as of Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression.</p>\n<p>Note that we can merge in with literal notation as well:</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, you may wish to do this in a backwards compatible way. The classically Pythonic way, available in Python 2 and Python 3.0-3.4, is to do this as a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>In both approaches, <code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<h2>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a <em>single expression</em></h2>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5, or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a <em>single expression</em>, the most performant while correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h2>Critiques of Other Answers</h2>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the formerly accepted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example). Even if your values are hashable, <strong>since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic.</p>\n<p>Here's an example of the usage being <a href=\"https://code.djangoproject.com/attachment/ticket/13357/django-pypy.2.diff\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">remediated in django</a>.</p>\n<p>Dicts are intended to take hashable keys (e.g. frozensets or tuples), but <strong>this method fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is my understanding (as well as the understanding of the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099485.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">creator of the language</a>) that the intended usage for <code>dict(**y)</code> is for creating dicts for readability purposes, e.g.:</p>\n<pre><code>dict(a=1, b=10, c=11)\n</code></pre>\n<p>instead of</p>\n<pre><code>{'a': 1, 'b': 10, 'c': 11}\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Response to comments</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Despite what Guido says, <code>dict(x, **y)</code> is in line with the dict specification, which btw. works for both Python 2 and 3. The fact that this only works for string keys is a direct consequence of how keyword parameters work and not a short-comming of dict. Nor is using the ** operator in this place an abuse of the mechanism, in fact ** was designed precisely to pass dicts as keywords.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Again, it doesn't work for 3 when keys are non-strings. The implicit calling contract is that namespaces take ordinary dicts, while users must only pass keyword arguments that are strings. All other callables enforced it. <code>dict</code> broke this consistency in Python 2:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; foo(**{('a', 'b'): None})\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: foo() keywords must be strings\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(**{('a', 'b'): None})\n{('a', 'b'): None}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This inconsistency was bad given other implementations of Python (Pypy, Jython, IronPython). Thus it was fixed in Python 3, as this usage could be a breaking change.</p>\n<p>I submit to you that it is malicious incompetence to intentionally write code that only works in one version of a language or that only works given certain arbitrary constraints.</p>\n<p>More comments:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>dict(x.items() + y.items())</code> is still the most readable solution for Python 2. Readability counts.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>My response: <code>merge_two_dicts(x, y)</code> actually seems much clearer to me, if we're actually concerned about readability. And it is not forward compatible, as Python 2 is increasingly deprecated.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>{**x, **y}</code> does not seem to handle nested dictionaries. the contents of nested keys are simply overwritten, not merged [...] I ended up being burnt by these answers that do not merge recursively and I was surprised no one mentioned it. In my interpretation of the word &quot;merging&quot; these answers describe &quot;updating one dict with another&quot;, and not merging.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes. I must refer you back to the question, which is asking for a <em>shallow</em> merge of <em><strong>two</strong></em> dictionaries, with the first's values being overwritten by the second's - in a single expression.</p>\n<p>Assuming two dictionary of dictionaries, one might recursively merge them in a single function, but you should be careful not to modify the dicts from either source, and the surest way to avoid that is to make a copy when assigning values. As keys must be hashable and are usually therefore immutable, it is pointless to copy them:</p>\n<pre><code>from copy import deepcopy\n\ndef dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y):\n    z = {}\n    overlapping_keys = x.keys() &amp; y.keys()\n    for key in overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = dict_of_dicts_merge(x[key], y[key])\n    for key in x.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(x[key])\n    for key in y.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(y[key])\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>Usage:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a':{1:{}}, 'b': {2:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b':{10:{}}, 'c': {11:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y)\n{'b': {2: {}, 10: {}}, 'a': {1: {}}, 'c': {11: {}}}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Coming up with contingencies for other value types is far beyond the scope of this question, so I will point you at <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/a/24088493/541136\">my answer to the canonical question on a &quot;Dictionaries of dictionaries merge&quot;</a>.</p>\n<h2>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h2>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dicts manually inside a dict comprehension:</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre><code>import itertools\nz = dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Performance Analysis</h2>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly.</p>\n<pre><code>import timeit\n</code></pre>\n<p>The following is done on Ubuntu 14.04</p>\n<p>In Python 2.7 (system Python):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.5726828575134277\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.163769006729126\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))))\n1.1614501476287842\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n2.2345519065856934\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.5 (deadsnakes PPA):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n0.4094954460160807\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.7881555100320838\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.4525277839857154\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.3143140770262107\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n3.2069112799945287\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Resources on Dictionaries</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/327311/how-are-pythons-built-in-dictionaries-implemented/44509302#44509302\">My explanation of Python's <strong>dictionary implementation</strong>, updated for 3.6.</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1024847/add-new-keys-to-a-dictionary/27208535#27208535\">Answer on how to add new keys to a dictionary</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/209840/map-two-lists-into-a-dictionary-in-python/33737067#33737067\">Mapping two lists into a dictionary</a></li>\n<li>The official Python <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#dictionaries\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">docs on dictionaries</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=66P5FMkWoVU\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The Dictionary Even Mightier</a> - talk by Brandon Rhodes at Pycon 2017</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=npw4s1QTmPg\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Modern Python Dictionaries, A Confluence of Great Ideas</a> - talk by Raymond Hettinger at Pycon 2017</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"update for update in PEP","revision_guid":"B4F7C033-D71B-4155-A025-4330796E9B28"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1577591189,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":34,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h2>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h2>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For dictionaries <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>, <code>z</code> becomes a shallowly merged dictionary with values from <code>y</code> replacing those from <code>x</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>In Python 3.5 or greater:</p>\n<pre><code>  z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>In Python 2, (or 3.4 or lower) write a function:</p>\n<pre><code>  def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n      z = x.copy()   # start with x's keys and values\n      z.update(y)    # modifies z with y's keys and values &amp; returns None\n      return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre><code>  z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Note there is a <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0584/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">proposal (PEP 584)</a>, <a href=\"https://bugs.python.org/issue36144\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">discussed here</a>, to further simplify this in a future version of Python by giving <code>dict</code> a merging operator, expected to be <code>+</code>, which would allow for:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x + y                       # pseudocode for now...\n</code></pre>\n<p>But this is not yet implemented.</p>\n<h3>Explanation</h3>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The desired result is to get a new dictionary (<code>z</code>) with the values merged, and the second dict's values overwriting those from the first.</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A new syntax for this, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a> and <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available as of Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression.</p>\n<p>Note that we can merge in with literal notation as well:</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, you may wish to do this in a backwards compatible way. The classically Pythonic way, available in Python 2 and Python 3.0-3.4, is to do this as a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>In both approaches, <code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<h2>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a <em>single expression</em></h2>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5, or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a <em>single expression</em>, the most performant while correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h2>Critiques of Other Answers</h2>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the formerly accepted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example). Even if your values are hashable, <strong>since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic.</p>\n<p>Here's an example of the usage being <a href=\"https://code.djangoproject.com/attachment/ticket/13357/django-pypy.2.diff\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">remediated in django</a>.</p>\n<p>Dicts are intended to take hashable keys (e.g. frozensets or tuples), but <strong>this method fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is my understanding (as well as the understanding of the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099485.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">creator of the language</a>) that the intended usage for <code>dict(**y)</code> is for creating dicts for readability purposes, e.g.:</p>\n<pre><code>dict(a=1, b=10, c=11)\n</code></pre>\n<p>instead of</p>\n<pre><code>{'a': 1, 'b': 10, 'c': 11}\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Response to comments</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Despite what Guido says, <code>dict(x, **y)</code> is in line with the dict specification, which btw. works for both Python 2 and 3. The fact that this only works for string keys is a direct consequence of how keyword parameters work and not a short-comming of dict. Nor is using the ** operator in this place an abuse of the mechanism, in fact ** was designed precisely to pass dicts as keywords.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Again, it doesn't work for 3 when keys are non-strings. The implicit calling contract is that namespaces take ordinary dicts, while users must only pass keyword arguments that are strings. All other callables enforced it. <code>dict</code> broke this consistency in Python 2:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; foo(**{('a', 'b'): None})\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: foo() keywords must be strings\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(**{('a', 'b'): None})\n{('a', 'b'): None}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This inconsistency was bad given other implementations of Python (Pypy, Jython, IronPython). Thus it was fixed in Python 3, as this usage could be a breaking change.</p>\n<p>I submit to you that it is malicious incompetence to intentionally write code that only works in one version of a language or that only works given certain arbitrary constraints.</p>\n<p>More comments:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>dict(x.items() + y.items())</code> is still the most readable solution for Python 2. Readability counts.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>My response: <code>merge_two_dicts(x, y)</code> actually seems much clearer to me, if we're actually concerned about readability. And it is not forward compatible, as Python 2 is increasingly deprecated.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>{**x, **y}</code> does not seem to handle nested dictionaries. the contents of nested keys are simply overwritten, not merged [...] I ended up being burnt by these answers that do not merge recursively and I was surprised no one mentioned it. In my interpretation of the word &quot;merging&quot; these answers describe &quot;updating one dict with another&quot;, and not merging.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes. I must refer you back to the question, which is asking for a <em>shallow</em> merge of <em><strong>two</strong></em> dictionaries, with the first's values being overwritten by the second's - in a single expression.</p>\n<p>Assuming two dictionary of dictionaries, one might recursively merge them in a single function, but you should be careful not to modify the dicts from either source, and the surest way to avoid that is to make a copy when assigning values. As keys must be hashable and are usually therefore immutable, it is pointless to copy them:</p>\n<pre><code>from copy import deepcopy\n\ndef dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y):\n    z = {}\n    overlapping_keys = x.keys() &amp; y.keys()\n    for key in overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = dict_of_dicts_merge(x[key], y[key])\n    for key in x.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(x[key])\n    for key in y.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(y[key])\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>Usage:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a':{1:{}}, 'b': {2:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b':{10:{}}, 'c': {11:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y)\n{'b': {2: {}, 10: {}}, 'a': {1: {}}, 'c': {11: {}}}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Coming up with contingencies for other value types is far beyond the scope of this question, so I will point you at <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/a/24088493/541136\">my answer to the canonical question on a &quot;Dictionaries of dictionaries merge&quot;</a>.</p>\n<h2>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h2>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dicts manually inside a dict comprehension:</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre><code>import itertools\nz = dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Performance Analysis</h2>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly.</p>\n<pre><code>import timeit\n</code></pre>\n<p>The following is done on Ubuntu 14.04</p>\n<p>In Python 2.7 (system Python):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.5726828575134277\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.163769006729126\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))))\n1.1614501476287842\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n2.2345519065856934\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.5 (deadsnakes PPA):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n0.4094954460160807\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.7881555100320838\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.4525277839857154\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.3143140770262107\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n3.2069112799945287\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Resources on Dictionaries</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/327311/how-are-pythons-built-in-dictionaries-implemented/44509302#44509302\">My explanation of Python's <strong>dictionary implementation</strong>, updated for 3.6.</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1024847/add-new-keys-to-a-dictionary/27208535#27208535\">Answer on how to add new keys to a dictionary</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/209840/map-two-lists-into-a-dictionary-in-python/33737067#33737067\">Mapping two lists into a dictionary</a></li>\n<li>The official Python <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#dictionaries\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">docs on dictionaries</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=66P5FMkWoVU\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The Dictionary Even Mightier</a> - talk by Brandon Rhodes at Pycon 2017</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=npw4s1QTmPg\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Modern Python Dictionaries, A Confluence of Great Ideas</a> - talk by Raymond Hettinger at Pycon 2017</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"foreshadowing z = x + y","revision_guid":"59C8F31B-AF4C-44AF-8E20-DABB95176E09"},{"user":{"account_id":10438857,"reputation":13785,"user_id":7696223,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh4.googleusercontent.com/-m7rmWbqxL8s/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAo/GsqJGi8qLdI/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Scotty Jamison","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/7696223/scotty-jamison"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1571333430,"post_id":58437909,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":1,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>A concise ES6 approach:</p>\n<pre><code>fetch('https://example.com?' + new URLSearchParams({\n    foo: 'value',\n    bar: 2,\n}))\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/URLSearchParams/toString\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">URLSearchParam's</a> toString() function will convert the query args into a string that can be appended onto the URL. In this example, toString() is called implicitly when it gets concatenated with the URL.</p>\n<p>IE does not support this feature, but there are <a href=\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/url-search-params-polyfill\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">polyfills</a> available.</p>\n","revision_guid":"BFE00A1B-37A5-41A4-BD34-2CF9E3017416"},{"user":{"account_id":6321039,"reputation":407685,"user_id":4909087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Zcszo.png?s=256","display_name":"coldspeed95","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/4909087/coldspeed95"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1557380653,"post_id":53954986,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":13,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<p>###How to deal with <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> in Pandas?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This post is meant for readers who,</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Would like to understand what this warning means</li>\n<li>Would like to understand different ways of suppressing this warning</li>\n<li>Would like to understand how to improve their code and follow good practices to avoid this warning in the future.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Setup</strong></p>\n<pre><code>np.random.seed(0)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(np.random.choice(10, (3, 5)), columns=list('ABCDE'))\ndf\n   A  B  C  D  E\n0  5  0  3  3  7\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#What is the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>?</p>\n<p>To know how to deal with this warning, it is important to understand what it means and why it is raised in the first place.</p>\n<p>When filtering DataFrames, it is possible slice/index a frame to return either a <em>view</em>, or a <em>copy</em>, depending on the internal layout and various implementation details. A &quot;view&quot; is, as the term suggests, a view into the original data, so modifying the view may modify the original object. On the other hand, a &quot;copy&quot; is a replication of data from the original, and modifying the copy has no effect on the original.</p>\n<p>As mentioned by other answers, the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> was created to flag &quot;chained assignment&quot; operations. Consider <code>df</code> in the setup above. Suppose you would like to select all values in column &quot;B&quot; where values in column &quot;A&quot; is &gt; 5. Pandas allows you to do this in different ways, some more correct than others. For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B']\n \n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B']\n\n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>These return the same result, so if you are only reading these values, it makes no difference. So, what is the issue? The problem with chained assignment, is that it is generally difficult to predict whether a view or a copy is returned, <strong>so this largely becomes an issue when you are attempting to assign values back.</strong> To build on the earlier example, consider how this code is executed by the interpreter:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__setitem__((df.A &gt; 5, 'B'), 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>With a single <code>__setitem__</code> call to <code>df</code>. OTOH, consider this code:</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__getitem__(df.A &gt; 5).__setitem__('B&quot;, 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now, depending on whether <code>__getitem__</code> returned a view or a copy, the <code>__setitem__</code> operation <strong>may not work</strong>.</p>\n<p>In general, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.loc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>loc</code></a> for label-based assignment, and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iloc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iloc</code></a> for integer/positional based assignment, as the spec guarantees that they always operate on the original. Additionally, for setting a single cell, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.at.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>at</code></a> and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iat.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iat</code></a>.</p>\n<p>More can be found in the <a href=\"http://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/indexing.html#returning-a-view-versus-a-copy\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">documentation</a>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Note</strong><br />\nAll boolean indexing operations done with <code>loc</code> can also be done with <code>iloc</code>. The only difference is that <code>iloc</code> expects either\nintegers/positions for index or a numpy array of boolean values, and\ninteger/position indexes for the columns.</p>\n<p>For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written nas</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[(df.A &gt; 5).values, 1] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'A'] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written as</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[1, 0] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>And so on.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<p>#Just tell me how to suppress the warning!</p>\n<p>Consider a simple operation on the &quot;A&quot; column of <code>df</code>. Selecting &quot;A&quot; and dividing by 2 will raise the warning, but the operation will work.</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']]\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n/Library/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.6/lib/python3.6/site-packages/IPython/__main__.py:1: SettingWithCopyWarning: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n\ndf2\n     A\n0  2.5\n1  4.5\n2  3.5\n</code></pre>\n<p>There are a couple ways of directly silencing this warning:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p><strong>Make a <code>deepcopy</code></strong></p>\n<pre><code> df2 = df[['A']].copy(deep=True)\n df2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Change <code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment</code></strong><br />\nCan be set to <code>None</code>, <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code>, or <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code>. <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code> is the default. <code>None</code> will suppress the warning entirely, and <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code> will throw a <code>SettingWithCopyError</code>, preventing the operation from going through.</p>\n<pre><code> pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = None\n df2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/20625582/how-to-deal-with-settingwithcopywarning-in-pandas#comment62589711_20625582\">@Peter Cotton</a> in the comments, came up with a nice way of non-intrusively changing the mode (modified from <a href=\"https://gist.github.com/notbanker/2be3ed34539c86e22ffdd88fd95ad8bc\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this gist</a>) using a context manager, to set the mode only as long as it is required, and the reset it back to the original state when finished.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>class ChainedAssignent:\n    def __init__(self, chained=None):\n        acceptable = [None, 'warn', 'raise']\n        assert chained in acceptable, &quot;chained must be in &quot; + str(acceptable)\n        self.swcw = chained\n\n    def __enter__(self):\n        self.saved_swcw = pd.options.mode.chained_assignment\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.swcw\n        return self\n\n    def __exit__(self, *args):\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.saved_swcw\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The usage is as follows:</p>\n<pre><code># some code here\nwith ChainedAssignent():\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n# more code follows\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or, to raise the exception</p>\n<pre><code>with ChainedAssignent(chained='raise'):\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n\nSettingWithCopyError: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#The &quot;XY Problem&quot;: What am I doing wrong?</p>\n<p>A lot of the time, users attempt to look for ways of suppressing this exception without fully understanding why it was raised in the first place. This is a good example of an <a href=\"https://meta.stackexchange.com/q/66377\">XY problem</a>, where users attempt to solve a problem &quot;Y&quot; that is actually a symptom of a deeper rooted problem &quot;X&quot;. Questions will be raised based on common problems that encounter this warning, and solutions will then be presented.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 1</strong><br />\nI have a DataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>df\n       A  B  C  D  E\n    0  5  0  3  3  7\n    1  9  3  5  2  4\n    2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I want to assign values in col &quot;A&quot; &gt; 5 to 1000. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>      A  B  C  D  E\n0     5  0  3  3  7\n1  1000  3  5  2  4\n2  1000  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Wrong way to do this:</p>\n<pre><code>df.A[df.A &gt; 5] = 1000         # works, because df.A returns a view\ndf[df.A &gt; 5]['A'] = 1000      # does not work\ndf.loc[df.A  5]['A'] = 1000   # does not work\n</code></pre>\n<p>Right way using <code>loc</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'A'] = 1000\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 2<sup>1</sup></strong><br />\nI am trying to set the value in cell (1, 'D') to 12345. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C      D  E\n0  5  0  3      3  7\n1  9  3  5  12345  4\n2  7  6  8      8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I have tried different ways of accessing this cell, such as\n<code>df['D'][1]</code>. What is the best way to do this?</p>\n<p><sub>1. This question isn't specifically related to the warning, but\nit is good to understand how to do this particular operation correctly\nso as to avoid situations where the warning could potentially arise in\nfuture.</sub></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You can use any of the following methods to do this.</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iloc[1, 3] = 12345\ndf.at[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iat[1, 3] = 12345\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 3</strong><br />\nI am trying to subset values based on some condition. I have a\nDataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I would like to assign values in &quot;D&quot; to 123 such that &quot;C&quot; == 5. I\ntried</p>\n<pre><code>df2.loc[df2.C == 5, 'D'] = 123\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which seems fine but I am <em>still</em> getting the\n<code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>! How do I fix this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is actually probably because of code higher up in your pipeline. Did you create <code>df2</code> from something larger, like</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>? In this case, boolean indexing will return a view, so <code>df2</code> will reference the original. What you'd need to do is assign <code>df2</code> to a <em>copy</em>:</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5].copy()\n# Or,\n# df2 = df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, :]\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 4</strong><br />\nI'm trying to drop column &quot;C&quot; in-place from</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>But using</p>\n<pre><code>df2.drop('C', axis=1, inplace=True)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Throws <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>. Why is this happening?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is because <code>df2</code> must have been created as a view from some other slicing operation, such as</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>The solution here is to either make a <code>copy()</code> of <code>df</code>, or use <code>loc</code>, as before.</p>\n","comment":"move footnote inside question pane","revision_guid":"53E29863-3B8B-4232-A03F-4BC43FD052B6"},{"user":{"account_id":6321039,"reputation":407685,"user_id":4909087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Zcszo.png?s=256","display_name":"coldspeed95","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/4909087/coldspeed95"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1557291973,"post_id":53954986,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":12,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<p>###How to deal with <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> in Pandas?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This post is meant for readers who,</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Would like to understand what this warning means</li>\n<li>Would like to understand different ways of suppressing this warning</li>\n<li>Would like to understand how to improve their code and follow good practices to avoid this warning in the future.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Setup</strong></p>\n<pre><code>np.random.seed(0)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(np.random.choice(10, (3, 5)), columns=list('ABCDE'))\ndf\n   A  B  C  D  E\n0  5  0  3  3  7\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#What is the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>?</p>\n<p>To know how to deal with this warning, it is important to understand what it means and why it is raised in the first place.</p>\n<p>When filtering DataFrames, it is possible slice/index a frame to return either a <em>view</em>, or a <em>copy</em>, depending on the internal layout and various implementation details. A &quot;view&quot; is, as the term suggests, a view into the original data, so modifying the view may modify the original object. On the other hand, a &quot;copy&quot; is a replication of data from the original, and modifying the copy has no effect on the original.</p>\n<p>As mentioned by other answers, the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> was created to flag &quot;chained assignment&quot; operations. Consider <code>df</code> in the setup above. Suppose you would like to select all values in column &quot;B&quot; where values in column &quot;A&quot; is &gt; 5. Pandas allows you to do this in different ways, some more correct than others. For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B']\n \n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B']\n\n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>These return the same result, so if you are only reading these values, it makes no difference. So, what is the issue? The problem with chained assignment, is that it is generally difficult to predict whether a view or a copy is returned, <strong>so this largely becomes an issue when you are attempting to assign values back.</strong> To build on the earlier example, consider how this code is executed by the interpreter:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__setitem__((df.A &gt; 5, 'B'), 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>With a single <code>__setitem__</code> call to <code>df</code>. OTOH, consider this code:</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__getitem__(df.A &gt; 5).__setitem__('B&quot;, 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now, depending on whether <code>__getitem__</code> returned a view or a copy, the <code>__setitem__</code> operation <strong>may not work</strong>.</p>\n<p>In general, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.loc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>loc</code></a> for label-based assignment, and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iloc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iloc</code></a> for integer/positional based assignment, as the spec guarantees that they always operate on the original. Additionally, for setting a single cell, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.at.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>at</code></a> and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iat.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iat</code></a>.</p>\n<p>More can be found in the <a href=\"http://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/indexing.html#returning-a-view-versus-a-copy\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">documentation</a>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Note</strong><br />\nAll boolean indexing operations done with <code>loc</code> can also be done with <code>iloc</code>. The only difference is that <code>iloc</code> expects either\nintegers/positions for index or a numpy array of boolean values, and\ninteger/position indexes for the columns.</p>\n<p>For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written nas</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[(df.A &gt; 5).values, 1] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'A'] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written as</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[1, 0] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>And so on.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<p>#Just tell me how to suppress the warning!</p>\n<p>Consider a simple operation on the &quot;A&quot; column of <code>df</code>. Selecting &quot;A&quot; and dividing by 2 will raise the warning, but the operation will work.</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']]\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n/Library/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.6/lib/python3.6/site-packages/IPython/__main__.py:1: SettingWithCopyWarning: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n\ndf2\n     A\n0  2.5\n1  4.5\n2  3.5\n</code></pre>\n<p>There are a couple ways of directly silencing this warning:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p><strong>Make a <code>deepcopy</code></strong></p>\n<pre><code> df2 = df[['A']].copy(deep=True)\n df2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Change <code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment</code></strong><br />\nCan be set to <code>None</code>, <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code>, or <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code>. <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code> is the default. <code>None</code> will suppress the warning entirely, and <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code> will throw a <code>SettingWithCopyError</code>, preventing the operation from going through.</p>\n<pre><code> pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = None\n df2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/20625582/how-to-deal-with-settingwithcopywarning-in-pandas#comment62589711_20625582\">@Peter Cotton</a> in the comments, came up with a nice way of non-intrusively changing the mode (modified from <a href=\"https://gist.github.com/notbanker/2be3ed34539c86e22ffdd88fd95ad8bc\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this gist</a>) using a context manager, to set the mode only as long as it is required, and the reset it back to the original state when finished.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>class ChainedAssignent:\n    def __init__(self, chained=None):\n        acceptable = [None, 'warn', 'raise']\n        assert chained in acceptable, &quot;chained must be in &quot; + str(acceptable)\n        self.swcw = chained\n\n    def __enter__(self):\n        self.saved_swcw = pd.options.mode.chained_assignment\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.swcw\n        return self\n\n    def __exit__(self, *args):\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.saved_swcw\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The usage is as follows:</p>\n<pre><code># some code here\nwith ChainedAssignent():\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n# more code follows\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or, to raise the exception</p>\n<pre><code>with ChainedAssignent(chained='raise'):\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n\nSettingWithCopyError: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#The &quot;XY Problem&quot;: What am I doing wrong?</p>\n<p>A lot of the time, users attempt to look for ways of suppressing this exception without fully understanding why it was raised in the first place. This is a good example of an <a href=\"https://meta.stackexchange.com/q/66377\">XY problem</a>, where users attempt to solve a problem &quot;Y&quot; that is actually a symptom of a deeper rooted problem &quot;X&quot;. Questions will be raised based on common problems that encounter this warning, and solutions will then be presented.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 1</strong><br />\nI have a DataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>df\n       A  B  C  D  E\n    0  5  0  3  3  7\n    1  9  3  5  2  4\n    2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I want to assign values in col &quot;A&quot; &gt; 5 to 1000. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>      A  B  C  D  E\n0     5  0  3  3  7\n1  1000  3  5  2  4\n2  1000  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Wrong way to do this:</p>\n<pre><code>df.A[df.A &gt; 5] = 1000         # works, because df.A returns a view\ndf[df.A &gt; 5]['A'] = 1000      # does not work\ndf.loc[df.A  5]['A'] = 1000   # does not work\n</code></pre>\n<p>Right way using <code>loc</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'A'] = 1000\n</code></pre>\n<p><sub>This next question isn't specifically related to the warning, but it is good to understand how to do this particular operation correctly so as to avoid situations where the warning could potentially arise in future.</sub></p>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 2</strong><br />\nI am trying to set the value in cell (1, 'D') to 12345. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C      D  E\n0  5  0  3      3  7\n1  9  3  5  12345  4\n2  7  6  8      8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I have tried different ways of accessing this cell, such as\n<code>df['D'][1]</code>. What is the best way to do this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You can use any of the following methods to do this.</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iloc[1, 3] = 12345\ndf.at[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iat[1, 3] = 12345\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 3</strong><br />\nI am trying to subset values based on some condition. I have a\nDataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I would like to assign values in &quot;D&quot; to 123 such that &quot;C&quot; == 5. I\ntried</p>\n<pre><code>df2.loc[df2.C == 5, 'D'] = 123\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which seems fine but I am <em>still</em> getting the\n<code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>! How do I fix this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is actually probably because of code higher up in your pipeline. Did you create <code>df2</code> from something larger, like</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>? In this case, boolean indexing will return a view, so <code>df2</code> will reference the original. What you'd need to do is assign <code>df2</code> to a <em>copy</em>:</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5].copy()\n# Or,\n# df2 = df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, :]\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 4</strong><br />\nI'm trying to drop column &quot;C&quot; in-place from</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>But using</p>\n<pre><code>df2.drop('C', axis=1, inplace=True)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Throws <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>. Why is this happening?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is because <code>df2</code> must have been created as a view from some other slicing operation, such as</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>The solution here is to either make a <code>copy()</code> of <code>df</code>, or use <code>loc</code>, as before.</p>\n","comment":"deleted 10 characters in body","revision_guid":"AD86A888-C125-43D4-AA1B-2BD590FE93B2"},{"user":{"account_id":6321039,"reputation":407685,"user_id":4909087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Zcszo.png?s=256","display_name":"coldspeed95","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/4909087/coldspeed95"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1556532090,"post_id":53954986,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":11,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<p>###How to deal with <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> in Pandas?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This post is meant for readers who,</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Would like to understand what this warning means</li>\n<li>Would like to understand different ways of suppressing this warning</li>\n<li>Would like to understand how to improve their code and follow good practices to avoid this warning in the future.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Setup</strong></p>\n<pre><code>np.random.seed(0)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(np.random.choice(10, (3, 5)), columns=list('ABCDE'))\ndf\n   A  B  C  D  E\n0  5  0  3  3  7\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#What is the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>?</p>\n<p>To know how to deal with this warning, it is important to understand what it means and why it is raised in the first place.</p>\n<p>When filtering DataFrames, it is possible slice/index a frame to return either a <em>view</em>, or a <em>copy</em>, depending on the internal layout and various implementation details. A &quot;view&quot; is, as the term suggests, a view into the original data, so modifying the view may modify the original object. On the other hand, a &quot;copy&quot; is a replication of data from the original, and modifying the copy has no effect on the original.</p>\n<p>As mentioned by other answers, the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> was created to flag &quot;chained assignment&quot; operations. Consider <code>df</code> in the setup above. Suppose you would like to select all values in column &quot;B&quot; where values in column &quot;A&quot; is &gt; 5. Pandas allows you to do this in different ways, some more correct than others. For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B']\n \n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B']\n\n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>These return the same result, so if you are only reading these values, it makes no difference. So, what is the issue? The problem with chained assignment, is that it is generally difficult to predict whether a view or a copy is returned, <strong>so this largely becomes an issue when you are attempting to assign values back.</strong> To build on the earlier example, consider how this code is executed by the interpreter:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__setitem__((df.A &gt; 5, 'B'), 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>With a single <code>__setitem__</code> call to <code>df</code>. OTOH, consider this code:</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__getitem__(df.A &gt; 5).__setitem__('B&quot;, 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now, depending on whether <code>__getitem__</code> returned a view or a copy, the <code>__setitem__</code> operation <strong>may not work</strong>.</p>\n<p>In general, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.loc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>loc</code></a> for label-based assignment, and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iloc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iloc</code></a> for integer/positional based assignment, as the spec guarantees that they always operate on the original. Additionally, for setting a single cell, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.at.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>at</code></a> and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iat.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iat</code></a>.</p>\n<p>More can be found in the <a href=\"http://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/indexing.html#returning-a-view-versus-a-copy\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">documentation</a>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Note</strong><br />\nAll boolean indexing operations done with <code>loc</code> can also be done with <code>iloc</code>. The only difference is that <code>iloc</code> expects either\nintegers/positions for index or a numpy array of boolean values, and\ninteger/position indexes for the columns.</p>\n<p>For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written nas</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[(df.A &gt; 5).values, 1] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'A'] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written as</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[1, 0] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>And so on.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<p>#Just tell me how to suppress the warning!</p>\n<p>Consider a simple operation on the &quot;A&quot; column of <code>df</code>. Selecting &quot;A&quot; and dividing by 2 will raise the warning, but the operation will work.</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']]\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n/Library/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.6/lib/python3.6/site-packages/IPython/__main__.py:1: SettingWithCopyWarning: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n\ndf2\n     A\n0  2.5\n1  4.5\n2  3.5\n</code></pre>\n<p>There are a couple ways of directly silencing this warning:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p><strong>Make a <code>deepcopy</code></strong></p>\n<pre><code> df2 = df[['A']].copy(deep=True)\n df2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Change <code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment</code></strong><br />\nCan be set to <code>None</code>, <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code>, or <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code>. <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code> is the default. <code>None</code> will suppress the warning entirely, and <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code> will throw a <code>SettingWithCopyError</code>, preventing the operation from going through.</p>\n<pre><code> pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = None\n df2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/20625582/how-to-deal-with-settingwithcopywarning-in-pandas#comment62589711_20625582\">@Peter Cotton</a> in the comments, came up with a nice way of non-intrusively changing the mode (modified from this gist <a href=\"https://gist.github.com/notbanker/2be3ed34539c86e22ffdd88fd95ad8bc\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this gist</a>) using a context manager, to set the mode only as long as it is required, and the reset it back to the original state when finished.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>class ChainedAssignent:\n    def __init__(self, chained=None):\n        acceptable = [None, 'warn', 'raise']\n        assert chained in acceptable, &quot;chained must be in &quot; + str(acceptable)\n        self.swcw = chained\n\n    def __enter__(self):\n        self.saved_swcw = pd.options.mode.chained_assignment\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.swcw\n        return self\n\n    def __exit__(self, *args):\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.saved_swcw\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The usage is as follows:</p>\n<pre><code># some code here\nwith ChainedAssignent():\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n# more code follows\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or, to raise the exception</p>\n<pre><code>with ChainedAssignent(chained='raise'):\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n\nSettingWithCopyError: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#The &quot;XY Problem&quot;: What am I doing wrong?</p>\n<p>A lot of the time, users attempt to look for ways of suppressing this exception without fully understanding why it was raised in the first place. This is a good example of an <a href=\"https://meta.stackexchange.com/q/66377\">XY problem</a>, where users attempt to solve a problem &quot;Y&quot; that is actually a symptom of a deeper rooted problem &quot;X&quot;. Questions will be raised based on common problems that encounter this warning, and solutions will then be presented.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 1</strong><br />\nI have a DataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>df\n       A  B  C  D  E\n    0  5  0  3  3  7\n    1  9  3  5  2  4\n    2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I want to assign values in col &quot;A&quot; &gt; 5 to 1000. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>      A  B  C  D  E\n0     5  0  3  3  7\n1  1000  3  5  2  4\n2  1000  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Wrong way to do this:</p>\n<pre><code>df.A[df.A &gt; 5] = 1000         # works, because df.A returns a view\ndf[df.A &gt; 5]['A'] = 1000      # does not work\ndf.loc[df.A  5]['A'] = 1000   # does not work\n</code></pre>\n<p>Right way using <code>loc</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'A'] = 1000\n</code></pre>\n<p><sub>This next question isn't specifically related to the warning, but it is good to understand how to do this particular operation correctly so as to avoid situations where the warning could potentially arise in future.</sub></p>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 2</strong><br />\nI am trying to set the value in cell (1, 'D') to 12345. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C      D  E\n0  5  0  3      3  7\n1  9  3  5  12345  4\n2  7  6  8      8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I have tried different ways of accessing this cell, such as\n<code>df['D'][1]</code>. What is the best way to do this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You can use any of the following methods to do this.</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iloc[1, 3] = 12345\ndf.at[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iat[1, 3] = 12345\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 3</strong><br />\nI am trying to subset values based on some condition. I have a\nDataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I would like to assign values in &quot;D&quot; to 123 such that &quot;C&quot; == 5. I\ntried</p>\n<pre><code>df2.loc[df2.C == 5, 'D'] = 123\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which seems fine but I am <em>still</em> getting the\n<code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>! How do I fix this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is actually probably because of code higher up in your pipeline. Did you create <code>df2</code> from something larger, like</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>? In this case, boolean indexing will return a view, so <code>df2</code> will reference the original. What you'd need to do is assign <code>df2</code> to a <em>copy</em>:</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5].copy()\n# Or,\n# df2 = df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, :]\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 4</strong><br />\nI'm trying to drop column &quot;C&quot; in-place from</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>But using</p>\n<pre><code>df2.drop('C', axis=1, inplace=True)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Throws <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>. Why is this happening?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is because <code>df2</code> must have been created as a view from some other slicing operation, such as</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>The solution here is to either make a <code>copy()</code> of <code>df</code>, or use <code>loc</code>, as before.</p>\n","comment":"spacing issues in quoted code","revision_guid":"E2F7A865-F63A-4046-9BEE-CB47780E41C1"},{"user":{"account_id":6321039,"reputation":407685,"user_id":4909087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Zcszo.png?s=256","display_name":"coldspeed95","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/4909087/coldspeed95"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1555185699,"post_id":53954986,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":10,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<p>###How to deal with <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> in Pandas?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This post is meant for readers who,</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Would like to understand what this warning means</li>\n<li>Would like to understand different ways of suppressing this warning</li>\n<li>Would like to understand how to improve their code and follow good practices to avoid this warning in the future.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Setup</strong></p>\n<pre><code>np.random.seed(0)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(np.random.choice(10, (3, 5)), columns=list('ABCDE'))\ndf\n   A  B  C  D  E\n0  5  0  3  3  7\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#What is the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>?</p>\n<p>To know how to deal with this warning, it is important to understand what it means and why it is raised in the first place.</p>\n<p>When filtering DataFrames, it is possible slice/index a frame to return either a <em>view</em>, or a <em>copy</em>, depending on the internal layout and various implementation details. A &quot;view&quot; is, as the term suggests, a view into the original data, so modifying the view may modify the original object. On the other hand, a &quot;copy&quot; is a replication of data from the original, and modifying the copy has no effect on the original.</p>\n<p>As mentioned by other answers, the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> was created to flag &quot;chained assignment&quot; operations. Consider <code>df</code> in the setup above. Suppose you would like to select all values in column &quot;B&quot; where values in column &quot;A&quot; is &gt; 5. Pandas allows you to do this in different ways, some more correct than others. For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B']\n \n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B']\n\n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>These return the same result, so if you are only reading these values, it makes no difference. So, what is the issue? The problem with chained assignment, is that it is generally difficult to predict whether a view or a copy is returned, <strong>so this largely becomes an issue when you are attempting to assign values back.</strong> To build on the earlier example, consider how this code is executed by the interpreter:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__setitem__((df.A &gt; 5, 'B'), 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>With a single <code>__setitem__</code> call to <code>df</code>. OTOH, consider this code:</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__getitem__(df.A &gt; 5).__setitem__('B&quot;, 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now, depending on whether <code>__getitem__</code> returned a view or a copy, the <code>__setitem__</code> operation <strong>may not work</strong>.</p>\n<p>In general, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.loc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>loc</code></a> for label-based assignment, and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iloc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iloc</code></a> for integer/positional based assignment, as the spec guarantees that they always operate on the original. Additionally, for setting a single cell, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.at.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>at</code></a> and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iat.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iat</code></a>.</p>\n<p>More can be found in the <a href=\"http://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/indexing.html#returning-a-view-versus-a-copy\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">documentation</a>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Note</strong><br />\nAll boolean indexing operations done with <code>loc</code> can also be done with <code>iloc</code>. The only difference is that <code>iloc</code> expects either\nintegers/positions for index or a numpy array of boolean values, and\ninteger/position indexes for the columns.</p>\n<p>For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written nas</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[(df.A &gt; 5).values, 1] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'A'] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written as</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[1, 0] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>And so on.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<p>#Just tell me how to suppress the warning!</p>\n<p>Consider a simple operation on the &quot;A&quot; column of <code>df</code>. Selecting &quot;A&quot; and dividing by 2 will raise the warning, but the operation will work.</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']]\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n/Library/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.6/lib/python3.6/site-packages/IPython/__main__.py:1: SettingWithCopyWarning: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n\ndf2\n     A\n0  2.5\n1  4.5\n2  3.5\n</code></pre>\n<p>There are a couple ways of directly silencing this warning:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p><strong>Make a <code>deepcopy</code></strong></p>\n<pre><code> df2 = df[['A']].copy(deep=True)\n df2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p><strong>Change <code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment</code></strong><br />\nCan be set to <code>None</code>, <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code>, or <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code>. <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code> is the default. <code>None</code> will suppress the warning entirely, and <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code> will throw a <code>SettingWithCopyError</code>, preventing the operation from going through.</p>\n<pre><code> pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = None\n df2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/20625582/how-to-deal-with-settingwithcopywarning-in-pandas#comment62589711_20625582\">@Peter Cotton</a> in the comments, came up with a nice way of non-intrusively changing the mode (see <a href=\"https://gist.github.com/notbanker/2be3ed34539c86e22ffdd88fd95ad8bc\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this gist</a>) using a context manager, to set the mode only as long as it is required, and the reset it back to the original state when finished.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>class ChainedAssignent:\n    def __init__(self, chained=None):\n        acceptable = [ None, 'warn','raise']\n        assert chained in acceptable, &quot;chained must be in &quot; + str(acceptable)\n        self.swcw = chained\n\n    def __enter__( self ):\n        self.saved_swcw = pd.options.mode.chained_assignment\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.swcw\n        return self\n\n    def __exit__(self, *args):\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.saved_swcw\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The usage is as follows:</p>\n<pre><code># some code here\nwith ChainedAssignent():\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n# more code follows\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or, to raise the exception</p>\n<pre><code>with ChainedAssignent(chained='raise'):\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n\nSettingWithCopyError: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#The &quot;XY Problem&quot;: What am I doing wrong?</p>\n<p>A lot of the time, users attempt to look for ways of suppressing this exception without fully understanding why it was raised in the first place. This is a good example of an <a href=\"https://meta.stackexchange.com/q/66377\">XY problem</a>, where users attempt to solve a problem &quot;Y&quot; that is actually a symptom of a deeper rooted problem &quot;X&quot;. Questions will be raised based on common problems that encounter this warning, and solutions will then be presented.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 1</strong><br />\nI have a DataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>df\n       A  B  C  D  E\n    0  5  0  3  3  7\n    1  9  3  5  2  4\n    2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I want to assign values in col &quot;A&quot; &gt; 5 to 1000. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>      A  B  C  D  E\n0     5  0  3  3  7\n1  1000  3  5  2  4\n2  1000  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Wrong way to do this:</p>\n<pre><code>df.A[df.A &gt; 5] = 1000         # works, because df.A returns a view\ndf[df.A &gt; 5]['A'] = 1000      # does not work\ndf.loc[df.A  5]['A'] = 1000   # does not work\n</code></pre>\n<p>Right way using <code>loc</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'A'] = 1000\n</code></pre>\n<p><sub>This next question isn't specifically related to the warning, but it is good to understand how to do this particular operation correctly so as to avoid situations where the warning could potentially arise in future.</sub></p>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 2</strong><br />\nI am trying to set the value in cell (1, 'D') to 12345. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C      D  E\n0  5  0  3      3  7\n1  9  3  5  12345  4\n2  7  6  8      8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I have tried different ways of accessing this cell, such as\n<code>df['D'][1]</code>. What is the best way to do this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You can use any of the following methods to do this.</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iloc[1, 3] = 12345\ndf.at[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iat[1, 3] = 12345\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 3</strong><br />\nI am trying to subset values based on some condition. I have a\nDataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I would like to assign values in &quot;D&quot; to 123 such that &quot;C&quot; == 5. I\ntried</p>\n<pre><code>df2.loc[df2.C == 5, 'D'] = 123\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which seems fine but I am <em>still</em> getting the\n<code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>! How do I fix this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is actually probably because of code higher up in your pipeline. Did you create <code>df2</code> from something larger, like</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>? In this case, boolean indexing will return a view, so <code>df2</code> will reference the original. What you'd need to do is assign <code>df2</code> to a <em>copy</em>:</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5].copy()\n# Or,\n# df2 = df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, :]\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 4</strong><br />\nI'm trying to drop column &quot;C&quot; in-place from</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>But using</p>\n<pre><code>df2.drop('C', axis=1, inplace=True)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Throws <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>. Why is this happening?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is because <code>df2</code> must have been created as a view from some other slicing operation, such as</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>The solution here is to either make a <code>copy()</code> of <code>df</code>, or use <code>loc</code>, as before.</p>\n","comment":"added 74 characters in body","revision_guid":"8280CC3D-8B24-4D71-B8C3-7DFC08866F1F"},{"user":{"account_id":6321039,"reputation":407685,"user_id":4909087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Zcszo.png?s=256","display_name":"coldspeed95","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/4909087/coldspeed95"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1552326395,"post_id":53954986,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":9,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>This post is meant for readers who,</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Would like to understand what this warning means</li>\n<li>Would like to understand different ways of suppressing this warning</li>\n<li>Would like to understand how to improve their code and follow good practices to avoid this warning in the future.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Setup</strong></p>\n<pre><code>np.random.seed(0)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(np.random.choice(10, (3, 5)), columns=list('ABCDE'))\ndf\n   A  B  C  D  E\n0  5  0  3  3  7\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#What is the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>?</p>\n<p>To know how to deal with this warning, it is important to understand what it means and why it is raised in the first place.</p>\n<p>When filtering DataFrames, it is possible slice/index a frame to return either a <em>view</em>, or a <em>copy</em>, depending on the internal layout and various implementation details. A &quot;view&quot; is, as the term suggests, a view into the original data, so modifying the view may modify the original object. On the other hand, a &quot;copy&quot; is a replication of data from the original, and modifying the copy has no effect on the original.</p>\n<p>As mentioned by other answers, the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> was created to flag &quot;chained assignment&quot; operations. Consider <code>df</code> in the setup above. Suppose you would like to select all values in column &quot;B&quot; where values in column &quot;A&quot; is &gt; 5. Pandas allows you to do this in different ways, some more correct than others. For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B']\n \n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B']\n\n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>These return the same result, so if you are only reading these values, it makes no difference. So, what is the issue? The problem with chained assignment, is that it is generally difficult to predict whether a view or a copy is returned, <strong>so this largely becomes an issue when you are attempting to assign values back.</strong> To build on the earlier example, consider how this code is executed by the interpreter:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__setitem__((df.A &gt; 5, 'B'), 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>With a single <code>__setitem__</code> call to <code>df</code>. OTOH, consider this code:</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__getitem__(df.A &gt; 5).__setitem__('B&quot;, 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now, depending on whether <code>__getitem__</code> returned a view or a copy, the <code>__setitem__</code> operation <strong>may not work</strong>.</p>\n<p>In general, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.loc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>loc</code></a> for label-based assignment, and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iloc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iloc</code></a> for integer/positional based assignment, as the spec guarantees that they always operate on the original. Additionally, for setting a single cell, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.at.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>at</code></a> and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iat.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iat</code></a>.</p>\n<p>More can be found in the <a href=\"http://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/indexing.html#returning-a-view-versus-a-copy\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">documentation</a>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Note</strong><br />\nAll boolean indexing operations done with <code>loc</code> can also be done with <code>iloc</code>. The only difference is that <code>iloc</code> expects either\nintegers/positions for index or a numpy array of boolean values, and\ninteger/position indexes for the columns.</p>\n<p>For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written nas</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[(df.A &gt; 5).values, 1] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'A'] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written as</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[1, 0] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>And so on.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<p>#Just tell me how to suppress the warning!</p>\n<p>Consider a simple operation on the &quot;A&quot; column of <code>df</code>. Selecting &quot;A&quot; and dividing by 2 will raise the warning, but the operation will work.</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']]\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n/Library/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.6/lib/python3.6/site-packages/IPython/__main__.py:1: SettingWithCopyWarning: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n\ndf2\n     A\n0  2.5\n1  4.5\n2  3.5\n</code></pre>\n<p>There are a few ways of silencing this warning, shown below.</p>\n<p><strong>Make a <code>deepcopy</code></strong></p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']].copy(deep=True)\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<p><strong>Change <code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment</code></strong><br />\nCan be set to <code>None</code>, <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code>, or <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code>. <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code> is the default. <code>None</code> will suppress the warning entirely, and <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code> will throw a <code>SettingWithCopyError</code>, preventing the operation from going through.</p>\n<pre><code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = None\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/20625582/how-to-deal-with-settingwithcopywarning-in-pandas#comment62589711_20625582\">@Peter Cotton</a> in the comments, came up with a nice way of non-intrusively changing the mode (see <a href=\"https://gist.github.com/notbanker/2be3ed34539c86e22ffdd88fd95ad8bc\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this gist</a>) using a context manager, to set the mode only as long as it is required, and the reset it back to the original state when finished.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>class ChainedAssignent:\n    def __init__(self, chained=None):\n        acceptable = [ None, 'warn','raise']\n        assert chained in acceptable, &quot;chained must be in &quot; + str(acceptable)\n        self.swcw = chained\n\n    def __enter__( self ):\n        self.saved_swcw = pd.options.mode.chained_assignment\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.swcw\n        return self\n\n    def __exit__(self, *args):\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.saved_swcw\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The usage is as follows:</p>\n<pre><code># some code here\nwith ChainedAssignent():\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n# more code follows\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or, to raise the exception</p>\n<pre><code>with ChainedAssignent(chained='raise'):\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n\nSettingWithCopyError: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#The &quot;XY Problem&quot;: What am I doing wrong?</p>\n<p>A lot of the time, users attempt to look for ways of suppressing this exception without fully understanding why it was raised in the first place. This is a good example of an <a href=\"https://meta.stackexchange.com/q/66377\">XY problem</a>, where users attempt to solve a problem &quot;Y&quot; that is actually a symptom of a deeper rooted problem &quot;X&quot;. Questions will be raised based on common problems that encounter this warning, and solutions will then be presented.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 1</strong><br />\nI have a DataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>df\n       A  B  C  D  E\n    0  5  0  3  3  7\n    1  9  3  5  2  4\n    2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I want to assign values in col &quot;A&quot; &gt; 5 to 1000. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>      A  B  C  D  E\n0     5  0  3  3  7\n1  1000  3  5  2  4\n2  1000  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Wrong way to do this:</p>\n<pre><code>df.A[df.A &gt; 5] = 1000         # works, because df.A returns a view\ndf[df.A &gt; 5]['A'] = 1000      # does not work\ndf.loc[df.A  5]['A'] = 1000   # does not work\n</code></pre>\n<p>Right way using <code>loc</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'A'] = 1000\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 2</strong><br />\n<sub>This question isn't specifically related to the warning, but it is good to understand how to do this particular\noperation correctly so as to avoid situations where the warning could\npotentially arise in future.</sub> <br/><br />\nI am trying to set the value in cell (1, 'D') to 12345. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C      D  E\n0  5  0  3      3  7\n1  9  3  5  12345  4\n2  7  6  8      8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I have tried different ways of accessing this cell, such as\n<code>df['D'][1]</code>. What is the best way to do this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You can use any of the following methods to do this.</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iloc[1, 3] = 12345\ndf.at[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iat[1, 3] = 12345\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 3</strong><br />\nI am trying to subset values based on some condition. I have a\nDataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I would like to assign values in &quot;D&quot; to 123 such that &quot;C&quot; == 5. I\ntried</p>\n<pre><code>df2.loc[df2.C == 5, 'D'] = 123\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which seems fine but I am <em>still</em> getting the\n<code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>! How do I fix this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is actually probably because of code higher up in your pipeline. Did you create <code>df2</code> from something larger, like</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>? In this case, boolean indexing will return a view, so <code>df2</code> will reference the original. What you'd need to do is assign <code>df2</code> to a <em>copy</em>:</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5].copy()\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or,</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, :]\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 4</strong><br />\nI'm trying to drop column &quot;C&quot; in-place from</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>But using</p>\n<pre><code>df2.drop('C', axis=1, inplace=True)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Throws <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>. Why is this happening?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is because <code>df2</code> must have been created as a view from some other slicing operation, such as</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>The solution here is to either make a <code>copy()</code> of <code>df</code>, or use <code>loc</code>, as before.</p>\n","comment":"edited to remove deprecated suggestion","revision_guid":"9AC462CF-A878-4DDE-8C1B-E65FD35B7CB8"},{"user":{"account_id":6321039,"reputation":407685,"user_id":4909087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Zcszo.png?s=256","display_name":"coldspeed95","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/4909087/coldspeed95"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1548322048,"post_id":53954986,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":8,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>This post is meant for readers who,</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Would like to understand what this warning means</li>\n<li>Would like to understand different ways of suppressing this warning</li>\n<li>Would like to understand how to improve their code and follow good practices to avoid this warning in the future.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Setup</strong></p>\n<pre><code>np.random.seed(0)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(np.random.choice(10, (3, 5)), columns=list('ABCDE'))\ndf\n   A  B  C  D  E\n0  5  0  3  3  7\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#What is the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>?</p>\n<p>To know how to deal with this warning, it is important to understand what it means and why it is raised in the first place.</p>\n<p>When filtering DataFrames, it is possible slice/index a frame to return either a <em>view</em>, or a <em>copy</em>, depending on the internal layout and various implementation details. A &quot;view&quot; is, as the term suggests, a view into the original data, so modifying the view may modify the original object. On the other hand, a &quot;copy&quot; is a replication of data from the original, and modifying the copy has no effect on the original.</p>\n<p>As mentioned by other answers, the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> was created to flag &quot;chained assignment&quot; operations. Consider <code>df</code> in the setup above. Suppose you would like to select all values in column &quot;B&quot; where values in column &quot;A&quot; is &gt; 5. Pandas allows you to do this in different ways, some more correct than others. For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B']\n \n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B']\n\n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>These return the same result, so if you are only reading these values, it makes no difference. So, what is the issue? The problem with chained assignment, is that it is generally difficult to predict whether a view or a copy is returned, <strong>so this largely becomes an issue when you are attempting to assign values back.</strong> To build on the earlier example, consider how this code is executed by the interpreter:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__setitem__((df.A &gt; 5, 'B'), 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>With a single <code>__setitem__</code> call to <code>df</code>. OTOH, consider this code:</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__getitem__(df.A &gt; 5).__setitem__('B&quot;, 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now, depending on whether <code>__getitem__</code> returned a view or a copy, the <code>__setitem__</code> operation <strong>may not work</strong>.</p>\n<p>In general, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.loc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>loc</code></a> for label-based assignment, and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iloc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iloc</code></a> for integer/positional based assignment, as the spec guarantees that they always operate on the original. Additionally, for setting a single cell, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.at.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>at</code></a> and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iat.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iat</code></a>.</p>\n<p>More can be found in the <a href=\"http://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/indexing.html#returning-a-view-versus-a-copy\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">documentation</a>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Note</strong><br />\nAll boolean indexing operations done with <code>loc</code> can also be done with <code>iloc</code>. The only difference is that <code>iloc</code> expects either\nintegers/positions for index or a numpy array of boolean values, and\ninteger/position indexes for the columns.</p>\n<p>For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written nas</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[(df.A &gt; 5).values, 1] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'A'] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written as</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[1, 0] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>And so on.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<p>#Just tell me how to suppress the warning!</p>\n<p>Consider a simple operation on the &quot;A&quot; column of <code>df</code>. Selecting &quot;A&quot; and dividing by 2 will raise the warning, but the operation will work.</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']]\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n/Library/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.6/lib/python3.6/site-packages/IPython/__main__.py:1: SettingWithCopyWarning: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n\ndf2\n     A\n0  2.5\n1  4.5\n2  3.5\n</code></pre>\n<p>There are a few ways of silencing this warning, shown below.</p>\n<p><strong>Make a <code>deepcopy</code></strong></p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']].copy(deep=True)\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<p><strong>Set <code>is_copy=False</code></strong><br />\nTurn off the check for that particular DataFrame by setting <code>is_copy=False</code>.</p>\n<p>&lt;!- -&gt;</p>\n<pre><code>df2.is_copy = False\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<p><strong>Change <code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment</code></strong><br />\nCan be set to <code>None</code>, <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code>, or <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code>. <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code> is the default. <code>None</code> will suppress the warning entirely, and <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code> will throw a <code>SettingWithCopyError</code>, preventing the operation from going through.</p>\n<pre><code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = None\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<br/>\n<p><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/20625582/how-to-deal-with-settingwithcopywarning-in-pandas#comment62589711_20625582\">@Peter Cotton</a> came up with a nice way of non-intrusively changing the mode (see <a href=\"https://gist.github.com/notbanker/2be3ed34539c86e22ffdd88fd95ad8bc\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this gist</a>) using a context manager, to set the mode only as long as it is required, and the reset it back to the original state when finished.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>class ChainedAssignent:\n    def __init__(self, chained=None):\n        acceptable = [ None, 'warn','raise']\n        assert chained in acceptable, &quot;chained must be in &quot; + str(acceptable)\n        self.swcw = chained\n\n    def __enter__( self ):\n        self.saved_swcw = pd.options.mode.chained_assignment\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.swcw\n        return self\n\n    def __exit__(self, *args):\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.saved_swcw\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The usage is as follows:</p>\n<pre><code># some code here\nwith ChainedAssignent():\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n# more code follows\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or, to raise the exception</p>\n<pre><code>with ChainedAssignent(chained='raise'):\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n\nSettingWithCopyError: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#The &quot;XY Problem&quot;: What am I doing wrong?</p>\n<p>A lot of the time, users attempt to look for ways of suppressing this exception without fully understanding why it was raised in the first place. This is a good example of an <a href=\"https://meta.stackexchange.com/q/66377\">XY problem</a>, where users attempt to solve a problem &quot;Y&quot; that is actually a symptom of a deeper rooted problem &quot;X&quot;. Questions will be raised based on common problems that encounter this warning, and solutions will then be presented.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 1</strong><br />\nI have a DataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>df\n       A  B  C  D  E\n    0  5  0  3  3  7\n    1  9  3  5  2  4\n    2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I want to assign values in col &quot;A&quot; &gt; 5 to 1000. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>      A  B  C  D  E\n0     5  0  3  3  7\n1  1000  3  5  2  4\n2  1000  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Wrong way to do this:</p>\n<pre><code>df.A[df.A &gt; 5] = 1000         # works, because df.A returns a view\ndf[df.A &gt; 5]['A'] = 1000      # does not work\ndf.loc[df.A  5]['A'] = 1000   # does not work\n</code></pre>\n<p>Right way using <code>loc</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'A'] = 1000\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 2</strong><br />\n<sub>This question isn't specifically related to the warning, but it is good to understand how to do this particular\noperation correctly so as to avoid situations where the warning could\npotentially arise in future.</sub> <br/><br />\nI am trying to set the value in cell (1, 'D') to 12345. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C      D  E\n0  5  0  3      3  7\n1  9  3  5  12345  4\n2  7  6  8      8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I have tried different ways of accessing this cell, such as\n<code>df['D'][1]</code>. What is the best way to do this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You can use any of the following methods to do this.</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iloc[1, 3] = 12345\ndf.at[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iat[1, 3] = 12345\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 3</strong><br />\nI am trying to subset values based on some condition. I have a\nDataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I would like to assign values in &quot;D&quot; to 123 such that &quot;C&quot; == 5. I\ntried</p>\n<pre><code>df2.loc[df2.C == 5, 'D'] = 123\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which seems fine but I am <em>still</em> getting the\n<code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>! How do I fix this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is actually probably because of code higher up in your pipeline. Did you create <code>df2</code> from something larger, like</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>? In this case, boolean indexing will return a view, so <code>df2</code> will reference the original. What you'd need to do is assign <code>df2</code> to a <em>copy</em>:</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5].copy()\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or,</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, :]\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 4</strong><br />\nI'm trying to drop column &quot;C&quot; in-place from</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>But using</p>\n<pre><code>df2.drop('C', axis=1, inplace=True)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Throws <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>. Why is this happening?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is because <code>df2</code> must have been created as a view from some other slicing operation, such as</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>The solution here is to either make a <code>copy()</code> of <code>df</code>, or use <code>loc</code>, as before.</p>\n","comment":"added 28 characters in body","revision_guid":"3023EDB3-9380-4094-8B72-96F2221A6D54"},{"user":{"account_id":6321039,"reputation":407685,"user_id":4909087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Zcszo.png?s=256","display_name":"coldspeed95","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/4909087/coldspeed95"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1546061873,"post_id":53954986,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":7,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>This post is meant for readers who,</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Would like to understand what this warning means</li>\n<li>Would like to understand different ways of suppressing this warning</li>\n<li>Would like to understand how to improve their code and follow good practices to avoid this warning in the future.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Setup</strong></p>\n<pre><code>np.random.seed(0)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(np.random.choice(10, (3, 5)), columns=list('ABCDE'))\ndf\n   A  B  C  D  E\n0  5  0  3  3  7\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#What is the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>?</p>\n<p>To know how to deal with this warning, it is important to understand what it means and why it is raised in the first place.</p>\n<p>When filtering DataFrames, it is possible slice/index a frame to return either a <em>view</em>, or a <em>copy</em>, depending on the internal layout and various implementation details. A &quot;view&quot; is, as the term suggests, a view into the original data, so modifying the view may modify the original object. On the other hand, a &quot;copy&quot; is a replication of data from the original, and modifying the copy has no effect on the original.</p>\n<p>As mentioned by other answers, the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> was created to flag &quot;chained assignment&quot; operations. Consider <code>df</code> in the setup above. Suppose you would like to select all values in column &quot;B&quot; where values in column &quot;A&quot; is &gt; 5. Pandas allows you to do this in different ways, some more correct than others. For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B']\n \n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B']\n\n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>These return the same result, so if you are only reading these values, it makes no difference. So, what is the issue? The problem with chained assignment, is that it is generally difficult to predict whether a view or a copy is returned, <strong>so this largely becomes an issue when you are attempting to assign values back.</strong> To build on the earlier example, consider how this code is executed by the interpreter:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__setitem__((df.A &gt; 5, 'B'), 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>With a single <code>__setitem__</code> call to <code>df</code>. OTOH, consider this code:</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__getitem__(df.A &gt; 5).__setitem__('B&quot;, 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now, depending on whether <code>__getitem__</code> returned a view or a copy, the <code>__setitem__</code> operation <strong>may not work</strong>.</p>\n<p>In general, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.loc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>loc</code></a> for label-based assignment, and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iloc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iloc</code></a> for integer/positional based assignment, as the spec guarantees that they always operate on the original. Additionally, for setting a single cell, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.at.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>at</code></a> and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iat.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iat</code></a>.</p>\n<p>More can be found in the <a href=\"http://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/indexing.html#returning-a-view-versus-a-copy\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">documentation</a>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Note</strong><br />\nAll boolean indexing operations done with <code>loc</code> can also be done with <code>iloc</code>. The only difference is that <code>iloc</code> expects either\nintegers/positions for index or a numpy array of boolean values, and\ninteger/position indexes for the columns.</p>\n<p>For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written nas</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[(df.A &gt; 5).values, 1] = 4\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'A'] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>Can be written as</p>\n<pre><code>df.iloc[1, 0] = 100\n</code></pre>\n<p>And so on.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<p>#Just tell me how to suppress the warning!</p>\n<p>Consider a simple operation on the &quot;A&quot; column of <code>df</code>. Selecting &quot;A&quot; and dividing by 2 will raise the warning, but the operation will work.</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']]\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n/Library/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.6/lib/python3.6/site-packages/IPython/__main__.py:1: SettingWithCopyWarning: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n\ndf2\n     A\n0  2.5\n1  4.5\n2  3.5\n</code></pre>\n<p>There are a few ways of silencing this warning, shown below:</p>\n<p>###Make a <code>(deep)copy</code></p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']].copy(deep=True)\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<p>###Set <code>is_copy=False</code>\nTurn off the check for that particular DataFrame by setting <code>is_copy=False</code>.</p>\n<p>&lt;!- -&gt;</p>\n<pre><code>df2.is_copy = False\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<p>###Change <code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment</code>\nCan be set to <code>None</code>, <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code>, or <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code>. <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code> is the default. <code>None</code> will suppress the warning entirely, and <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code> will throw a <code>SettingWithCopyError</code>, preventing the operation from going through.</p>\n<pre><code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = None\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/20625582/how-to-deal-with-settingwithcopywarning-in-pandas#comment62589711_20625582\">@Peter Cotton</a> came up with a nice way of non-intrusively changing the mode (see <a href=\"https://gist.github.com/notbanker/2be3ed34539c86e22ffdd88fd95ad8bc\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this gist</a>) using a context manager, to set the mode only as long as it is required, and the reset it back to the original state when finished.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>class ChainedAssignent:\n    def __init__(self, chained=None):\n        acceptable = [ None, 'warn','raise']\n        assert chained in acceptable, &quot;chained must be in &quot; + str(acceptable)\n        self.swcw = chained\n\n    def __enter__( self ):\n        self.saved_swcw = pd.options.mode.chained_assignment\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.swcw\n        return self\n\n    def __exit__(self, *args):\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.saved_swcw\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The usage is as follows:</p>\n<pre><code># some code here\nwith ChainedAssignent():\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n# more code follows\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or, to raise the exception</p>\n<pre><code>with ChainedAssignent(chained='raise'):\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n\nSettingWithCopyError: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#The &quot;XY Problem&quot;: What am I doing wrong?</p>\n<p>A lot of the time, users attempt to look for ways of suppressing this exception without fully understanding why it was raised in the first place. This is a good example of an <a href=\"https://meta.stackexchange.com/q/66377\">XY problem</a>, where users attempt to solve a problem &quot;Y&quot; that is actually a symptom of a deeper rooted problem &quot;X&quot;. Questions will be raised based on common problems that encounter this warning, and solutions will then be presented.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 1</strong><br />\nI have a DataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>df\n       A  B  C  D  E\n    0  5  0  3  3  7\n    1  9  3  5  2  4\n    2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I want to assign values in col &quot;A&quot; &gt; 5 to 1000. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>      A  B  C  D  E\n0     5  0  3  3  7\n1  1000  3  5  2  4\n2  1000  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Wrong way to do this:</p>\n<pre><code>df.A[df.A &gt; 5] = 1000         # works, because df.A returns a view\ndf[df.A &gt; 5]['A'] = 1000      # does not work\ndf.loc[df.A  5]['A'] = 1000   # does not work\n</code></pre>\n<p>Right way using <code>loc</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'A'] = 1000\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 2</strong><br />\n<sub>This question isn't specifically related to the warning, but it is good to understand how to do this particular\noperation correctly so as to avoid situations where the warning could\npotentially arise in future.</sub> <br/><br />\nI am trying to set the value in cell (1, 'D') to 12345. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C      D  E\n0  5  0  3      3  7\n1  9  3  5  12345  4\n2  7  6  8      8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I have tried different ways of accessing this cell, such as\n<code>df['D'][1]</code>. What is the best way to do this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You can use any of the following methods to do this.</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iloc[1, 3] = 12345\ndf.at[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iat[1, 3] = 12345\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 3</strong><br />\nI am trying to subset values based on some condition. I have a\nDataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I would like to assign values in &quot;D&quot; to 123 such that &quot;C&quot; == 5. I\ntried</p>\n<pre><code>df2.loc[df2.C == 5, 'D'] = 123\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which seems fine but I am <em>still</em> getting the\n<code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>! How do I fix this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is actually probably because of code higher up in your pipeline. Did you create <code>df2</code> from something larger, like</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>? In this case, boolean indexing will return a view, so <code>df2</code> will reference the original. What you'd need to do is assign <code>df2</code> to a <em>copy</em>:</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5].copy()\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or,</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, :]\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 4</strong><br />\nI'm trying to drop to drop column &quot;C&quot; in-place from</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>But using</p>\n<pre><code>df2.drop('C', axis=1, inplace=True)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Throws <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>. Why is this happening?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is because <code>df2</code> must have been created as a view from some other slicing operation, such as</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>The solution here is to either make a <code>copy()</code> of <code>df</code>, or use <code>loc</code>, as before.</p>\n","comment":"added 525 characters in body","revision_guid":"76CE7AD9-3120-4695-B2C0-E8D9A4C92C9A"},{"user":{"account_id":6321039,"reputation":407685,"user_id":4909087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Zcszo.png?s=256","display_name":"coldspeed95","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/4909087/coldspeed95"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1546059502,"post_id":53954986,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":6,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>This post is meant for readers who,</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Would like to understand what this warning means</li>\n<li>Would like to understand different ways of suppressing this warning</li>\n<li>Would like to understand how to improve their code and follow good practices to avoid this warning in the future.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Setup</strong></p>\n<pre><code>np.random.seed(0)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(np.random.choice(10, (3, 5)), columns=list('ABCDE'))\ndf\n   A  B  C  D  E\n0  5  0  3  3  7\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#What is the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>?</p>\n<p>To know how to deal with this warning, it is important to understand what it means and why it is raised in the first place.</p>\n<p>When filtering DataFrames, it is possible slice/index a frame to return either a <em>view</em>, or a <em>copy</em>, depending on the internal layout and various implementation details. A &quot;view&quot; is, as the term suggests, a view into the original data, so modifying the view may modify the original object. On the other hand, a &quot;copy&quot; is a replication of data from the original, and modifying the copy has no effect on the original.</p>\n<p>As mentioned by other answers, the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> was created to flag &quot;chained assignment&quot; operations. Consider <code>df</code> in the setup above. Suppose you would like to select all values in column &quot;B&quot; where values in column &quot;A&quot; is &gt; 5. Pandas allows you to do this in different ways, some more correct than others. For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B']\n \n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B']\n\n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>These return the same result, so if you are only reading these values, it makes no difference. So, what is the issue? The problem with chained assignment, is that it is generally difficult to predict whether a view or a copy is returned, <strong>so this largely becomes an issue when you are attempting to assign values back.</strong> To build on the earlier example, consider how this code is executed by the interpreter:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__setitem__((df.A &gt; 5, 'B'), 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>With a single <code>__setitem__</code> call to <code>df</code>. OTOH, consider this code:</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__getitem__(df.A &gt; 5).__setitem__('B&quot;, 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now, depending on whether <code>__getitem__</code> returned a view or a copy, the <code>__setitem__</code> operation <strong>may not work</strong>.</p>\n<p>In general, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.loc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>loc</code></a> for label-based assignment, and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iloc.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iloc</code></a> for integer/positional based assignment, as the spec guarantees that they always operate on the original. Additionally, for setting a single cell, you should use <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.at.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>at</code></a> and <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/generated/pandas.DataFrame.iat.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>iat</code></a>.</p>\n<p>More can be found in the <a href=\"http://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/indexing.html#returning-a-view-versus-a-copy\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">documentation</a>.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>#Just tell me how to suppress the warning!</p>\n<p>Consider a simple operation on the &quot;A&quot; column of <code>df</code>. Selecting &quot;A&quot; and dividing by 2 will raise the warning, but the operation will work.</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']]\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n/Library/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.6/lib/python3.6/site-packages/IPython/__main__.py:1: SettingWithCopyWarning: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n\ndf2\n     A\n0  2.5\n1  4.5\n2  3.5\n</code></pre>\n<p>There are a few ways of silencing this warning, shown below:</p>\n<p>###Make a <code>(deep)copy</code></p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']].copy(deep=True)\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<p>###Set <code>is_copy=False</code>\nTurn off the check for that particular DataFrame by setting <code>is_copy=False</code>.</p>\n<p>&lt;!- -&gt;</p>\n<pre><code>df2.is_copy = False\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<p>###Change <code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment</code>\nCan be set to <code>None</code>, <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code>, or <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code>. <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code> is the default. <code>None</code> will suppress the warning entirely, and <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code> will throw a <code>SettingWithCopyError</code>, preventing the operation from going through.</p>\n<pre><code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = None\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/20625582/how-to-deal-with-settingwithcopywarning-in-pandas#comment62589711_20625582\">@Peter Cotton</a> came up with a nice way of non-intrusively changing the mode (see <a href=\"https://gist.github.com/notbanker/2be3ed34539c86e22ffdd88fd95ad8bc\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this gist</a>) using a context manager, to set the mode only as long as it is required, and the reset it back to the original state when finished.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>class ChainedAssignent:\n    def __init__(self, chained=None):\n        acceptable = [ None, 'warn','raise']\n        assert chained in acceptable, &quot;chained must be in &quot; + str(acceptable)\n        self.swcw = chained\n\n    def __enter__( self ):\n        self.saved_swcw = pd.options.mode.chained_assignment\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.swcw\n        return self\n\n    def __exit__(self, *args):\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.saved_swcw\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The usage is as follows:</p>\n<pre><code># some code here\nwith ChainedAssignent():\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n# more code follows\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or, to raise the exception</p>\n<pre><code>with ChainedAssignent(chained='raise'):\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n\nSettingWithCopyError: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#The &quot;XY Problem&quot;: What am I doing wrong?</p>\n<p>A lot of the time, users attempt to look for ways of suppressing this exception without fully understanding why it was raised in the first place. This is a good example of an <a href=\"https://meta.stackexchange.com/q/66377\">XY problem</a>, where users attempt to solve a problem &quot;Y&quot; that is actually a symptom of a deeper rooted problem &quot;X&quot;. Questions will be raised based on common problems that encounter this warning, and solutions will then be presented.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 1</strong><br />\nI have a DataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>df\n       A  B  C  D  E\n    0  5  0  3  3  7\n    1  9  3  5  2  4\n    2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I want to assign values in col &quot;A&quot; &gt; 5 to 1000. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>      A  B  C  D  E\n0     5  0  3  3  7\n1  1000  3  5  2  4\n2  1000  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Wrong way to do this:</p>\n<pre><code>df.A[df.A &gt; 5] = 1000         # works, because df.A returns a view\ndf[df.A &gt; 5]['A'] = 1000      # does not work\ndf.loc[df.A  5]['A'] = 1000   # does not work\n</code></pre>\n<p>Right way using <code>loc</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'A'] = 1000\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 2</strong><br />\n<sub>This question isn't specifically related to the warning, but it is good to understand how to do this particular\noperation correctly so as to avoid situations where the warning could\npotentially arise in future.</sub> <br/><br />\nI am trying to set the value in cell (1, 'D') to 12345. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C      D  E\n0  5  0  3      3  7\n1  9  3  5  12345  4\n2  7  6  8      8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I have tried different ways of accessing this cell, such as\n<code>df['D'][1]</code>. What is the best way to do this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You can use any of the following methods to do this.</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iloc[1, 3] = 12345\ndf.at[1, 'D'] = 12345\ndf.iat[1, 3] = 12345\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 3</strong><br />\nI am trying to subset values based on some condition. I have a\nDataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I would like to assign values in &quot;D&quot; to 123 such that &quot;C&quot; == 5. I\ntried</p>\n<pre><code>df2.loc[df2.C == 5, 'D'] = 123\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which seems fine but I am <em>still</em> getting the\n<code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>! How do I fix this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is actually probably because of code higher up in your pipeline. Did you create <code>df2</code> from something larger, like</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>? In this case, boolean indexing will return a view, so <code>df2</code> will reference the original. What you'd need to do is assign <code>df2</code> to a <em>copy</em>:</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5].copy()\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or,</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, :]\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 4</strong><br />\nI'm trying to drop to drop column &quot;C&quot; in-place from</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>But using</p>\n<pre><code>df2.drop('C', axis=1, inplace=True)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Throws <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>. Why is this happening?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is because <code>df2</code> must have been created as a view from some other slicing operation, such as</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>The solution here is to either make a <code>copy()</code> of <code>df</code>, or use <code>loc</code>, as before.</p>\n","comment":"added 1185 characters in body","revision_guid":"36DF01E1-21CD-4525-BDD3-91196553C093"},{"user":{"account_id":6321039,"reputation":407685,"user_id":4909087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Zcszo.png?s=256","display_name":"coldspeed95","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/4909087/coldspeed95"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1546022657,"post_id":53954986,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":5,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>This post is meant for readers who,</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Would like to understand what this warning means</li>\n<li>Would like to understand different ways of suppressing this warning</li>\n<li>Would like to understand how to improve their code and follow good practices to avoid this warning in the future.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Setup</strong></p>\n<pre><code>np.random.seed(0)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(np.random.choice(10, (3, 5)), columns=list('ABCDE'))\ndf\n   A  B  C  D  E\n0  5  0  3  3  7\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#What is the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>?</p>\n<p>To know how to deal with this warning, it is important to understand what it means and why it is raised in the first place.</p>\n<p>When filtering DataFrames, it is possible slice/index a frame to return either a <em>view</em>, or a <em>copy</em>, depending on the internal layout and various implementation details. A &quot;view&quot; is, as the term suggests, a view into the original data, so modifying the view may modify the original object. On the other hand, a &quot;copy&quot; is a replication of data from the original, and modifying the copy has no effect on the original.</p>\n<p>As mentioned by other answers, the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> was created to flag &quot;chained assignment&quot; operations. Consider <code>df</code> in the setup above. Suppose you would like to select all values in column &quot;B&quot; where values in column &quot;A&quot; is &gt; 5. Pandas allows you to do this in different ways, some more correct than others. For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B']\n \n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B']\n\n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>These return the same result, so if you are only reading these values, it makes no difference. So, what is the issue? The problem with chained assignment, is that it is generally difficult to predict whether a view or a copy is returned, <strong>so this largely becomes an issue when you are attempting to assign values back.</strong> To build on the earlier example, consider how this code is executed by the interpreter:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__setitem__((df.A &gt; 5, 'B'), 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>With a single <code>__setitem__</code> call to <code>df</code>. OTOH, consider this code:</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__getitem__(df.A &gt; 5).__setitem__('B&quot;, 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now, depending on whether <code>__getitem__</code> returned a view or a copy, the <code>__setitem__</code> operation <strong>may not work</strong>.</p>\n<p>In general, you should use <code>loc</code>/<code>iloc</code> for selection and assignment, as the spec guarantees that they always operate on the original. More can be found in the <a href=\"http://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/indexing.html#returning-a-view-versus-a-copy\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">documentation</a>.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>#Just tell me how to suppress the warning!</p>\n<p>Consider a simple operation on the &quot;A&quot; column of <code>df</code>. Selecting &quot;A&quot; and dividing by 2 will raise the warning, but the operation will work.</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']]\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n/Library/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.6/lib/python3.6/site-packages/IPython/__main__.py:1: SettingWithCopyWarning: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n\ndf2\n     A\n0  2.5\n1  4.5\n2  3.5\n</code></pre>\n<p>There are a few ways of silencing this warning, shown below:</p>\n<p>###Make a <code>(deep)copy</code></p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']].copy(deep=True)\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<p>###Set <code>is_copy=False</code>\nTurn off the check for that particular DataFrame by setting <code>is_copy=False</code>.</p>\n<p>&lt;!- -&gt;</p>\n<pre><code>df2.is_copy = False\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<p>###Change <code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment</code>\nCan be set to <code>None</code>, <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code>, or <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code>. <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code> is the default. <code>None</code> will suppress the warning entirely, and <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code> will throw a <code>SettingWithCopyError</code>, preventing the operation from going through.</p>\n<pre><code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = None\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/20625582/how-to-deal-with-settingwithcopywarning-in-pandas#comment62589711_20625582\">@Peter Cotton</a> came up with a nice way of non-intrusively changing the mode (see <a href=\"https://gist.github.com/notbanker/2be3ed34539c86e22ffdd88fd95ad8bc\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this gist</a>) using a context manager, to set the mode only as long as it is required, and the reset it back to the original state when finished.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>class ChainedAssignent:\n    def __init__(self, chained=None):\n        acceptable = [ None, 'warn','raise']\n        assert chained in acceptable, &quot;chained must be in &quot; + str(acceptable)\n        self.swcw = chained\n\n    def __enter__( self ):\n        self.saved_swcw = pd.options.mode.chained_assignment\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.swcw\n        return self\n\n    def __exit__(self, *args):\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.saved_swcw\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The usage is as follows:</p>\n<pre><code># some code here\nwith ChainedAssignent():\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n# more code follows\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or, to raise the exception</p>\n<pre><code>with ChainedAssignent(chained='raise'):\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n\nSettingWithCopyError: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#The &quot;XY Problem&quot;: What am I doing wrong?</p>\n<p>A lot of the time, users attempt to look for ways of suppressing this exception without fully understanding why it was raised in the first place. This is a good example of an <a href=\"https://meta.stackexchange.com/q/66377\">XY problem</a>, where users attempt to solve a problem &quot;Y&quot; that is actually a symptom of a deeper rooted problem &quot;X&quot;. Questions will be raised based on common problems that encounter this warning, and solutions will then be presented.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 1</strong><br />\nI have a DataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>df\n       A  B  C  D  E\n    0  5  0  3  3  7\n    1  9  3  5  2  4\n    2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I want to assign values in col &quot;A&quot; &gt; 5 to 1000. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>      A  B  C  D  E\n0     5  0  3  3  7\n1  1000  3  5  2  4\n2  1000  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Wrong way to do this:</p>\n<pre><code>df.A[df.A &gt; 5] = 1000         # works, because df.A returns a view\ndf[df.A &gt; 5]['A'] = 1000      # does not work\ndf.loc[df.A  5]['A'] = 1000   # does not work\n</code></pre>\n<p>Right way using <code>loc</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'A'] = 1000\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 2</strong><br />\nI am trying to subset values based on some condition. I have a\nDataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I would like to assign values in &quot;D&quot; to 123 such that &quot;C&quot; == 5. I\ntried</p>\n<pre><code>df2.loc[df2.C == 5, 'D'] = 123\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which seems fine but I am <em>still</em> getting the\n<code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>! How do I fix this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is actually probably because of code higher up in your pipeline. Did you create <code>df2</code> from something larger, like</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>? In this case, boolean indexing will return a view, so <code>df2</code> will reference the original. What you'd need to do is assign <code>df2</code> to a <em>copy</em>:</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5].copy()\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or,</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, :]\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 3</strong><br />\nI'm trying to drop to drop column &quot;C&quot; in-place from</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>But using</p>\n<pre><code>df2.drop('C', axis=1, inplace=True)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Throws <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>. Why is this happening?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is because <code>df2</code> must have been created as a view from some other slicing operation, such as</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>The solution here is to either make a <code>copy()</code> of <code>df</code>, or use <code>loc</code>, as before.</p>\n","comment":"added 30 characters in body","revision_guid":"32CEF09B-050C-4E01-B114-9EE454A02526"},{"user":{"account_id":6321039,"reputation":407685,"user_id":4909087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Zcszo.png?s=256","display_name":"coldspeed95","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/4909087/coldspeed95"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1545983244,"post_id":53954986,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":4,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>Are you reading this because</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You want to understand what this warning is, or</li>\n<li>You want to understand the best way to deal with this warning, and</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The answers above did not solve your problem? If so, then this post is exactly for you. Keep reading.</p>\n<p><strong>Setup</strong></p>\n<pre><code>np.random.seed(0)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(np.random.choice(10, (3, 5)), columns=list('ABCDE'))\ndf\n   A  B  C  D  E\n0  5  0  3  3  7\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#What is the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>?</p>\n<p>To know how to deal with this warning, it is important to understand what it means and why it is raised in the first place.</p>\n<p>When filtering DataFrames, it is possible slice/index a frame to return either a <em>view</em>, or a <em>copy</em>, depending on the internal layout and various implementation details. A &quot;view&quot; is, as the term suggests, a view into the original data, so modifying the view may modify the original object. On the other hand, a &quot;copy&quot; is a replication of data from the original, and modifying the copy has no effect on the original.</p>\n<p>As mentioned by other answers, the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> was created to flag &quot;chained assignment&quot; operations. Consider <code>df</code> in the setup above. Suppose you would like to select all values in column &quot;B&quot; where values in column &quot;A&quot; is &gt; 5. Pandas allows you to do this in different ways, some more correct than others. For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B']\n \n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B']\n\n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>These return the same result, so if you are only reading these values, it makes no difference. So, what is the issue? The problem with chained assignment, is that it is generally difficult to predict whether a view or a copy is returned, <strong>so this largely becomes an issue when you are attempting to assign values back.</strong> To build on the earlier example, consider how this code is executed by the interpreter:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__setitem__((df.A &gt; 5, 'B'), 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>With a single <code>__setitem__</code> call to <code>df</code>. OTOH, consider this code:</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__getitem__(df.A &gt; 5).__setitem__('B&quot;, 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now, depending on whether <code>__getitem__</code> returned a view or a copy, the <code>__setitem__</code> operation <strong>may not work</strong>.</p>\n<p>In general, you should use <code>loc</code>/<code>iloc</code> for selection and assignment, as the spec guarantees that they always operate on the original. More can be found in the <a href=\"http://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/indexing.html#returning-a-view-versus-a-copy\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">documentation</a>.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>#Just tell me how to suppress the warning!</p>\n<p>Consider a simple operation on the &quot;A&quot; column of <code>df</code>. Selecting &quot;A&quot; and dividing by 2 will raise the warning, but the operation will work.</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']]\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n/Library/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.6/lib/python3.6/site-packages/IPython/__main__.py:1: SettingWithCopyWarning: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n\ndf2\n     A\n0  2.5\n1  4.5\n2  3.5\n</code></pre>\n<p>There are a few ways of silencing this warning, shown below:</p>\n<p>###Make a <code>(deep)copy</code></p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']].copy(deep=True)\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<p>###Set <code>is_copy=False</code>\nTurn off the check for that particular DataFrame by setting <code>is_copy=False</code>.</p>\n<p>&lt;!- -&gt;</p>\n<pre><code>df2.is_copy = False\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<p>###Change <code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment</code>\nCan be set to <code>None</code>, <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code>, or <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code>. <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code> is the default. <code>None</code> will suppress the warning entirely, and <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code> will throw a <code>SettingWithCopyError</code>, preventing the operation from going through.</p>\n<pre><code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = None\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/20625582/how-to-deal-with-settingwithcopywarning-in-pandas#comment62589711_20625582\">@Peter Cotton</a> came up with a nice way of non-intrusively changing the mode (see <a href=\"https://gist.github.com/notbanker/2be3ed34539c86e22ffdd88fd95ad8bc\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this gist</a>) using a context manager, to set the mode only as long as it is required, and the reset it back to the original state when finished.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>class ChainedAssignent:\n    def __init__(self, chained=None):\n        acceptable = [ None, 'warn','raise']\n        assert chained in acceptable, &quot;chained must be in &quot; + str(acceptable)\n        self.swcw = chained\n\n    def __enter__( self ):\n        self.saved_swcw = pd.options.mode.chained_assignment\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.swcw\n        return self\n\n    def __exit__(self, *args):\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.saved_swcw\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The usage is as follows:</p>\n<pre><code># some code here\nwith ChainedAssignent():\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n# more code follows\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or, to raise the exception</p>\n<pre><code>with ChainedAssignent(chained='raise'):\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n\nSettingWithCopyError: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#The &quot;XY Problem&quot;: What am I doing wrong?</p>\n<p>A lot of the time, users attempt to look for ways of suppressing this exception without fully understanding why it was raised in the first place. This is a good example of an <a href=\"https://meta.stackexchange.com/q/66377\">XY problem</a>, where users attempt to solve a problem &quot;Y&quot; that is actually a symptom of a deeper rooted problem &quot;X&quot;. Questions will be raised based on common problems that encounter this warning, and solutions will then be presented.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 1</strong><br />\nI have a DataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>df\n       A  B  C  D  E\n    0  5  0  3  3  7\n    1  9  3  5  2  4\n    2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I want to assign values in col &quot;A&quot; &gt; 5 to 1000. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>      A  B  C  D  E\n0     5  0  3  3  7\n1  1000  3  5  2  4\n2  1000  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Wrong way to do this:</p>\n<pre><code>df.A[df.A &gt; 5] = 1000         # works, because df.A returns a view\ndf[df.A &gt; 5]['A'] = 1000      # does not work\ndf.loc[df.A  5]['A'] = 1000   # does not work\n</code></pre>\n<p>Right way using <code>loc</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'A'] = 1000\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 2</strong><br />\nI am trying to subset values based on some condition. I have a\nDataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I would like to assign values in &quot;D&quot; to 123 such that &quot;C&quot; == 5. I\ntried</p>\n<pre><code>df2.loc[df2.C == 5, 'D'] = 123\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which seems fine but I am <em>still</em> getting the\n<code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>! How do I fix this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is actually probably because of code higher up in your pipeline. Did you create <code>df2</code> from something larger, like</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>? In this case, boolean indexing will return a view, so <code>df2</code> will reference the original. What you'd need to do is assign <code>df2</code> to a <em>copy</em>:</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5].copy()\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or,</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, :]\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 3</strong><br />\nI'm trying to drop to drop column &quot;C&quot; in-place from</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>But using</p>\n<pre><code>df2.drop('C', axis=1, inplace=True)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Throws <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>. Why is this happening?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is because <code>df2</code> must have been created as a view from some other slicing operation, such as</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>The solution here is to either make a <code>copy()</code> of <code>df</code>, or use <code>loc</code>, as before.</p>\n","comment":"added 498 characters in body","revision_guid":"7F44F03B-ADC2-42A0-8DFD-A92349089907"},{"user":{"account_id":6321039,"reputation":407685,"user_id":4909087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Zcszo.png?s=256","display_name":"coldspeed95","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/4909087/coldspeed95"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1545982699,"post_id":53954986,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":3,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>Are you reading this because</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You want to understand what this warning is, or</li>\n<li>You want to understand the best way to deal with this warning, and</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The answers above did not solve your problem? If so, then this post is exactly for you. Keep reading.</p>\n<p><strong>Setup</strong></p>\n<pre><code>np.random.seed(0)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(np.random.choice(10, (3, 5)), columns=list('ABCDE'))\ndf\n   A  B  C  D  E\n0  5  0  3  3  7\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#What is the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>?</p>\n<p>To know how to deal with this warning, it is important to understand what it means and why it is raised in the first place.</p>\n<p>When filtering DataFrames, it is possible slice/index a frame to return either a <em>view</em>, or a <em>copy</em>, depending on the internal layout and various implementation details. A &quot;view&quot; is, as the term suggests, a view into the original data, so modifying the view may modify the original object. On the other hand, a &quot;copy&quot; is a replication of data from the original, and modifying the copy has no effect on the original.</p>\n<p>As mentioned by other answers, the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> was created to flag &quot;chained assignment&quot; operations. Consider <code>df</code> in the setup above. Suppose you would like to select all values in column &quot;B&quot; where values in column &quot;A&quot; is &gt; 5. Pandas allows you to do this in different ways, some more correct than others. For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B']\n \n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B']\n\n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>These return the same result, so if you are only reading these values, it makes no difference. So, what is the issue? The problem with chained assignment, is that it is generally difficult to predict whether a view or a copy is returned, <strong>so this largely becomes an issue when you are attempting to assign values back.</strong> To build on the earlier example, consider how this code is executed by the interpreter:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__setitem__((df.A &gt; 5, 'B'), 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>With a single <code>__setitem__</code> call to <code>df</code>. OTOH, consider this code:</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__getitem__(df.A &gt; 5).__setitem__('B&quot;, 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now, depending on whether <code>__getitem__</code> returned a view or a copy, the <code>__setitem__</code> operation <strong>may not work</strong>.</p>\n<p>In general, you should use <code>loc</code>/<code>iloc</code> for selection and assignment, as the spec guarantees that they always operate on the original. More can be found in the <a href=\"http://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/indexing.html#returning-a-view-versus-a-copy\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">documentation</a>.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>#Just tell me how to suppress the warning!</p>\n<p>Consider a simple operation on the &quot;A&quot; column of <code>df</code>. Selecting &quot;A&quot; and dividing by 2 will raise the warning, but the operation will work.</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']]\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n/Library/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.6/lib/python3.6/site-packages/IPython/__main__.py:1: SettingWithCopyWarning: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n\ndf2\n     A\n0  2.5\n1  4.5\n2  3.5\n</code></pre>\n<p>There are a few ways of silencing this warning, shown below:</p>\n<p>###Make a <code>(deep)copy</code></p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']].copy(deep=True)\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<p>###Set <code>is_copy=False</code>\nTurn off the check for that particular DataFrame by setting <code>is_copy=False</code>.</p>\n<p>&lt;!- -&gt;</p>\n<pre><code>df2.is_copy = False\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<p>###Change <code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment</code>\nCan be set to <code>None</code>, <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code>, or <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code>. <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code> is the default. <code>None</code> will suppress the warning entirely, and <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code> will throw a <code>SettingWithCopyError</code>, preventing the operation from going through.</p>\n<pre><code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = None\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/20625582/how-to-deal-with-settingwithcopywarning-in-pandas#comment62589711_20625582\">@Peter Cotton</a> came up with a nice way of non-intrusively changing the mode (see <a href=\"https://gist.github.com/notbanker/2be3ed34539c86e22ffdd88fd95ad8bc\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this gist</a>) using a context manager, to set the mode only as long as it is required, and the reset it back to the original state when finished.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>class ChainedAssignent:\n    def __init__(self, chained=None):\n        acceptable = [ None, 'warn','raise']\n        assert chained in acceptable, &quot;chained must be in &quot; + str(acceptable)\n        self.swcw = chained\n\n    def __enter__( self ):\n        self.saved_swcw = pd.options.mode.chained_assignment\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.swcw\n        return self\n\n    def __exit__(self, *args):\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.saved_swcw\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The usage is as follows:</p>\n<pre><code># some code here\nwith ChainedAssignent():\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n# more code follows\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or, to raise the exception</p>\n<pre><code>with ChainedAssignent(chained='raise'):\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n\nSettingWithCopyError: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#The &quot;XY Problem&quot;: What am I doing wrong?</p>\n<p>A lot of the time, users attempt to look for ways of suppressing this exception without fully understanding why it was raised in the first place. This is a good example of an <a href=\"https://meta.stackexchange.com/q/66377\">XY problem</a>, where users attempt to solve a problem &quot;Y&quot; that is actually a symptom of a deeper rooted problem &quot;X&quot;. Questions will be raised based on common problems that encounter this warning, and solutions will then be presented.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 1</strong><br />\nI have a DataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>df\n       A  B  C  D  E\n    0  5  0  3  3  7\n    1  9  3  5  2  4\n    2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I want to assign values in col &quot;A&quot; &gt; 5 to 1000. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>      A  B  C  D  E\n0     5  0  3  3  7\n1  1000  3  5  2  4\n2  1000  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Wrong way to do this:</p>\n<pre><code>df.A[df.A &gt; 5] = 1000         # works, because df.A returns a view\ndf[df.A &gt; 5]['A'] = 1000      # does not work\ndf.loc[df.A  5]['A'] = 1000   # does not work\n</code></pre>\n<p>Right way using <code>loc</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'A'] = 1000\n</code></pre>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 2</strong><br />\nI am trying to subset values based on some condition. I have a\nDataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I would like to assign values in &quot;D&quot; to 123 such that &quot;C&quot; == 5. I\ntried</p>\n<pre><code>df2.loc[df2.C == 5, 'D'] = 123\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which seems fine but I am <em>still</em> getting the\n<code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>! How do I fix this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is actually probably because of code higher up in your pipeline. Did you create <code>df2</code> from something larger, like</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>? In this case, boolean indexing will return a view, so <code>df2</code> will reference the original. What you'd need to do is assign <code>df2</code> to a <em>copy</em>:</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5].copy()\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or,</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, :]\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"added 2 characters in body","revision_guid":"7FA3BF9E-E456-4BED-812D-FA1E5FC9AA6F"},{"user":{"account_id":6321039,"reputation":407685,"user_id":4909087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Zcszo.png?s=256","display_name":"coldspeed95","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/4909087/coldspeed95"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1545982168,"post_id":53954986,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":2,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>Are you reading this because</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You want to understand what this warning is, or</li>\n<li>You want to understand the best way to deal with this warning, and</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The answers above did not solve your problem? If so, then this post is exactly for you. Keep reading.</p>\n<p><strong>Setup</strong></p>\n<pre><code>np.random.seed(0)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(np.random.choice(10, (3, 5)), columns=list('ABCDE'))\ndf\n   A  B  C  D  E\n0  5  0  3  3  7\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#What is the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>?</p>\n<p>To know how to deal with this warning, it is important to understand what it means and why it is raised in the first place.</p>\n<p>When filtering DataFrames, it is possible slice/index a frame to return either a <em>view</em>, or a <em>copy</em>, depending on the internal layout and various implementation details. A &quot;view&quot; is, as the term suggests, a view into the original data, so modifying the view may modify the original object. On the other hand, a &quot;copy&quot; is a replication of data from the original, and modifying the copy has no effect on the original.</p>\n<p>As mentioned by other answers, the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> was created to flag &quot;chained assignment&quot; operations. Consider <code>df</code> in the setup above. Suppose you would like to select all values in column &quot;B&quot; where values in column &quot;A&quot; is &gt; 5. Pandas allows you to do this in different ways, some more correct than others. For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B']\n \n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B']\n\n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>These return the same result, so if you are only reading these values, it makes no difference. So, what is the issue? The problem with chained assignment, is that it is generally difficult to predict whether a view or a copy is returned, <strong>so this largely becomes an issue when you are attempting to assign values back.</strong> To build on the earlier example, consider how this code is executed by the interpreter:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__setitem__((df.A &gt; 5, 'B'), 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>With a single <code>__setitem__</code> call to <code>df</code>. OTOH, consider this code:</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__getitem__(df.A &gt; 5).__setitem__('B&quot;, 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now, depending on whether <code>__getitem__</code> returned a view or a copy, the <code>__setitem__</code> operation <strong>may not work</strong>.</p>\n<p>In general, you should use <code>loc</code>/<code>iloc</code> for selection and assignment, as the spec guarantees that they always operate on the original. More can be found in the <a href=\"http://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/indexing.html#returning-a-view-versus-a-copy\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">documentation</a>.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>#Just tell me how to suppress the warning!</p>\n<p>Consider a simple operation on the &quot;A&quot; column of <code>df</code>. Selecting &quot;A&quot; and dividing by 2 will raise the warning, but the operation will work.</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']]\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n/Library/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.6/lib/python3.6/site-packages/IPython/__main__.py:1: SettingWithCopyWarning: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n\ndf2\n     A\n0  2.5\n1  4.5\n2  3.5\n</code></pre>\n<p>There are a few ways of silencing this warning, shown below:</p>\n<p>###Make a <code>(deep)copy</code></p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']].copy(deep=True)\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<p>###Set <code>is_copy=False</code>\nTurn off the check for that particular DataFrame by setting <code>is_copy=False</code>.</p>\n<p>&lt;!- -&gt;</p>\n<pre><code>df2.is_copy = False\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<p>###Change <code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment</code>\nCan be set to <code>None</code>, <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code>, or <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code>. <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code> is the default. <code>None</code> will suppress the warning entirely, and <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code> will throw a <code>SettingWithCopyError</code>, preventing the operation from going through.</p>\n<pre><code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = None\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/20625582/how-to-deal-with-settingwithcopywarning-in-pandas#comment62589711_20625582\">@Peter Cotton</a> came up with a nice way of non-intrusively changing the mode (see <a href=\"https://gist.github.com/notbanker/2be3ed34539c86e22ffdd88fd95ad8bc\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this gist</a>) using a context manager, to set the mode only as long as it is required, and the reset it back to the original state when finished.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>class ChainedAssignent:\n    def __init__(self, chained=None):\n        acceptable = [ None, 'warn','raise']\n        assert chained in acceptable, &quot;chained must be in &quot; + str(acceptable)\n        self.swcw = chained\n\n    def __enter__( self ):\n        self.saved_swcw = pd.options.mode.chained_assignment\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.swcw\n        return self\n\n    def __exit__(self, *args):\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.saved_swcw\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The usage is as follows:</p>\n<pre><code># some code here\nwith ChainedAssignent():\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n# more code follows\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or, to raise the exception</p>\n<pre><code>with ChainedAssignent(chained='raise'):\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n\nSettingWithCopyError: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#The &quot;XY Problem&quot;: What am I doing wrong?</p>\n<p>A lot of the time, users attempt to look for ways of suppressing this exception without fully understanding why it was raised in the first place. This is a good example of an <a href=\"https://meta.stackexchange.com/q/66377\">XY problem</a>, where users attempt to solve a problem &quot;Y&quot; that is actually a symptom of a deeper rooted problem &quot;X&quot;. Questions will be raised based on common problems that encounter this warning, and solutions will then be presented.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 1</strong><br />\nI have a DataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>df\n       A  B  C  D  E\n    0  5  0  3  3  7\n    1  9  3  5  2  4\n    2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I want to assign values in col &quot;A&quot; &gt; 5 to 1000. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>      A  B  C  D  E\n0     5  0  3  3  7\n1  1000  3  5  2  4\n2  1000  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Wrong way to do this:</p>\n<pre><code>df.A[df.A &gt; 5] = 1000         # works, because df.A returns a view\ndf[df.A &gt; 5]['A'] = 1000      # does not work\ndf.loc[df.A  5]['A'] = 1000   # does not work\n</code></pre>\n<p>Right way using <code>loc</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'A'] = 1000\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Question 2</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am trying to subset values based on some condition. I have a\nDataFrame</p>\n<pre><code>   A  B  C  D  E\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<p>I would like to assign values in &quot;D&quot; to 123 such that &quot;C&quot; == 5. I\ntried</p>\n<pre><code>df2.loc[df2.C == 5, 'D'] = 123\n</code></pre>\n<p>Which seems fine but I am <em>still</em> getting the\n<code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>! How do I fix this?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is actually probably because of code higher up in your pipeline. Did you create <code>df2</code> from something larger, like</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5]\n</code></pre>\n<p>? In this case, boolean indexing will return a view, so <code>df2</code> will reference the original. What you'd need to do is assign <code>df2</code> to a <em>copy</em>:</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[df.A &gt; 5].copy()\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or,</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, :]\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"added 832 characters in body","revision_guid":"65A400D7-D1D1-4341-BAB6-43552B7D134B"},{"user":{"account_id":6321039,"reputation":407685,"user_id":4909087,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Zcszo.png?s=256","display_name":"coldspeed95","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/4909087/coldspeed95"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1545981535,"post_id":53954986,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":1,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<p>Are you reading this because</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You want to understand what this warning is, or</li>\n<li>You want to understand the best way to deal with this warning, and</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The answers above did not solve your problem? If so, then this post is exactly for you. Keep reading.</p>\n<p><strong>Setup</strong></p>\n<pre><code>np.random.seed(0)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(np.random.choice(10, (3, 5)), columns=list('ABCDE'))\ndf\n   A  B  C  D  E\n0  5  0  3  3  7\n1  9  3  5  2  4\n2  7  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#What is the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>?</p>\n<p>To know how to deal with this warning, it is important to understand what it means and why it is raised in the first place.</p>\n<p>When filtering DataFrames, it is possible slice/index a frame to return either a <em>view</em>, or a <em>copy</em>, depending on the internal layout and various implementation details. A &quot;view&quot; is, as the term suggests, a view into the original data, so modifying the view may modify the original object. On the other hand, a &quot;copy&quot; is a replication of data from the original, and modifying the copy has no effect on the original.</p>\n<p>As mentioned by other answers, the <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> was created to flag &quot;chained assignment&quot; operations. Consider <code>df</code> in the setup above. Suppose you would like to select all values in column &quot;B&quot; where values in column &quot;A&quot; is &gt; 5. Pandas allows you to do this in different ways, some more correct than others. For example,</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B']\n \n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>And,</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B']\n\n1    3\n2    6\nName: B, dtype: int64\n</code></pre>\n<p>These return the same result, so if you are only reading these values, it makes no difference. So, what is the issue? The problem with chained assignment, is that it is generally difficult to predict whether a view or a copy is returned, <strong>so this largely becomes an issue when you are attempting to assign values back.</strong> To build on the earlier example, consider how this code is executed by the interpreter:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__setitem__((df.A &gt; 5, 'B'), 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>With a single <code>__setitem__</code> call to <code>df</code>. OTOH, consider this code:</p>\n<pre><code>df[df.A &gt; 5]['B'] = 4\n# becomes\ndf.__getitem__(df.A &gt; 5).__setitem__('B&quot;, 4)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now, depending on whether <code>__getitem__</code> returned a view or a copy, the <code>__setitem__</code> operation <strong>may not work</strong>.</p>\n<p>In general, you should use <code>loc</code>/<code>iloc</code> for selection and assignment, as the spec guarantees that they always operate on the original. More can be found in the <a href=\"http://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/indexing.html#returning-a-view-versus-a-copy\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">documentation</a>.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>#The &quot;XY Problem&quot;: What am I doing wrong?</p>\n<p>A lot of the time, users attempt to look for ways of suppressing this exception without fully understanding why it was raised in the first place. This is a good example of an <a href=\"https://meta.stackexchange.com/q/66377\">XY problem</a>, where users attempt to solve a problem &quot;Y&quot; that is actually a symptom of a deeper rooted problem &quot;X&quot;. Questions will be raised with respect to the sample DataFrame <code>df</code> above. I will then show you incorrect ways of solving the problem, followed by the right way of doing it.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Question 1</strong></p>\n<p>I want to assign values in col &quot;A&quot; &gt; 5 to 1000. My expected output is</p>\n<pre><code>      A  B  C  D  E\n0     5  0  3  3  7\n1  1000  3  5  2  4\n2  1000  6  8  8  1\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Wrong way to do this:</p>\n<pre><code>df.A[df.A &gt; 5] = 1000         # works, because df.A returns a view\ndf[df.A &gt; 5]['A'] = 1000      # does not work\ndf.loc[df.A  5]['A'] = 1000   # does not work\n</code></pre>\n<p>Right way using <code>loc</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>df.loc[df.A &gt; 5, 'A'] = 1000\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>#I know what I'm doing! Just tell me how to suppress the warning!</p>\n<p>Consider a simple operation on the &quot;A&quot; column of <code>df</code>. Selecting &quot;A&quot; and dividing by 2 will raise the warning, but the operation will work.</p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']]\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n/Library/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.6/lib/python3.6/site-packages/IPython/__main__.py:1: SettingWithCopyWarning: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n\ndf2\n     A\n0  2.5\n1  4.5\n2  3.5\n</code></pre>\n<p>There are a few ways of silencing this warning, shown below:</p>\n<p>###Make a <code>(deep)copy</code></p>\n<pre><code>df2 = df[['A']].copy(deep=True)\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<p>###Set <code>is_copy=False</code>\nTurn off the check for that particular DataFrame by setting <code>is_copy=False</code>.</p>\n<p>&lt;!- -&gt;</p>\n<pre><code>df2.is_copy = False\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<p>###Change <code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment</code>\nCan be set to <code>None</code>, <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code>, or <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code>. <code>&quot;warn&quot;</code> is the default. <code>None</code> will suppress the warning entirely, and <code>&quot;raise&quot;</code> will throw a <code>SettingWithCopyError</code>, preventing the operation from going through.</p>\n<pre><code>pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = None\ndf2['A'] /= 2\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/20625582/how-to-deal-with-settingwithcopywarning-in-pandas#comment62589711_20625582\">@Peter Cotton</a> came up with a nice way of non-intrusively changing the mode (see <a href=\"https://gist.github.com/notbanker/2be3ed34539c86e22ffdd88fd95ad8bc\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">this gist</a>) using a context manager, to set the mode only as long as it is required, and the reset it back to the original state when finished.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<pre><code>class ChainedAssignent:\n    def __init__(self, chained=None):\n        acceptable = [ None, 'warn','raise']\n        assert chained in acceptable, &quot;chained must be in &quot; + str(acceptable)\n        self.swcw = chained\n\n    def __enter__( self ):\n        self.saved_swcw = pd.options.mode.chained_assignment\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.swcw\n        return self\n\n    def __exit__(self, *args):\n        pd.options.mode.chained_assignment = self.saved_swcw\n</code></pre>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The usage is as follows:</p>\n<pre><code># some code here\nwith ChainedAssignent():\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n# more code follows\n</code></pre>\n<p>Or, to raise the exception</p>\n<pre><code>with ChainedAssignent(chained='raise'):\n    df2['A'] /= 2\n\nSettingWithCopyError: \nA value is trying to be set on a copy of a slice from a DataFrame.\nTry using .loc[row_indexer,col_indexer] = value instead\n</code></pre>\n","revision_guid":"CC4CEE2A-80AB-4003-BF2F-523148D907E0"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1541729938,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":33,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h2>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h2>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For dictionaries <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>, <code>z</code> becomes a shallowly merged dictionary with values from <code>y</code> replacing those from <code>x</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>In Python 3.5 or greater:</p>\n<pre><code>  z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>In Python 2, (or 3.4 or lower) write a function:</p>\n<pre><code>  def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n      z = x.copy()   # start with x's keys and values\n      z.update(y)    # modifies z with y's keys and values &amp; returns None\n      return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre><code>  z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Explanation</h3>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The desired result is to get a new dictionary (<code>z</code>) with the values merged, and the second dict's values overwriting those from the first.</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A new syntax for this, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a> and <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available as of Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression.</p>\n<p>Note that we can merge in with literal notation as well:</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, you may wish to do this in a backwards compatible way. The classically Pythonic way, available in Python 2 and Python 3.0-3.4, is to do this as a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>In both approaches, <code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<h2>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a <em>single expression</em></h2>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5, or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a <em>single expression</em>, the most performant while correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h2>Critiques of Other Answers</h2>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the formerly accepted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example). Even if your values are hashable, <strong>since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic.</p>\n<p>Here's an example of the usage being <a href=\"https://code.djangoproject.com/attachment/ticket/13357/django-pypy.2.diff\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">remediated in django</a>.</p>\n<p>Dicts are intended to take hashable keys (e.g. frozensets or tuples), but <strong>this method fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is my understanding (as well as the understanding of the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099485.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">creator of the language</a>) that the intended usage for <code>dict(**y)</code> is for creating dicts for readability purposes, e.g.:</p>\n<pre><code>dict(a=1, b=10, c=11)\n</code></pre>\n<p>instead of</p>\n<pre><code>{'a': 1, 'b': 10, 'c': 11}\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Response to comments</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Despite what Guido says, <code>dict(x, **y)</code> is in line with the dict specification, which btw. works for both Python 2 and 3. The fact that this only works for string keys is a direct consequence of how keyword parameters work and not a short-comming of dict. Nor is using the ** operator in this place an abuse of the mechanism, in fact ** was designed precisely to pass dicts as keywords.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Again, it doesn't work for 3 when keys are non-strings. The implicit calling contract is that namespaces take ordinary dicts, while users must only pass keyword arguments that are strings. All other callables enforced it. <code>dict</code> broke this consistency in Python 2:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; foo(**{('a', 'b'): None})\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: foo() keywords must be strings\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(**{('a', 'b'): None})\n{('a', 'b'): None}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This inconsistency was bad given other implementations of Python (Pypy, Jython, IronPython). Thus it was fixed in Python 3, as this usage could be a breaking change.</p>\n<p>I submit to you that it is malicious incompetence to intentionally write code that only works in one version of a language or that only works given certain arbitrary constraints.</p>\n<p>More comments:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>dict(x.items() + y.items())</code> is still the most readable solution for Python 2. Readability counts.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>My response: <code>merge_two_dicts(x, y)</code> actually seems much clearer to me, if we're actually concerned about readability. And it is not forward compatible, as Python 2 is increasingly deprecated.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>{**x, **y}</code> does not seem to handle nested dictionaries. the contents of nested keys are simply overwritten, not merged [...] I ended up being burnt by these answers that do not merge recursively and I was surprised no one mentioned it. In my interpretation of the word &quot;merging&quot; these answers describe &quot;updating one dict with another&quot;, and not merging.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes. I must refer you back to the question, which is asking for a <em>shallow</em> merge of <em><strong>two</strong></em> dictionaries, with the first's values being overwritten by the second's - in a single expression.</p>\n<p>Assuming two dictionary of dictionaries, one might recursively merge them in a single function, but you should be careful not to modify the dicts from either source, and the surest way to avoid that is to make a copy when assigning values. As keys must be hashable and are usually therefore immutable, it is pointless to copy them:</p>\n<pre><code>from copy import deepcopy\n\ndef dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y):\n    z = {}\n    overlapping_keys = x.keys() &amp; y.keys()\n    for key in overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = dict_of_dicts_merge(x[key], y[key])\n    for key in x.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(x[key])\n    for key in y.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(y[key])\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>Usage:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a':{1:{}}, 'b': {2:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b':{10:{}}, 'c': {11:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y)\n{'b': {2: {}, 10: {}}, 'a': {1: {}}, 'c': {11: {}}}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Coming up with contingencies for other value types is far beyond the scope of this question, so I will point you at <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/a/24088493/541136\">my answer to the canonical question on a &quot;Dictionaries of dictionaries merge&quot;</a>.</p>\n<h2>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h2>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dicts manually inside a dict comprehension:</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre><code>import itertools\nz = dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Performance Analysis</h2>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly.</p>\n<pre><code>import timeit\n</code></pre>\n<p>The following is done on Ubuntu 14.04</p>\n<p>In Python 2.7 (system Python):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.5726828575134277\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.163769006729126\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))))\n1.1614501476287842\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n2.2345519065856934\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.5 (deadsnakes PPA):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n0.4094954460160807\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.7881555100320838\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.4525277839857154\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.3143140770262107\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n3.2069112799945287\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Resources on Dictionaries</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/327311/how-are-pythons-built-in-dictionaries-implemented/44509302#44509302\">My explanation of Python's <strong>dictionary implementation</strong>, updated for 3.6.</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1024847/add-new-keys-to-a-dictionary/27208535#27208535\">Answer on how to add new keys to a dictionary</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/209840/map-two-lists-into-a-dictionary-in-python/33737067#33737067\">Mapping two lists into a dictionary</a></li>\n<li>The official Python <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#dictionaries\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">docs on dictionaries</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=66P5FMkWoVU\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The Dictionary Even Mightier</a> - talk by Brandon Rhodes at Pycon 2017</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=npw4s1QTmPg\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Modern Python Dictionaries, A Confluence of Great Ideas</a> - talk by Raymond Hettinger at Pycon 2017</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"deleted 6 characters in body","revision_guid":"685929FA-9CE7-4419-AFD8-3BE55375228F"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1541728642,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":32,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h2>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h2>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For dictionaries <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>, <code>z</code> becomes a shallowly merged dictionary with values from <code>y</code> replacing those from <code>x</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>In Python 3.5 or greater:</p>\n<pre><code>  z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>In Python 2, (or 3.4 or lower) write a function:</p>\n<pre><code>  def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n      z = x.copy()   # start with x's keys and values\n      z.update(y)    # modifies z with y's keys and values &amp; returns None\n      return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre><code>  z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Explanation</h3>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The desired result is to get a new dictionary (<code>z</code>) with the values merged, and the second dict's values overwriting those from the first.</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A new syntax for this, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a> and <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available as of Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression.</p>\n<p>Note that we can merge in with literal notation as well:</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>and now:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'foo': 1, 'bar': 2, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, you may wish to do this in a backwards compatible way. The classically Pythonic way, available in Python 2 and Python 3.0-3.4, is to do this as a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>In both approaches, <code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<h2>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a <em>single expression</em></h2>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5, or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a <em>single expression</em>, the most performant while correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h2>Critiques of Other Answers</h2>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the formerly accepted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example). Even if your values are hashable, <strong>since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic.</p>\n<p>Here's an example of the usage being <a href=\"https://code.djangoproject.com/attachment/ticket/13357/django-pypy.2.diff\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">remediated in django</a>.</p>\n<p>Dicts are intended to take hashable keys (e.g. frozensets or tuples), but <strong>this method fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is my understanding (as well as the understanding of the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099485.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">creator of the language</a>) that the intended usage for <code>dict(**y)</code> is for creating dicts for readability purposes, e.g.:</p>\n<pre><code>dict(a=1, b=10, c=11)\n</code></pre>\n<p>instead of</p>\n<pre><code>{'a': 1, 'b': 10, 'c': 11}\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Response to comments</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Despite what Guido says, <code>dict(x, **y)</code> is in line with the dict specification, which btw. works for both Python 2 and 3. The fact that this only works for string keys is a direct consequence of how keyword parameters work and not a short-comming of dict. Nor is using the ** operator in this place an abuse of the mechanism, in fact ** was designed precisely to pass dicts as keywords.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Again, it doesn't work for 3 when keys are non-strings. The implicit calling contract is that namespaces take ordinary dicts, while users must only pass keyword arguments that are strings. All other callables enforced it. <code>dict</code> broke this consistency in Python 2:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; foo(**{('a', 'b'): None})\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: foo() keywords must be strings\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(**{('a', 'b'): None})\n{('a', 'b'): None}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This inconsistency was bad given other implementations of Python (Pypy, Jython, IronPython). Thus it was fixed in Python 3, as this usage could be a breaking change.</p>\n<p>I submit to you that it is malicious incompetence to intentionally write code that only works in one version of a language or that only works given certain arbitrary constraints.</p>\n<p>More comments:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>dict(x.items() + y.items())</code> is still the most readable solution for Python 2. Readability counts.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>My response: <code>merge_two_dicts(x, y)</code> actually seems much clearer to me, if we're actually concerned about readability. And it is not forward compatible, as Python 2 is increasingly deprecated.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>{**x, **y}</code> does not seem to handle nested dictionaries. the contents of nested keys are simply overwritten, not merged [...] I ended up being burnt by these answers that do not merge recursively and I was surprised no one mentioned it. In my interpretation of the word &quot;merging&quot; these answers describe &quot;updating one dict with another&quot;, and not merging.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes. I must refer you back to the question, which is asking for a <em>shallow</em> merge of <em><strong>two</strong></em> dictionaries, with the first's values being overwritten by the second's - in a single expression.</p>\n<p>Assuming two dictionary of dictionaries, one might recursively merge them in a single function, a'la, but you should be careful not to modify the dicts from either source, and the surest way to avoid that is to make a copy when assigning values. As keys must be hashable and are usually therefore immutable, it is pointless to copy them:</p>\n<pre><code>from copy import deepcopy\n\ndef dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y):\n    z = {}\n    overlapping_keys = x.keys() &amp; y.keys()\n    for key in overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = dict_of_dicts_merge(x[key], y[key])\n    for key in x.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(x[key])\n    for key in y.keys() - overlapping_keys:\n        z[key] = deepcopy(y[key])\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>Usage:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a':{1:{}}, 'b': {2:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b':{10:{}}, 'c': {11:{}}}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict_of_dicts_merge(x, y)\n{'b': {2: {}, 10: {}}, 'a': {1: {}}, 'c': {11: {}}}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Coming up with contingencies for other value types is far beyond the scope of this question, so I will point you at <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/a/24088493/541136\">my answer to the canonical question on a &quot;Dictionaries of dictionaries merge&quot;</a>.</p>\n<h2>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h2>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dicts manually inside a dict comprehension:</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre><code>import itertools\nz = dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Performance Analysis</h2>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly.</p>\n<pre><code>import timeit\n</code></pre>\n<p>The following is done on Ubuntu 14.04</p>\n<p>In Python 2.7 (system Python):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.5726828575134277\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.163769006729126\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))))\n1.1614501476287842\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n2.2345519065856934\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.5 (deadsnakes PPA):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n0.4094954460160807\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.7881555100320838\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.4525277839857154\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.3143140770262107\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n3.2069112799945287\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Resources on Dictionaries</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/327311/how-are-pythons-built-in-dictionaries-implemented/44509302#44509302\">My explanation of Python's <strong>dictionary implementation</strong>, updated for 3.6.</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1024847/add-new-keys-to-a-dictionary/27208535#27208535\">Answer on how to add new keys to a dictionary</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/209840/map-two-lists-into-a-dictionary-in-python/33737067#33737067\">Mapping two lists into a dictionary</a></li>\n<li>The official Python <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#dictionaries\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">docs on dictionaries</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=66P5FMkWoVU\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The Dictionary Even Mightier</a> - talk by Brandon Rhodes at Pycon 2017</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=npw4s1QTmPg\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Modern Python Dictionaries, A Confluence of Great Ideas</a> - talk by Raymond Hettinger at Pycon 2017</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"address comments asking about nested dictionaries","revision_guid":"BAEADEEE-243D-4B59-A806-51085A5FD590"},{"user":{"account_id":129736,"reputation":8045,"user_id":328501,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/1ad3d9bbf5b8f4f07519de9970c7423f?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Jesse Dhillon","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/328501/jesse-dhillon"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1530637341,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":31,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h1>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h1>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For dictionaries <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>, <code>z</code> becomes a merged dictionary with values from <code>y</code> replacing those from <code>x</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>In Python 3.5 or greater, :</p>\n<pre><code>  z = {**x, **y}\n  w = {'foo': 'bar', 'baz': 'qux', **y}  # merge a dict with literal values\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>In Python 2, (or 3.4 or lower) write a function:</p>\n<pre><code>  def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n      z = x.copy()   # start with x's keys and values\n      z.update(y)    # modifies z with y's keys and values &amp; returns None\n      return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and</p>\n<pre><code>  z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Explanation</h2>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The desired result is to get a new dictionary (<code>z</code>) with the values merged, and the second dict's values overwriting those from the first.</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A new syntax for this, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a> and <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available as of Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression. It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, you may wish to do this in a backwards compatible way. The classically Pythonic way, available in Python 2 and Python 3.0-3.4, is to do this as a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>In both approaches, <code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<h1>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a <em>single expression</em></h1>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5, or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a <em>single expression</em>, the most performant while correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h1>Critiques of Other Answers</h1>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the formerly accepted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example). Even if your values are hashable, <strong>since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic.</p>\n<p>Here's an example of the usage being <a href=\"https://code.djangoproject.com/attachment/ticket/13357/django-pypy.2.diff\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">remediated in django</a>.</p>\n<p>Dicts are intended to take hashable keys (e.g. frozensets or tuples), but <strong>this method fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is my understanding (as well as the understanding of the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099485.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">creator of the language</a>) that the intended usage for <code>dict(**y)</code> is for creating dicts for readability purposes, e.g.:</p>\n<pre><code>dict(a=1, b=10, c=11)\n</code></pre>\n<p>instead of</p>\n<pre><code>{'a': 1, 'b': 10, 'c': 11}\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Response to comments</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Despite what Guido says, <code>dict(x, **y)</code> is in line with the dict specification, which btw. works for both Python 2 and 3. The fact that this only works for string keys is a direct consequence of how keyword parameters work and not a short-comming of dict. Nor is using the ** operator in this place an abuse of the mechanism, in fact ** was designed precisely to pass dicts as keywords.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Again, it doesn't work for 3 when keys are non-strings. The implicit calling contract is that namespaces take ordinary dicts, while users must only pass keyword arguments that are strings. All other callables enforced it. <code>dict</code> broke this consistency in Python 2:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; foo(**{('a', 'b'): None})\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: foo() keywords must be strings\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(**{('a', 'b'): None})\n{('a', 'b'): None}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This inconsistency was bad given other implementations of Python (Pypy, Jython, IronPython). Thus it was fixed in Python 3, as this usage could be a breaking change.</p>\n<p>I submit to you that it is malicious incompetence to intentionally write code that only works in one version of a language or that only works given certain arbitrary constraints.</p>\n<p>Another comment:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>dict(x.items() + y.items())</code> is still the most readable solution for Python 2. Readability counts.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>My response: <code>merge_two_dicts(x, y)</code> actually seems much clearer to me, if we're actually concerned about readability. And it is not forward compatible, as Python 2 is increasingly deprecated.</p>\n<h1>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h1>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dicts manually inside a dict comprehension:</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre><code>import itertools\nz = dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))\n</code></pre>\n<h1>Performance Analysis</h1>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly.</p>\n<pre><code>import timeit\n</code></pre>\n<p>The following is done on Ubuntu 14.04</p>\n<p>In Python 2.7 (system Python):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.5726828575134277\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.163769006729126\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))))\n1.1614501476287842\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n2.2345519065856934\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.5 (deadsnakes PPA):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n0.4094954460160807\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.7881555100320838\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.4525277839857154\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.3143140770262107\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n3.2069112799945287\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Resources on Dictionaries</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/327311/how-are-pythons-built-in-dictionaries-implemented/44509302#44509302\">My explanation of Python's <strong>dictionary implementation</strong>, updated for 3.6.</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1024847/add-new-keys-to-a-dictionary/27208535#27208535\">Answer on how to add new keys to a dictionary</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/209840/map-two-lists-into-a-dictionary-in-python/33737067#33737067\">Mapping two lists into a dictionary</a></li>\n<li>The official Python <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#dictionaries\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">docs on dictionaries</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=66P5FMkWoVU\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The Dictionary Even Mightier</a> - talk by Brandon Rhodes at Pycon 2017</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=npw4s1QTmPg\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Modern Python Dictionaries, A Confluence of Great Ideas</a> - talk by Raymond Hettinger at Pycon 2017</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"added an example of splatting with a literal dict","revision_guid":"1826D1F2-68A7-48F3-A439-ADA010E83F9C"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1508447798,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":30,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h1>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h1>\n</blockquote>\n<p>For dictionaries <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>, <code>z</code> becomes a merged dictionary with values from <code>y</code> replacing those from <code>x</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>In Python 3.5 or greater, :</p>\n<pre><code>  z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>In Python 2, (or 3.4 or lower) write a function:</p>\n<pre><code>  def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n      z = x.copy()   # start with x's keys and values\n      z.update(y)    # modifies z with y's keys and values &amp; returns None\n      return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and</p>\n<pre><code>  z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Explanation</h2>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The desired result is to get a new dictionary (<code>z</code>) with the values merged, and the second dict's values overwriting those from the first.</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A new syntax for this, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a> and <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available as of Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression. It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, you may wish to do this in a backwards compatible way. The classically Pythonic way, available in Python 2 and Python 3.0-3.4, is to do this as a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>In both approaches, <code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<h1>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a <em>single expression</em></h1>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5, or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a <em>single expression</em>, the most performant while correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h1>Critiques of Other Answers</h1>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the formerly accepted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example). Even if your values are hashable, <strong>since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic.</p>\n<p>Here's an example of the usage being <a href=\"https://code.djangoproject.com/attachment/ticket/13357/django-pypy.2.diff\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">remediated in django</a>.</p>\n<p>Dicts are intended to take hashable keys (e.g. frozensets or tuples), but <strong>this method fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is my understanding (as well as the understanding of the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099485.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">creator of the language</a>) that the intended usage for <code>dict(**y)</code> is for creating dicts for readability purposes, e.g.:</p>\n<pre><code>dict(a=1, b=10, c=11)\n</code></pre>\n<p>instead of</p>\n<pre><code>{'a': 1, 'b': 10, 'c': 11}\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Response to comments</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Despite what Guido says, <code>dict(x, **y)</code> is in line with the dict specification, which btw. works for both Python 2 and 3. The fact that this only works for string keys is a direct consequence of how keyword parameters work and not a short-comming of dict. Nor is using the ** operator in this place an abuse of the mechanism, in fact ** was designed precisely to pass dicts as keywords.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Again, it doesn't work for 3 when keys are non-strings. The implicit calling contract is that namespaces take ordinary dicts, while users must only pass keyword arguments that are strings. All other callables enforced it. <code>dict</code> broke this consistency in Python 2:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; foo(**{('a', 'b'): None})\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: foo() keywords must be strings\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(**{('a', 'b'): None})\n{('a', 'b'): None}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This inconsistency was bad given other implementations of Python (Pypy, Jython, IronPython). Thus it was fixed in Python 3, as this usage could be a breaking change.</p>\n<p>I submit to you that it is malicious incompetence to intentionally write code that only works in one version of a language or that only works given certain arbitrary constraints.</p>\n<p>Another comment:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>dict(x.items() + y.items())</code> is still the most readable solution for Python 2. Readability counts.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>My response: <code>merge_two_dicts(x, y)</code> actually seems much clearer to me, if we're actually concerned about readability. And it is not forward compatible, as Python 2 is increasingly deprecated.</p>\n<h1>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h1>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dicts manually inside a dict comprehension:</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre><code>import itertools\nz = dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))\n</code></pre>\n<h1>Performance Analysis</h1>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly.</p>\n<pre><code>import timeit\n</code></pre>\n<p>The following is done on Ubuntu 14.04</p>\n<p>In Python 2.7 (system Python):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.5726828575134277\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.163769006729126\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))))\n1.1614501476287842\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n2.2345519065856934\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.5 (deadsnakes PPA):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n0.4094954460160807\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.7881555100320838\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.4525277839857154\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.3143140770262107\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n3.2069112799945287\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Resources on Dictionaries</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/327311/how-are-pythons-built-in-dictionaries-implemented/44509302#44509302\">My explanation of Python's <strong>dictionary implementation</strong>, updated for 3.6.</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1024847/add-new-keys-to-a-dictionary/27208535#27208535\">Answer on how to add new keys to a dictionary</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/209840/map-two-lists-into-a-dictionary-in-python/33737067#33737067\">Mapping two lists into a dictionary</a></li>\n<li>The official Python <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#dictionaries\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">docs on dictionaries</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=66P5FMkWoVU\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The Dictionary Even Mightier</a> - talk by Brandon Rhodes at Pycon 2017</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=npw4s1QTmPg\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Modern Python Dictionaries, A Confluence of Great Ideas</a> - talk by Raymond Hettinger at Pycon 2017</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"added 493 characters in body","revision_guid":"D75A049E-0071-41D2-A998-2E13F9D212CE"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1506704504,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":29,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h1>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h1>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The desired result is to get a new dictionary (<code>z</code>) with the values merged, and the second dict's values overwriting those from the first.</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A new syntax for this, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a> and <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available as of Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression. It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, you may wish to do this in a backwards compatible way. The classically Pythonic way, available in Python 2 and Python 3.0-3.4, is to do this as a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>In both approaches, <code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<h1>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a <em>single expression</em></h1>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5, or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a <em>single expression</em>, the most performant while correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h1>Critiques of Other Answers</h1>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the formerly accepted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example). Even if your values are hashable, <strong>since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic.</p>\n<p>Here's an example of the usage being <a href=\"https://code.djangoproject.com/attachment/ticket/13357/django-pypy.2.diff\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">remediated in django</a>.</p>\n<p>Dicts are intended to take hashable keys (e.g. frozensets or tuples), but <strong>this method fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is my understanding (as well as the understanding of the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099485.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">creator of the language</a>) that the intended usage for <code>dict(**y)</code> is for creating dicts for readability purposes, e.g.:</p>\n<pre><code>dict(a=1, b=10, c=11)\n</code></pre>\n<p>instead of</p>\n<pre><code>{'a': 1, 'b': 10, 'c': 11}\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Response to comments</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Despite what Guido says, <code>dict(x, **y)</code> is in line with the dict specification, which btw. works for both Python 2 and 3. The fact that this only works for string keys is a direct consequence of how keyword parameters work and not a short-comming of dict. Nor is using the ** operator in this place an abuse of the mechanism, in fact ** was designed precisely to pass dicts as keywords.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Again, it doesn't work for 3 when keys are non-strings. The implicit calling contract is that namespaces take ordinary dicts, while users must only pass keyword arguments that are strings. All other callables enforced it. <code>dict</code> broke this consistency in Python 2:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; foo(**{('a', 'b'): None})\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: foo() keywords must be strings\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(**{('a', 'b'): None})\n{('a', 'b'): None}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This inconsistency was bad given other implementations of Python (Pypy, Jython, IronPython). Thus it was fixed in Python 3, as this usage could be a breaking change.</p>\n<p>I submit to you that it is malicious incompetence to intentionally write code that only works in one version of a language or that only works given certain arbitrary constraints.</p>\n<p>Another comment:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>dict(x.items() + y.items())</code> is still the most readable solution for Python 2. Readability counts.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>My response: <code>merge_two_dicts(x, y)</code> actually seems much clearer to me, if we're actually concerned about readability. And it is not forward compatible, as Python 2 is increasingly deprecated.</p>\n<h1>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h1>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dicts manually inside a dict comprehension:</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre><code>import itertools\nz = dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))\n</code></pre>\n<h1>Performance Analysis</h1>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly.</p>\n<pre><code>import timeit\n</code></pre>\n<p>The following is done on Ubuntu 14.04</p>\n<p>In Python 2.7 (system Python):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.5726828575134277\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.163769006729126\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))))\n1.1614501476287842\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n2.2345519065856934\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.5 (deadsnakes PPA):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n0.4094954460160807\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.7881555100320838\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.4525277839857154\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.3143140770262107\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n3.2069112799945287\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Resources on Dictionaries</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/327311/how-are-pythons-built-in-dictionaries-implemented/44509302#44509302\">My explanation of Python's <strong>dictionary implementation</strong>, updated for 3.6.</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1024847/add-new-keys-to-a-dictionary/27208535#27208535\">Answer on how to add new keys to a dictionary</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/209840/map-two-lists-into-a-dictionary-in-python/33737067#33737067\">Mapping two lists into a dictionary</a></li>\n<li>The official Python <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#dictionaries\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">docs on dictionaries</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=66P5FMkWoVU\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The Dictionary Even Mightier</a> - talk by Brandon Rhodes at Pycon 2017</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=npw4s1QTmPg\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Modern Python Dictionaries, A Confluence of Great Ideas</a> - talk by Raymond Hettinger at Pycon 2017</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"added 323 characters in body","revision_guid":"A3BAC6AE-A911-4501-AF75-964645963BE3"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1502575539,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":28,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h1>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h1>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The desired result is to get a new dictionary (<code>z</code>) with the values merged, and the second dict's values overwriting those from the first.</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A new syntax for this, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a> and <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available as of Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression. It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, you may wish to do this in a backwards compatible way. The classically Pythonic way, available in Python 2 and Python 3.0-3.4, is to do this as a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>In both approaches, <code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<h1>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a <em>single expression</em></h1>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5, or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a <em>single expression</em>, the most performant while correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h1>Critiques of Other Answers</h1>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the formerly accepted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example). Even if your values are hashable, <strong>since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic.</p>\n<p>Here's an example of the usage being <a href=\"https://code.djangoproject.com/attachment/ticket/13357/django-pypy.2.diff\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">remediated in django</a>.</p>\n<p>Dicts are intended to take hashable keys (e.g. frozensets or tuples), but <strong>this method fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is my understanding (as well as the understanding of the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099485.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">creator of the language</a>) that the intended usage for <code>dict(**y)</code> is for creating dicts for readability purposes, e.g.:</p>\n<pre><code>dict(a=1, b=10, c=11)\n</code></pre>\n<p>instead of</p>\n<pre><code>{'a': 1, 'b': 10, 'c': 11}\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Response to comment</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Despite what Guido says, <code>dict(x, **y)</code> is in line with the dict specification, which btw. works for both Python 2 and 3. The fact that this only works for string keys is a direct consequence of how keyword parameters work and not a short-comming of dict. Nor is using the ** operator in this place an abuse of the mechanism, in fact ** was designed precisely to pass dicts as keywords.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Again, it doesn't work for 3 when keys are non-strings. The implicit calling contract is that namespaces take ordinary dicts, while users must only pass keyword arguments that are strings. All other callables enforced it. <code>dict</code> broke this consistency in Python 2:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; foo(**{('a', 'b'): None})\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: foo() keywords must be strings\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(**{('a', 'b'): None})\n{('a', 'b'): None}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This inconsistency was bad given other implementations of Python (Pypy, Jython, IronPython). Thus it was fixed in Python 3, as this usage could be a breaking change.</p>\n<p>I submit to you that it is malicious incompetence to intentionally write code that only works in one version of a language or that only works given certain arbitrary constraints.</p>\n<h1>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h1>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dicts manually inside a dict comprehension:</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre><code>import itertools\nz = dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))\n</code></pre>\n<h1>Performance Analysis</h1>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly.</p>\n<pre><code>import timeit\n</code></pre>\n<p>The following is done on Ubuntu 14.04</p>\n<p>In Python 2.7 (system Python):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.5726828575134277\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.163769006729126\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))))\n1.1614501476287842\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n2.2345519065856934\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.5 (deadsnakes PPA):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n0.4094954460160807\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.7881555100320838\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.4525277839857154\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.3143140770262107\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n3.2069112799945287\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Resources on Dictionaries</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/327311/how-are-pythons-built-in-dictionaries-implemented/44509302#44509302\">My explanation of Python's <strong>dictionary implementation</strong>, updated for 3.6.</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1024847/add-new-keys-to-a-dictionary/27208535#27208535\">Answer on how to add new keys to a dictionary</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/209840/map-two-lists-into-a-dictionary-in-python/33737067#33737067\">Mapping two lists into a dictionary</a></li>\n<li>The official Python <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#dictionaries\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">docs on dictionaries</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=66P5FMkWoVU\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The Dictionary Even Mightier</a> - talk by Brandon Rhodes at Pycon 2017</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=npw4s1QTmPg\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Modern Python Dictionaries, A Confluence of Great Ideas</a> - talk by Raymond Hettinger at Pycon 2017</li>\n</ul>\n","comment":"added 964 characters in body","revision_guid":"10A832CA-2237-486E-B913-F6CD5B5C6284"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1492283587,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":27,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h1>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h1>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The desired result is to get a new dictionary (<code>z</code>) with the values merged, and the second dict's values overwriting those from the first.</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A new syntax for this, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a> and <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available as of Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression. It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, you may wish to do this in a backwards compatible way. The classically Pythonic way, available in Python 2 and Python 3.0-3.4, is to do this as a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>In both approaches, <code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<h1>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a <em>single expression</em></h1>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5, or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a <em>single expression</em>, the most performant while correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h1>Critiques of Other Answers</h1>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the formerly accepted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example). Even if your values are hashable, <strong>since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic.</p>\n<p>Here's an example of the usage being <a href=\"https://code.djangoproject.com/attachment/ticket/13357/django-pypy.2.diff\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">remediated in django</a>.</p>\n<p>Dicts are intended to take hashable keys (e.g. frozensets or tuples), but <strong>this method fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is my understanding (as well as the understanding of the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099485.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">creator of the language</a>) that the intended usage for <code>dict(**y)</code> is for creating dicts for readability purposes, e.g.:</p>\n<pre><code>dict(a=1, b=10, c=11)\n</code></pre>\n<p>instead of</p>\n<pre><code>{'a': 1, 'b': 10, 'c': 11}\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Response to comment</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Despite what Guido says, <code>dict(x, **y)</code> is in line with the dict specification, which btw. works for both Python 2 and 3. The fact that this only works for string keys is a direct consequence of how keyword parameters work and not a short-comming of dict. Nor is using the ** operator in this place an abuse of the mechanism, in fact ** was designed precisely to pass dicts as keywords.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Again, it doesn't work for 3 when keys are non-strings. The implicit calling contract is that namespaces take ordinary dicts, while users must only pass keyword arguments that are strings. All other callables enforced it. <code>dict</code> broke this consistency in Python 2:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; foo(**{('a', 'b'): None})\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: foo() keywords must be strings\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(**{('a', 'b'): None})\n{('a', 'b'): None}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This inconsistency was bad given other implementations of Python (Pypy, Jython, IronPython). Thus it was fixed in Python 3, as this usage could be a breaking change.</p>\n<p>I submit to you that it is malicious incompetence to intentionally write code that only works in one version of a language or that only works given certain arbitrary constraints.</p>\n<h1>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h1>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dicts manually inside a dict comprehension:</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre><code>import itertools\nz = dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))\n</code></pre>\n<h1>Performance Analysis</h1>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly.</p>\n<pre><code>import timeit\n</code></pre>\n<p>The following is done on Ubuntu 14.04</p>\n<p>In Python 2.7 (system Python):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.5726828575134277\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.163769006729126\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))))\n1.1614501476287842\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n2.2345519065856934\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.5 (deadsnakes PPA):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n0.4094954460160807\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.7881555100320838\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.4525277839857154\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.3143140770262107\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n3.2069112799945287\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"added 147 characters in body","revision_guid":"7D34CDB9-D29A-423A-BBB9-2DCE5D4D0D14"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1492278438,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":26,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h1>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h1>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The desired result is to get a new dictionary (<code>z</code>) with the values merged, and the second dict's values overwriting those from the first.</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A new syntax for this, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a> and <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available as of Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression. It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, you may wish to do this in a backwards compatible way. The classically Pythonic way, available in Python 2 and Python 3.0-3.4, is to do this as a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>In both approaches, <code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<h1>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a <em>single expression</em></h1>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5, or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a <em>single expression</em>, the most performant while correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h1>Critiques of Other Answers</h1>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the formerly accepted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example). Even if your values are hashable, <strong>since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic.</p>\n<p>Dicts are intended to take hashable keys (e.g. frozensets or tuples), but <strong>this method fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is my understanding (as well as the understanding of the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099485.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">creator of the language</a>) that the intended usage for <code>dict(**y)</code> is for creating dicts for readability purposes, e.g.:</p>\n<pre><code>dict(a=1, b=10, c=11)\n</code></pre>\n<p>instead of</p>\n<pre><code>{'a': 1, 'b': 10, 'c': 11}\n</code></pre>\n<h2>Response to comment</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Despite what Guido says, <code>dict(x, **y)</code> is in line with the dict specification, which btw. works for both Python 2 and 3. The fact that this only works for string keys is a direct consequence of how keyword parameters work and not a short-comming of dict. Nor is using the ** operator in this place an abuse of the mechanism, in fact ** was designed precisely to pass dicts as keywords.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Again, it doesn't work for 3 when keys are non-strings. The implicit calling contract is that namespaces take ordinary dicts, while users must only pass keyword arguments that are strings. All other callables enforced it. <code>dict</code> broke this consistency in Python 2:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; foo(**{('a', 'b'): None})\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: foo() keywords must be strings\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(**{('a', 'b'): None})\n{('a', 'b'): None}\n</code></pre>\n<p>This inconsistency was bad given other implementations of Python (Pypy, Jython, IronPython). Thus it was fixed in Python 3, as this usage could be a breaking change.</p>\n<p>I submit to you that it is malicious incompetence to intentionally write code that only works in one version of a language or that only works given certain arbitrary constraints.</p>\n<h1>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h1>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dicts manually inside a dict comprehension:</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre><code>import itertools\nz = dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))\n</code></pre>\n<h1>Performance Analysis</h1>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly.</p>\n<pre><code>import timeit\n</code></pre>\n<p>The following is done on Ubuntu 14.04</p>\n<p>In Python 2.7 (system Python):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.5726828575134277\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.163769006729126\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))))\n1.1614501476287842\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n2.2345519065856934\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.5 (deadsnakes PPA):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n0.4094954460160807\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.7881555100320838\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.4525277839857154\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.3143140770262107\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n3.2069112799945287\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"added 1681 characters in body","revision_guid":"81271F84-EB29-4B7C-84A2-05F37C49D74E"},{"user":{"account_id":1234837,"reputation":604,"user_id":1198642,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/73b7b0229d7c617ad9fafd86307d4a1c?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Markus Schanta","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/1198642/markus-schanta"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1481730365,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":25,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h1>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h1>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The desired result is to get a new dictionary (<code>z</code>) with the values merged, and the second dict's values overwriting those from the first.</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A new syntax for this, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a> and <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available as of Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression. It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, you may wish to do this in a backwards compatible way. The classically Pythonic way, available in Python 2 and Python 3.0-3.4, is to do this as a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>In both approaches, <code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<h1>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a <em>single expression</em></h1>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5, or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a <em>single expression</em>, the most performant while correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h1>Critiques of Other Answers</h1>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the formerly accepted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example). Even if your values are hashable, <strong>since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic. <strong>Also, this fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h1>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h1>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dicts manually inside a dict comprehension:</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre><code>import itertools\nz = dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))\n</code></pre>\n<h1>Performance Analysis</h1>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly.</p>\n<pre><code>import timeit\n</code></pre>\n<p>The following is done on Ubuntu 14.04</p>\n<p>In Python 2.7 (system Python):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.5726828575134277\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.163769006729126\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))))\n1.1614501476287842\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n2.2345519065856934\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.5 (deadsnakes PPA):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n0.4094954460160807\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.7881555100320838\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.4525277839857154\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.3143140770262107\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n3.2069112799945287\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"Use triple double quotes for function docstrings (as per PEP257 https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0257/#what-is-a-docstring)","revision_guid":"59821415-D13E-407D-A184-6E30F9217027"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1480713864,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":24,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h1>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h1>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The desired result is to get a new dictionary (<code>z</code>) with the values merged, and the second dict's values overwriting those from the first.</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A new syntax for this, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a> and <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available as of Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression. It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, you may wish to do this in a backwards compatible way. The classically Pythonic way, available in Python 2 and Python 3.0-3.4, is to do this as a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>In both approaches, <code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<h1>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a <em>single expression</em></h1>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5, or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a <em>single expression</em>, the most performant while correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    '''Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.'''\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    '''\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    '''\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h1>Critiques of Other Answers</h1>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the formerly accepted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example). Even if your values are hashable, <strong>since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic. <strong>Also, this fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h1>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h1>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dicts manually inside a dict comprehension:</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre><code>import itertools\nz = dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))\n</code></pre>\n<h1>Performance Analysis</h1>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly.</p>\n<pre><code>import timeit\n</code></pre>\n<p>The following is done on Ubuntu 14.04</p>\n<p>In Python 2.7 (system Python):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.5726828575134277\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.163769006729126\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))))\n1.1614501476287842\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n2.2345519065856934\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.5 (deadsnakes PPA):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n0.4094954460160807\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.7881555100320838\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.4525277839857154\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.3143140770262107\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n3.2069112799945287\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"added 5 characters in body","revision_guid":"AA4C625D-F8B5-4980-BEC5-413F19282705"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1466944618,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":23,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h1>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h1>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The desired result is to get a new dictionary (<code>z</code>) with the values merged, and the second dict's values overwriting those from the first.</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; z\n{'a': 1, 'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A new syntax for this, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a> and <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available as of Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression. It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, you may wish to do this in a backwards compatible way. The classically Pythonic way, available in Python 2 and Python 3.0-3.4, is to do this as a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>In both approaches, <code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<h1>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a <em>single expression</em></h1>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5, or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a <em>single expression</em>, the most performant while correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    '''Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.'''\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    '''\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    '''\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h1>Critiques of Other Answers</h1>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the most upvoted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example). Even if your values are hashable, <strong>since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic. <strong>Also, this fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h1>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h1>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dicts manually inside a dict comprehension:</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre><code>import itertools\nz = dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))\n</code></pre>\n<h1>Performance Analysis</h1>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly.</p>\n<pre><code>import timeit\n</code></pre>\n<p>The following is done on Ubuntu 14.04</p>\n<p>In Python 2.7 (system Python):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.5726828575134277\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.163769006729126\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))))\n1.1614501476287842\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n2.2345519065856934\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.5 (deadsnakes PPA):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n0.4094954460160807\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.7881555100320838\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.4525277839857154\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.3143140770262107\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n3.2069112799945287\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"added 168 characters in body","revision_guid":"EB6DEB4E-A911-431B-896E-CBE183A1AAD2"},{"user":{"account_id":1891677,"reputation":159708,"user_id":1709587,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/cP8pZ.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Mark Amery","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/1709587/mark-amery"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1466936377,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":22,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h1>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h1>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p>A new syntax for this, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a> and <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available in Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression. It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, it is unlikely to be available in many production environments for years.</p>\n<p>The classically Pythonic way, available in Python 2 and Python 3.0-3.4, is to do this is a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>In both approaches, <code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<h1>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a <em>single expression</em></h1>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5, or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a <em>single expression</em>, the most performant while correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    '''Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.'''\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    '''\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    '''\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h1>Critiques of Other Answers</h1>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the most upvoted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example). Even if your values are hashable, <strong>since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic. <strong>Also, this fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h1>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h1>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dicts manually inside a dict comprehension:</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre><code>import itertools\nz = dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))\n</code></pre>\n<h1>Performance Analysis</h1>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly.</p>\n<pre><code>import timeit\n</code></pre>\n<p>The following is done on Ubuntu 14.04</p>\n<p>In Python 2.7 (system Python):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.5726828575134277\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.163769006729126\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))))\n1.1614501476287842\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n2.2345519065856934\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.5 (deadsnakes PPA):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n0.4094954460160807\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.7881555100320838\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.4525277839857154\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.3143140770262107\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n3.2069112799945287\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"Reordered to put the most modern approach first; added emphasis on *single expression* since I imagine that may have gone over some readers&#39; heads; tweaked language slightly for clarity in a few places. Feel free to revert if you disagree!","revision_guid":"E77A1486-8A8A-4641-AF2C-E0AEA77B53E9"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1465857988,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":21,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h1>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h1>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<p>The classically Pythonic way to do this is a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>New syntax, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a>, that is <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available in Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression. It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, it is unlikely to be available in many production environments for years.</p>\n<h1>Not yet on Python 3.5, but want a single expression</h1>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5, or need to write backward-compatible code, and you want this in a single expression, the most performant while correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    '''Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.'''\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    '''\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    '''\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h1>Critiques of Other Answers</h1>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the most upvoted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example), and even if your values are hashable, since <strong>sets are semantically unordered, and the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic. <strong>Also, this fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h1>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h1>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dicts manually inside a dict comprehension:</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre><code>import itertools\nz = dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))\n</code></pre>\n<h1>Performance Analysis</h1>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly.</p>\n<pre><code>import timeit\n</code></pre>\n<p>The following is done on Ubuntu 14.04</p>\n<p>In Python 2.7 (system Python):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.5726828575134277\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.163769006729126\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))))\n1.1614501476287842\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n2.2345519065856934\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.5 (deadsnakes PPA):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n0.4094954460160807\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.7881555100320838\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.4525277839857154\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.3143140770262107\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n3.2069112799945287\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"added 62 characters in body","revision_guid":"9A043CCA-5355-46C0-A2EF-ED0CEF205516"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1460819003,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":20,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<h1>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</h1>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<p>The classically Pythonic way to do this is a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>New syntax, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a>, that is <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available in Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression. It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, it is unlikely to be available in many production environments for years.</p>\n<h1>Python 2, In a single expression</h1>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5, and you want this in a single expression, the most performant while correct approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    '''Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.'''\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    '''\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    '''\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h1>Critiques of Other Answers</h1>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the most upvoted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example), and even if your values are hashable, since <strong>sets are semantically unordered, and the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic. <strong>Also, this fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h1>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h1>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dicts manually inside a dict comprehension:</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre><code>import itertools\nz = dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))\n</code></pre>\n<h1>Performance Analysis</h1>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly.</p>\n<pre><code>import timeit\n</code></pre>\n<p>The following is done on Ubuntu 14.04</p>\n<p>In Python 2.7 (system Python):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.5726828575134277\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.163769006729126\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))))\n1.1614501476287842\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n2.2345519065856934\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.5 (deadsnakes PPA):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n0.4094954460160807\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.7881555100320838\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.4525277839857154\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.3143140770262107\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n3.2069112799945287\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"added 12 characters in body","revision_guid":"FD01DEBD-BBBE-48CF-AC4E-1935DFC0AEBD"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1459534697,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":19,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<p>The classically Pythonic way to do this is a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y) # which returns None since it mutates z\n</code></pre>\n<p>New syntax, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a>, that is <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available in Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it is indeed a single expression. It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, it is unlikely to be available in many production environments for years.</p>\n<h1>Python 2, In a single expression</h1>\n<p>If you are not yet on Python 3.5, and you want this in a single expression, the most performant approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    '''Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.'''\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    '''\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    '''\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h1>Critiques of Other Answers</h1>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the most upvoted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example), and even if your values are hashable, since <strong>sets are semantically unordered, and the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic. <strong>Also, this fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h1>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h1>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dicts manually inside a dict comprehension:</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre><code>import itertools\nz = dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))\n</code></pre>\n<h1>Performance Analysis</h1>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly.</p>\n<pre><code>import timeit\n</code></pre>\n<p>The following is done on Ubuntu 14.04</p>\n<p>In Python 2.7 (system Python):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.5726828575134277\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.163769006729126\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))))\n1.1614501476287842\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n2.2345519065856934\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.5 (deadsnakes PPA):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n0.4094954460160807\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.7881555100320838\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.4525277839857154\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.3143140770262107\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n3.2069112799945287\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"deleted 123 characters in body","revision_guid":"EDEB57B6-91FE-4E7E-9880-076ED7E13BDB"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1454952770,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":18,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<p>The classically Pythonic way to do this is a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>New syntax, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a>, that is <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available in Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it meets the requirement that you ask for (a single expression). It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, it is unlikely to be available in most production environments for years.</p>\n<h1>Python 2, In a single expression</h1>\n<p>The new solution coming in Python 3.5 will become the main solution to this question as organizations move to Python 3.</p>\n<p>However, if you are not yet on Python 3.5, and you want this in a single expression, the most performant approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    '''Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.'''\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    '''\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    '''\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h1>Critiques of Other Answers</h1>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the most upvoted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example), and even if your values are hashable, since <strong>sets are semantically unordered, and the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic. <strong>Also, this fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>From the <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2010-April/099459.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">mailing list</a>, Guido van Rossum, the creator of the language, wrote:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am fine with\ndeclaring dict({}, **{1:3}) illegal, since after all it is abuse of\nthe ** mechanism.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>and</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently dict(x, **y) is going around as &quot;cool hack&quot; for &quot;call\nx.update(y) and return x&quot;. Personally I find it more despicable than\ncool.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h1>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h1>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dicts manually inside a dict comprehension:</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre><code>import itertools\nz = dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))\n</code></pre>\n<h1>Performance Analysis</h1>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly.</p>\n<pre><code>import timeit\n</code></pre>\n<p>The following is done on Ubuntu 14.04</p>\n<p>In Python 2.7 (system Python):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.5726828575134277\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.163769006729126\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))))\n1.1614501476287842\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n2.2345519065856934\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.5 (deadsnakes PPA):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n0.4094954460160807\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.7881555100320838\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.4525277839857154\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.3143140770262107\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n3.2069112799945287\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"added 432 characters in body","revision_guid":"F1278C95-9427-4638-828C-944FCBEDB6F2"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1454855055,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":17,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<p>The classically Pythonic way to do this is a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>New syntax, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a>, that is <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available in Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it meets the requirement that you ask for (a single expression). It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, it is unlikely to be available in most production environments for years.</p>\n<h1>Python 2, In a single expression</h1>\n<p>The new solution coming in Python 3.5 will become the main solution to this question as organizations move to Python 3.</p>\n<p>However, if you are not yet on Python 3.5, and you want this in a single expression, the most performant approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    '''Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.'''\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    '''\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    '''\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h1>Critiques of Other Answers</h1>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the most upvoted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example), and even if your values are hashable, since <strong>sets are semantically unordered, and the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic. <strong>Also, this fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<h1>Less Performant But Correct Ad-hocs</h1>\n<p>These approaches are less performant, but they will provide correct behavior.\nThey will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> or the new unpacking because they iterate through each key-value pair at a higher level of abstraction, but they <em>do</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence)</p>\n<p>You can also chain the dicts manually inside a dict comprehension:</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>itertools.chain</code> will chain the iterators over the key-value pairs in the correct order:</p>\n<pre><code>import itertools\nz = dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))\n</code></pre>\n<h1>Performance Analysis</h1>\n<p>I'm only going to do the performance analysis of the usages known to behave correctly.</p>\n<pre><code>import timeit\n</code></pre>\n<p>The following is done on Ubuntu 14.04</p>\n<p>In Python 2.7 (system Python):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.5726828575134277\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.163769006729126\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.iteritems(), y.iteritems()))))\n1.1614501476287842\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n2.2345519065856934\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 3.5 (deadsnakes PPA):</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {**x, **y}))\n0.4094954460160807\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: merge_two_dicts(x, y)))\n0.7881555100320838\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: {k: v for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items()} ))\n1.4525277839857154\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict(itertools.chain(x.items(), y.items()))))\n2.3143140770262107\n&gt;&gt;&gt; min(timeit.repeat(lambda: dict((k, v) for d in (x, y) for k, v in d.items())))\n3.2069112799945287\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"added 1475 characters in body","revision_guid":"E7543608-981C-46FD-ADE9-A791D43491B5"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1452097778,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":16,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<p>The classically Pythonic way to do this is a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>New syntax, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a>, that is <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available in Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it meets the requirement that you ask for (a single expression). It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, it is unlikely to be available in most production environments for years.</p>\n<h3>Python 2, In a single expression</h3>\n<p>The new solution coming in Python 3.5 will become the main solution to this question as organizations move to Python 3.</p>\n<p>However, if you are not yet on Python 3.5, and you want this in a single expression, the most performant approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    '''Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.'''\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    '''\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    '''\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h3>Critiques of Other Answers</h3>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the most upvoted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when values are unhashable objects (like lists, for example), and even if your values are hashable, since <strong>sets are semantically unordered, and the behavior is undefined in regards to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>This example demonstrates what happens when values are unhashable:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'b': []}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic. <strong>Also, this fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>If you just want a manual ad-hoc solution, keep in mind that this approach will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> because it is assigning each key-value pair, instead of all at once, but it <em>does</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence):</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"added 297 characters in body","revision_guid":"8705AD28-EEA8-41F8-BB35-7682F36E7A17"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1447099274,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":15,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<p>The classically Pythonic way to do this is a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>New syntax, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a>, that is <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available in Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it meets the requirement that you ask for (a single expression). It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, it is unlikely to be available in most production environments for years.</p>\n<h3>Python 2, In a single expression</h3>\n<p>The new solution coming in Python 3.5 will become the main solution to this question as organizations move to Python 3.</p>\n<p>However, if you are not yet on Python 3.5, and you want this in a single expression, the most performant approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    '''Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.'''\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    '''\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    '''\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h3>Critiques of Other Answers</h3>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the most upvoted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when unhashable objects are values (like lists, for example), and even if they are, since <strong>sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined with regard to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic. <strong>Also, this fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>If you just want a manual ad-hoc solution, keep in mind that this approach will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> because it is assigning each key-value pair, instead of all at once, but it <em>does</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence):</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"added 4 characters in body","revision_guid":"CD408077-ACAD-4E7E-9ADD-53848301F19E"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1446564454,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":14,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<p>The classically Pythonic way to do this is a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>New syntax, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a>, that is <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available in Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it meets the requirement that you ask for (a single expression). It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and it has now made its way into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html#pep-448-additional-unpacking-generalizations\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document.</p>\n<p>However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, it is unlikely to be available in a production environment for years.</p>\n<h3>Python 2, In a single expression</h3>\n<p>The new solution coming in Python 3.5 will become the main solution to this question as organizations move to Python 3.</p>\n<p>However, if you are not yet on Python 3.5, and you want this in a single expression, the most performant approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    '''Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.'''\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    '''\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    '''\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h3>Critiques of Other Answers</h3>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the most upvoted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when unhashable objects are values (like lists, for example), and even if they are, since <strong>sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined with regard to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic. <strong>Also, this fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>If you just want a manual ad-hoc solution, keep in mind that this approach will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> because it is assigning each key-value pair, instead of all at once, but it <em>does</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence):</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"added 54 characters in body","revision_guid":"C370632A-F6AA-468B-B261-90526C1CF72D"},{"user":{"account_id":2736748,"reputation":8745,"user_id":2359271,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/y1Mud.gif?s=256","display_name":"Air","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/2359271/air"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1441917573,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":13,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<p>The Pythonic way to do this is a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>New syntax, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a>, that is <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available in Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it meets the requirement that you ask for (a single expression). It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and I expect it may soon make it into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document. However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, it is unlikely to be available in a production environment for years.</p>\n<h3>Python 2, In a single expression</h3>\n<p>The new solution coming in Python 3.5 will become the main solution to this question as organizations move to Python 3.</p>\n<p>However, if you're working in the real world and you want this in a single expression, the most performant approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    '''Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.'''\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    '''\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    '''\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<h3>Critiques of Other Answers</h3>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the most upvoted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, create a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, and then discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when unhashable objects are values (like lists, for example), and even if they are, since <strong>sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined with regard to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic. <strong>Also, this fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>If you just want a manual ad-hoc solution, keep in mind that this approach will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> because it is assigning each key-value pair, instead of all at once, but it <em>does</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence):</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"It is not meet that you meet the ask","revision_guid":"D51D7BC0-7A00-4A91-9B95-EA077D1D5D9C"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1438190332,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":12,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<p>The Pythonic way to do this is a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>New syntax, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a>, that is <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available in Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it meets the ask for a single expression. It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and I expect it may soon make it into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document. However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, it is unlikely to be available in a production environment for years.</p>\n<p><strong>Python 2, In a single expression</strong></p>\n<p>The new solution coming in Python 3.5 will become the main solution to this question as organizations move to Python 3.</p>\n<p>However, if you're working in the real world and you want this in a single expression, the most performant approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    '''Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.'''\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    '''\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    '''\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<p><strong>Critiques of Other Answers</strong></p>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the most upvoted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, and then creates a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, then you discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when unhashable objects are values (like lists, for example), and even if they are, since <strong>sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined with regard to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>Here's an example, where y should have precedence, but instead the value from x is retained due to the arbitrary order of sets:</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; x = {'a': 2}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; y = {'a': 1}\n&gt;&gt;&gt; dict(x.items() | y.items())\n{'a': 2}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic. <strong>Also, this fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>If you just want a manual ad-hoc solution, keep in mind that this approach will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> because it is assigning each key-value pair, instead of all at once, but it <em>does</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence):</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"added 228 characters in body","revision_guid":"A36446BE-9780-4F0F-A75A-9CB25BBB168A"},{"user":{"account_id":35314,"reputation":33621,"user_id":99989,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/oLbIt.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Neil G","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/99989/neil-g"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1437502494,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":11,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<p>The Pythonic way to do this is a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>New syntax, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a>, that is <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">available in Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it meets the ask for a single expression. It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and I expect it may soon make it into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document. However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, it is unlikely to be available in a production environment for years.</p>\n<p><strong>Python 2, In a single expression</strong></p>\n<p>The new solution coming in Python 3.5 will become the main solution to this question as organizations move to Python 3.</p>\n<p>However, if you're working in the real world and you want this in a single expression, the most performant approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    '''Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.'''\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    '''\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    '''\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<p><strong>Critiques of Other Answers</strong></p>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the most upvoted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, and then creates a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, then you discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when unhashable objects are values (like lists, for example), and even if they are, since <strong>sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined with regard to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic. <strong>Also, this fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>If you just want a manual ad-hoc solution, keep in mind that this approach will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> because it is assigning each key-value pair, instead of all at once, but it <em>does</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence):</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"deleted 17 characters in body","revision_guid":"3295693E-6293-441F-9F65-C11EBC0A0B88"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1435757020,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":10,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<p>The Pythonic way to do this is a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>New syntax, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a>, that is <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">slated to become available in Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>And it meets the ask for a single expression. It is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, and I expect it may soon make it into <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document. However, since many organizations are still on Python 2, it is unlikely to be available in a production environment for years.</p>\n<p><strong>Python 2, In a single expression</strong></p>\n<p>The new solution coming in Python 3.5 will become the main solution to this question as organizations move to Python 3.</p>\n<p>However, if you're working in the real world and you want this in a single expression, the most performant approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    '''Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.'''\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    '''\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    '''\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<p><strong>Critiques of Other Answers</strong></p>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the most upvoted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, and then creates a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, then you discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when unhashable objects are values (like lists, for example), and even if they are, since <strong>sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined with regard to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic. <strong>Also, this fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>If you just want a manual ad-hoc solution, keep in mind that this approach will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> because it is assigning each key-value pair, instead of all at once, but it <em>does</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence):</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"updated to improve currency of the article","revision_guid":"91F09D62-D3A4-4457-A0D3-24CF290B1243"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1431298224,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":9,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<p>The Pythonic way to do this is a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>New syntax, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a>, that is <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">slated to become available in Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>But, although it is now showing as implemented in the <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0478/#features-for-3-5\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">release schedule for 3.5, PEP 478</a>, it is still so new that it is not even in the <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document. Since many organizations are still on Python 2, it is unlikely to be available in a production environment for years.</p>\n<p><strong>In a single expression</strong></p>\n<p>The new solution coming in Python 3.5 will become the main solution to this question.</p>\n<p>However, if you're working in the real world and you want this in a single expression, the most performant approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    '''Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.'''\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    '''\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    '''\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<p><strong>Critiques of Other Answers</strong></p>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the most upvoted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, and then creates a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, then you discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when unhashable objects are values (like lists, for example), and even if they are, since <strong>sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined with regard to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic. <strong>Also, this fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>If you just want a manual ad-hoc solution, keep in mind that this approach will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> because it is assigning each key-value pair, instead of all at once, but it <em>does</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence):</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"added 165 characters in body","revision_guid":"6E73B3E6-F33C-45F1-8284-FB69DD802696"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1426111443,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":8,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<p>The Pythonic way to do this is a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>New syntax, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a>, that is <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">slated to become available in Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>But it is so new that it is not even in the <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document. Since many organizations are still on Python 2, it is unlikely to be available in a production environment for years.</p>\n<p><strong>In a single expression</strong></p>\n<p>The new solution coming in Python 3.5 will become the main solution to this question.</p>\n<p>However, if you're working in the real world and you want this in a single expression, the most performant approach is to put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    '''Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.'''\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    '''\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    '''\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<p><strong>Critiques of Other Answers</strong></p>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the most upvoted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, and then creates a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, then you discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when unhashable objects are values (like lists, for example), and even if they are, since <strong>sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined with regard to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic. <strong>Also, this fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n<p>If you just want a manual ad-hoc solution, keep in mind that this approach will be <em>much less</em> performant than <code>copy</code> and <code>update</code> because it is assigning each key-value pair, instead of all at once, but it <em>does</em> respect the order of precedence (latter dicts have precedence):</p>\n<pre><code>{k: v for d in dicts for k, v in d.items()} # iteritems in Python 2.7\n</code></pre>\n<p>or in python 2.6 (and perhaps as early as 2.4 when generator expressions were introduced):</p>\n<pre><code>dict((k, v) for d in dicts for k, v in d.items())\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"added 508 characters in body","revision_guid":"888CCBEE-C3AB-42D4-9C8B-D9BD5D279189"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1425569309,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":7,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<p>The Pythonic way to do this is a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>New syntax, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a>, that is <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">slated to become available in Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>But it is so new that it is not even in the <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document. Since many organizations are still on Python 2, it is unlikely to be available in a production environment for years.</p>\n<p><strong>In a single expression</strong></p>\n<p>The new solution coming in Python 3.5 will become the main solution to this question.</p>\n<p>However, if you're working in the real world and you want this in a single expression, put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    '''Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.'''\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    '''\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    '''\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<p><strong>Critique of Top Answer</strong></p>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the most upvoted answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, and then creates a third list in memory with length equal to the length of the first two put together, then you discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p><strong>Critiques of other answers</strong></p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when unhashable objects are values (like lists, for example), and even if they are, since <strong>sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined with regard to precedence. So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic. <strong>Also, this fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"deleted 102 characters in body","revision_guid":"0A6FFE79-8043-4806-A2A3-4DF6EF7346C7"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1425521615,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":6,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<p>The Pythonic way to do this is a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>New syntax, proposed in <a href=\"https://www.python.org/dev/peps/pep-0448\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">PEP 448</a>, that is <a href=\"https://mail.python.org/pipermail/python-dev/2015-February/138564.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">slated to become available in Python 3.5</a>, is</p>\n<pre><code>z = {**x, **y}\n</code></pre>\n<p>But it is so new that it is not even in the <a href=\"https://docs.python.org/dev/whatsnew/3.5.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">What's New in Python 3.5</a> document. Since many organizations are still on Python 2, it is unlikely to be available in a production environment for years.</p>\n<p><strong>In a single expression</strong></p>\n<p>The new solution coming in Python 3.5 will become the main solution to this answer.</p>\n<p>However, if you're working in the real world and you want this in a single expression, put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    '''Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.'''\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    '''\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    '''\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<p><strong>Critique of Top Answer</strong></p>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the top answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, and then creates a third list in memory equal to the length of the first two put together, then you discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p><strong>Critiques of other answers</strong></p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when unhashable objects are values (like lists, for example), and even if they are, since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined with regard to precedence. <strong>So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic. <strong>Also, this fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"added 644 characters in body","revision_guid":"A030C8C1-84F1-461F-AB77-1C3E63ABDD2F"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1424888219,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":5,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<p>The Pythonic way to do this is a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y)\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>In a single expression</strong></p>\n<p>If you want this in a single expression, put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_two_dicts(x, y):\n    '''Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.'''\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_two_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>You can also make a function to merge an undefined number of dicts, from zero to a very large number:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(*dict_args):\n    '''\n    Given any number of dicts, shallow copy and merge into a new dict,\n    precedence goes to key value pairs in latter dicts.\n    '''\n    result = {}\n    for dictionary in dict_args:\n        result.update(dictionary)\n    return result\n</code></pre>\n<p>This function will work in Python 2 and 3 for all dicts. e.g. given dicts <code>a</code> to <code>g</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(a, b, c, d, e, f, g) \n</code></pre>\n<p>and key value pairs in <code>g</code> will take precedence over dicts <code>a</code> to <code>f</code>, and so on.</p>\n<p><strong>Critique of Top Answer</strong></p>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the top answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, and then creates a third list in memory equal to the length of the first two put together, then you discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two <code>dict_items</code> objects together, not two lists -</p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() + b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'dict_items' and 'dict_items'\n</code></pre>\n<p>and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p><strong>Critiques of other answers</strong></p>\n<p>Similarly, taking the union of <code>items()</code> in Python 3 (<code>viewitems()</code> in Python 2.7) will also fail when unhashable objects are values (like lists, for example), and even if they are, since sets are semantically unordered, the behavior is undefined with regard to precedence. <strong>So don't do this:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a.items() | b.items())\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: unhashable type: 'list'\n</code></pre>\n<p>Another hack you should not use:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being passed as keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic. <strong>Also, this fails in Python 3 when keys are not strings.</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; c = dict(a, **b)\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File &quot;&lt;stdin&gt;&quot;, line 1, in &lt;module&gt;\nTypeError: keyword arguments must be strings\n</code></pre>\n","comment":"added 1571 characters in body","revision_guid":"30F7DB24-2F89-4A6B-88FB-828B59953760"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1423255731,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":4,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How can I merge two Python dictionaries in a single expression?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them into a new dict without altering the original dicts:</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<p>The Pythonic way to do this is a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y)\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>In a single expression</strong></p>\n<p>If you want this in a single expression, put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(x, y):\n    '''Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.'''\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Critique of Top Answer</strong></p>\n<p>Don't use what you see in the top answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, and then creates a third list in memory equal to the length of the first two put together, then you discard all three lists to create the dict. <strong>In Python 3, this will fail</strong> because you're adding two iterators together, not two lists, and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p><strong>A Workaround</strong></p>\n<p>A hack to get around this is:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slightly more-so than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here (that is, the second dict is being expanded into keyword arguments to the dict constructor), it's difficult to read, it's not the intended usage, and so it is not Pythonic.</p>\n","comment":"added 279 characters in body","revision_guid":"6466DA85-8D02-4ACF-9950-D31BDC2B3736"},{"user":{"account_id":43866,"reputation":160916,"user_id":128421,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/71770d043c0f7e3c7bc5f74190015c26?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"the Tin Man","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/128421/the-tin-man"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1420856472,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":3,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them (without altering the original dicts):</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>y</code> will come second and its values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to <code>3</code> in our final result.</p>\n<p>The Pythonic way to do this is a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>If you want this in a single expression, put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(x, y):\n    '''Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.'''\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then you have a single expression:</p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>Don't use what you see in the top answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, and then creates a third list in memory equal to the length of the first two put together, then you discard all three lists to create the dict. In Python 3, this will fail because you're adding two iterators together, not two lists, and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p>A hack to get around this is:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the <code>dict</code> constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slighly moreso than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here, it's difficult to read, and so it is not Pythonic.</p>\n","comment":"deleted 5 characters in body","revision_guid":"26787A55-60BC-4D1E-8120-0E6EAAFE4FF0"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1417561098,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":2,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them (without altering the original dicts):</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>y</code> will come second and it's values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to 3 in our final result.</p>\n<p>The Pythonic way to do this is a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>If you want this in a single expression, put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(x, y):\n    '''Given two dicts, merge them into a new dict as a shallow copy.'''\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>and then you have a single expression:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>Don't use what you see in the top answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, and then creates a third list in memory equal to the length of the first two put together, then you discard all three lists to create the dict. In Python 3, this will fail because you're adding two iterators together, not two lists, and you would have to explicitly create them as lists, e.g. <code>z = dict(list(x.items()) + list(y.items()))</code>. This is a waste of resources and computation power.</p>\n<p><strong>A hack</strong> to get around this is:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the dict constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slighly moreso than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here, it's difficult to read, and so it is not Pythonic.</p>\n","comment":"added 143 characters in body","revision_guid":"58079841-B345-4B7C-9DB0-47D2D3816C57"},{"user":{"account_id":258754,"reputation":402395,"user_id":541136,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/wftMn.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Aaron Hall","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/541136/aaron-hall"},"set_community_wiki":false,"is_rollback":false,"creation_date":1415657508,"post_id":26853961,"post_type":"answer","revision_type":"single_user","revision_number":1,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","body":"<p>Say you have two dicts and you want to merge them (without altering the original dicts):</p>\n<pre><code>x = {'a': 1, 'b': 2}\ny = {'b': 3, 'c': 4}\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>y</code> will come second and it's values will replace <code>x</code>'s values, thus <code>'b'</code> will point to 3 in our final result.</p>\n<p>The Pythonic way to do this is a two-step process:</p>\n<pre><code>z = x.copy()\nz.update(y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>If you want this in a single expression, put it in a function:</p>\n<pre><code>def merge_dicts(x, y):\n    z = x.copy()\n    z.update(y)\n    return z\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>and then you have a single expression:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>z = merge_dicts(x, y)\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>Don't use what you see in the top answer:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x.items() + y.items())\n</code></pre>\n<p>In Python 2, you create two lists in memory for each dict, and then creates a third list in memory equal to the length of the first two put together, then you discard all three lists to create the dict. In Python 3, I believe this will fail because you're adding two iterators together, not two lists.</p>\n<p><strong>A hack</strong> to get around this is:</p>\n<pre><code>z = dict(x, **y)\n</code></pre>\n<p>This uses the dict constructor, and is very fast and memory efficient (even slighly moreso than our two-step process) but unless you know precisely what is happening here, it's difficult to read, and so it is not Pythonic.</p>\n","revision_guid":"3090E929-710F-43D7-A9A3-0C3B37D17D55"}],"has_more":false,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":204}